var tafsirData_7 = {
  "1": "'Alif-Lãm-Mĩm-Ṣãd.' (Quran 7:1). Four letters, and no explanation given.\n\nTwenty-nine surahs of the Quran begin with disconnected letters like these. Al-Baqarah opens with 'Alif-Lãm-Mĩm' (Quran 2:1). This one adds a fourth letter, and the combination appears nowhere else.\n\nThe honest position on their meaning is that nobody knows. Ibn Kathir, commenting on the opening of al-Baqarah, records the view of a number of the early scholars that these letters are among the matters whose knowledge Allah kept to Himself, and reports that some read them as names of the surahs.\n\nThat uncertainty is itself instructive, and the Quran addresses it directly at 3:7: some verses are precise and foundational, others are elusive, 'but none grasps their full meaning except Allah'.\n\nSo the reader meets, in the first line of a long surah, something he cannot decode. That is a useful thing to encounter at the start. It sets the terms: you are reading a book that does not answer to you.\n\nWhat can be observed is what follows the letters. Almost every time they appear, the next verse mentions the Book — and 7:2 does exactly that.\n\nThe letters are the raw material of Arabic. The Quran was composed from these same sounds, available to every poet in Mecca, and none of them produced it.\n\nThat may be the point being made without being stated.",
  "2": "'˹This is˺ a Book sent down to you ˹O Prophet˺— do not let anxiety into your heart regarding it—so with it you may warn ˹the disbelievers˺, and as a reminder to the believers.' (Quran 7:2). The Book, and a word about carrying it.\n\n'Do not let anxiety into your heart' — fa la yakun fi sadrika harajun minh. 'Haraj' is constriction, tightness in the chest.\n\nThat word appeared in the previous surah at 6:125, where the chest of the person resisting guidance was described as 'tight and constricted'. Here the Prophet is told not to feel the same thing about the Book he is delivering.\n\nWhy would he? Because of the reception. Surah al-An'am spent a hundred and sixty-five verses describing mockery, demands for signs, and refusal, and 6:33 said plainly: 'We certainly know that what they say grieves you.'\n\nThe Quran says the same at 20:2: 'We have not revealed the Quran to you to cause you distress.'\n\nThen the purpose, in two parts: 'so with it you may warn, and as a reminder to the believers' — li tundhira bihi wa dhikra lil-mu'minin.\n\nOne Book, two functions, depending on who is reading. A warning to those outside; a reminder to those already in.\n\nNote that believers need reminding. The Book is not only for persuading opponents, and this surah — with its long accounts of Nuh, Hud, Salih, Lut, Shu'ayb and Musa — is largely aimed at people who already believe.",
  "3": "'Follow what has been sent down to you from your Lord, and do not take others as guardians besides Him. How seldom are you mindful!' (Quran 7:3). The instruction, before any of the stories begin.\n\n'Follow what has been sent down' — ittabi'u ma unzila ilaykum. Plural now. Verse 7:2 addressed the Prophet; this addresses everyone.\n\nThe command is deliberately narrow: follow what was sent down. Not what was inherited, not what the community settled into. The surah will show seven nations that failed precisely here.\n\n'Do not take others as guardians besides Him' — wa la tattabi'u min dunihi awliya'. 'Awliya'' — patrons, protectors, the ones you rely on. The previous surah closed on this at 6:14 and 6:127.\n\n'How seldom are you mindful!' — qalilan ma tadhakkarun. A complaint, and it is aimed at how rarely people bring this to mind rather than at whether they agree with it.\n\nThat is an accurate description of the actual problem. Almost nobody in the Quran's audience denied that Allah made them. What they did was forget it during the hours when it mattered.\n\nThe verse pairs with 7:10, which ends 'you seldom give any thanks' — the same construction, the same word for 'seldom'.\n\nBoth faults are about frequency, not conviction. A person can hold the right belief and consult it twice a year.\n\nThe surah is about to show what that costs, seven times over.",
  "4": "'˹Imagine˺ how many societies We have destroyed! Our torment took them by surprise ˹while sleeping˺ at night or midday.' (Quran 7:4). The subject of the surah, announced in one line.\n\n'How many societies' — wa kam min qaryah. The question is left open, and the surah will answer it with names: Nuh's people, 'Ad, Thamud, Lut's people, Madyan, and Pharaoh's Egypt.\n\n'Took them by surprise' — ja'aha ba'suna bayatan. 'Bayat' is a night raid — attack while the enemy sleeps.\n\n'Or midday' — aw hum qa'ilun. 'Qaylulah' is the midday nap taken in hot countries.\n\nBoth times named are rest times. Not the moments of danger a community prepares for, but the hours when everyone has put their guard down.\n\nThat is the specific point being made. Destruction did not arrive during a crisis they were bracing for. It came in the middle of an ordinary afternoon.\n\nThe previous surah made the same observation at 6:44: 'just as they became prideful of what they were given, We seized them by surprise.'\n\nThere is nothing in this verse about the sins involved. That comes later, seven times over. What it establishes first is the timing, because the timing is what makes warning useful.\n\nA danger that announces itself does not require a prophet. This one does.",
  "5": "'Their only cry—when overwhelmed by Our torment—was, \"We have indeed been wrongdoers.\"' (Quran 7:5). The confession, made at the one moment when it changes nothing.\n\n'Their only cry' — fa ma kana da'wahum. Their entire plea, everything they had to say.\n\n'We have indeed been wrongdoers' — inna kunna zalimin. Not a defence, not an argument, not a request. An admission.\n\nThat is remarkable given what the surah will show. These are people who spent generations disputing with prophets, mocking them, demanding proof, threatening them with expulsion. Every argument they made evaporates in a sentence.\n\nAl-An'am recorded the same collapse at 6:130: 'They will say, \"We confess against ourselves!\"'\n\nThe confession here is accurate. They were wrongdoers, and they say so precisely. Nothing has changed about the facts — only about their willingness to state them.\n\n'When overwhelmed by Our torment' — idh ja'ahum ba'suna. The timing is the whole problem.\n\nThere is a practical point in this that has nothing to do with ancient nations. Most people know what is wrong in their own lives, and could name it accurately if pressed hard enough. The knowledge is not usually the missing piece.\n\nWhat is missing is saying it while saying it is still worth something.",
  "6": "'We will surely question those who received messengers and We will question the messengers ˹themselves˺.' (Quran 7:6). Two examinations, and the second is unexpected.\n\n'Those who received messengers' — alladhina ursila ilayhim. The audiences will be asked what they did with what came to them.\n\n'And We will question the messengers themselves' — wa la nas'alanna al-mursalin. The messengers are examined too.\n\nAl-Ma'idah showed the scene at 5:109: 'the Day Allah will gather the messengers and say, \"What response did you receive?\" They will reply, \"We have no knowledge! You alone are indeed the Knower of all unseen.\"'\n\nSo both parties stand. The prophets are asked whether they delivered; the peoples are asked what they did with the delivery.\n\nNobody is exempt because of rank. That principle ran through al-An'am, where the Prophet was told to say 'I truly fear—if I were to disobey my Lord—the torment of a tremendous Day' (Quran 6:15).\n\nThis verse is placed immediately after the destroyed nations of 7:4 and 7:5 for a reason. Those peoples were ruined, but the account is not settled by ruin. There is still a questioning.\n\nAnd it establishes the structure of the whole surah. Seven prophets are about to be sent to seven peoples, and this verse says that every one of those encounters is on record and will be gone through.",
  "7": "'Then We will give them a full account with sure knowledge—for We were never absent.' (Quran 7:7). Why the questioning of 7:6 will be accurate.\n\n'We will give them a full account' — fa la naqussanna 'alayhim bi 'ilm. The verb 'qassa' is to narrate, to tell the story. They will be told their own history back to them.\n\n'With sure knowledge' — bi 'ilm. Not from testimony, not from records compiled after the fact.\n\n'For We were never absent' — wa ma kunna gha'ibin. The reason, given in four words.\n\nThat is the answer to the obvious question about the questioning in 7:6. If Allah knows everything already, what is the examination for? The answer is that the examination is not for Him. He was present; He is telling them.\n\nThe previous surah established this in detail at 6:59: not a leaf falls, not a grain lies in the darkness of the earth, without it being known and written.\n\n'Never absent' is worth sitting with, because absence is what most wrongdoing quietly assumes. Not that Allah does not exist, but that He was elsewhere at the time.\n\nEvery private act was performed in company. That is the claim, and this verse states it without decoration.\n\nThe narration on that Day will not be a summary. It will be an account given by the only witness who never looked away.",
  "8": "'The weighing on that Day will be just. As for those whose scale will be heavy ˹with good deeds˺, ˹only˺ they will be successful.' (Quran 7:8). The image of the balance.\n\n'The weighing on that Day will be just' — wa al-waznu yawma'idhini al-haqq. The weighing is 'al-haqq' — the truth, the real thing.\n\nVerse 21:47 describes the same scales: 'We will set up the scales of justice on the Day of Judgment, so no soul will be wronged in the least. And even if a deed is the weight of a mustard seed, We will bring it forth.'\n\nSo the instrument is fine enough to register a mustard seed. Nothing is too small to appear, which is what 6:59 said about the falling leaf.\n\n'Whose scale will be heavy' — man thaqulat mawazinuh. Weight, not quantity. The Quran does not say 'whoever has more deeds'.\n\nThat distinction matters. Abu Hurayrah reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'Two words that are very easy for the tongue to say, and very heavy in the balance of reward, and most beloved to the Gracious Almighty: Subhan Allahi wa bi-hamdihi; Subhan Allahi-l-'Adhim' (Sahih al-Bukhari 6682).\n\nEasy on the tongue, heavy on the scale. Weight is not proportional to effort or to visibility.\n\n'Only they will be successful' — al-muflihun. The same word that closed the passage on wine and gambling at 5:90, and the surah will use it again.\n\nThe same statement appears at 23:102 and 101:6.",
  "9": "'But those whose scale is light, they have doomed themselves for wrongfully denying Our signs.' (Quran 7:9). The other outcome, and the cause named.\n\n'Whose scale is light' — man khaffat mawazinuh. Light, not empty. The verse does not describe people with nothing on the scale.\n\nThat is worth noticing carefully. A person may have a lifetime of activity and still weigh little, because the measure is not volume.\n\n'They have doomed themselves' — alladhina khasiru anfusahum. Literally: those who lost themselves. The exact phrase used three times in al-An'am, at 6:12, 6:20 and 6:31.\n\nThe loss is reflexive in the Arabic, and the surah keeps it that way. Nobody else does this to them.\n\n'For wrongfully denying Our signs' — bima kanu bi ayatina yazlimun. And here is what makes a life weigh nothing: denial of the signs.\n\nThat connects to the whole method of al-An'am, which spent verse after verse pointing at seeds, rain, dawn and stars. The signs were the evidence. Refusing them is not a neutral act of scepticism; the verse calls it 'zulm', wrongdoing.\n\nWhy wrongdoing? Because a sign is a message from someone. To wave it away is to treat the sender as though He had not spoken, which 6:91 called failing to show Allah 'His proper reverence'.\n\nThe scale measures response. That is the connection between this verse and the one before it.",
  "10": "'We have indeed established you on earth and provided you with a means of livelihood. ˹Yet˺ you seldom give any thanks.' (Quran 7:10). What was given, and what came back.\n\n'Established you on earth' — makkanna lakum fi al-ard. 'Tamkin' is being given a firm position, a secure footing.\n\nThe same word was used in al-An'am at 6:6 about the destroyed peoples: 'We had made them more established in the land than you.' The word carries a warning inside it.\n\n'And provided you with a means of livelihood' — wa ja'alna lakum fiha ma'ayish. The plural — 'ma'ayish' — covers all the ways people get a living: trade, farming, herding, craft.\n\nEvery one of those is described here as something placed on the earth in advance, for you to find.\n\n'Yet you seldom give any thanks' — qalilan ma tashkurun. The same construction as 7:3: 'How seldom are you mindful!'\n\nTwo verses, two complaints about frequency, and they are the same complaint. Remembering and thanking are the same act performed on the same fact.\n\nThe Quran states what follows from each at 14:7: 'If you are grateful, I will certainly give you more. But if you are ungrateful, surely My punishment is severe.'\n\nNotice that this verse closes the introduction. What follows, from 7:11 to the end of the surah, is the story of what happens when people are established, provided for, and not grateful.\n\nStarting with the first refusal ever made.",
  "11": "'Surely We created you, then shaped you, then said to the angels, \"Prostrate before Adam,\" so they all did—but not Iblîs, who refused to prostrate with the others.' (Quran 7:11). The first scene, and it explains everything that follows.\n\n'We created you, then shaped you' — khalaqnakum thumma sawwarnakum. Two stages: brought into being, then given form.\n\nThe address is plural — 'you' — though the story is about Adam. The Quran is telling every reader that this happened to him.\n\n'Prostrate before Adam' — usjudu li Adam. Not worship; the prostration is one of honour, commanded by Allah.\n\n'So they all did' — fa sajadu. The angels complied immediately.\n\n'But not Iblîs' — illa Iblis. One refusal, and it is the origin of every refusal in this surah.\n\nConsider the placement. The surah has just described peoples destroyed for denying signs (7:4, 7:9) and people who seldom thank (7:10). It now goes back to the first instance of the behaviour.\n\n'Who refused to prostrate with the others' — lam yakun mina al-sajidin. He was not among those who prostrated.\n\nAl-Baqarah adds what this verse leaves out: 'who refused and acted arrogantly, becoming unfaithful' (Quran 2:34). And 18:50 identifies him: 'Iblîs, who was one of the jinn, but he rebelled against the command of his Lord.'\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported the Prophet saying that when a person recites a verse of prostration and prostrates, Satan withdraws weeping and says: 'the son of Adam was commanded to prostrate, and he prostrated and Paradise was entitled to him and I was commanded to prostrate, but I refused and am doomed to Hell' (Sahih Muslim 81a).",
  "12": "'Allah asked, \"What prevented you from prostrating when I commanded you?\" He replied, \"I am better than he is: You created me from fire and him from clay.\"' (Quran 7:12). The reason given for the first refusal.\n\n'What prevented you' — ma mana'aka. He is asked, not simply condemned. The question gives him the opportunity to answer, and the answer is what convicts him.\n\n'I am better than he is' — ana khayrun minh. Three words, and they are the root of the disobedience.\n\nNotice what he does not say. He does not deny the command, dispute that it came from Allah, or claim he misunderstood. He acknowledges all of it and offers a comparison instead.\n\n'You created me from fire and him from clay' — khalaqtani min narin wa khalaqtahu min tin. The same words are recorded at 38:76.\n\nThe argument is an argument from material, and it assumes fire ranks above clay. That assumption is never examined. It is asserted, and everything is built on it.\n\nAl-An'am described human origin the same way at 6:2: 'created you from clay'. The Quran does not dispute the raw material; it disputes what follows from it.\n\nAnd what the reasoning actually does is set his own judgement above an explicit command. He was told to prostrate. He decided the order was mistaken because the recipient did not deserve it.\n\nThat is the structure of arrogance, and the surah will show it again in every nation. The leaders of 'Ad, Thamud and Madyan all reject their prophets on the same grounds: not that the message is false, but that the messenger is beneath them.",
  "13": "'Allah said, \"Then get down from Paradise! It is not for you to be arrogant here. So get out! You are truly one of the disgraced.\"' (Quran 7:13). The sentence, and the offence named.\n\n'It is not for you to be arrogant here' — fa ma yakunu laka an tatakabbara fiha. Here is the charge, stated by Allah Himself: 'takabbur', arrogance.\n\nVerse 7:12 gave the reasoning. This verse gives the diagnosis, and it identifies the reasoning as a symptom rather than a cause. He did not refuse because of the fire-and-clay argument; he produced the argument because he had refused.\n\nThat order matters, and the surah has already been established on it. Al-Ma'idah named arrogance as the single disqualifying quality at 5:82, praising those who 'are not arrogant'.\n\n'Get down' — ihbit. Descend. The word implies going from a higher place to a lower one, which is exactly what arrogance produces.\n\n'You are truly one of the disgraced' — mina al-saghirin. From 'sighar' — smallness, being made little.\n\nThat is the exact reversal of what he claimed. He said 'I am better'; the verdict makes him small. Al-An'am used the same word at 6:124 for those who wanted the status of messengers: 'the wicked will soon be overwhelmed by humiliation.'\n\nWhoever elevates himself is lowered. The Quran states this as a rule of the universe, and it is demonstrated here at the very beginning, before any human being had sinned.",
  "14": "'He appealed, \"Then delay my end until the Day of their resurrection.\"' (Quran 7:14). The request made after the sentence.\n\n'He appealed' — qala anzirni. 'Inzar' is granting a reprieve, a postponement of what is due.\n\nLook at what he asks for. Not forgiveness. Not another chance to prostrate. Time.\n\nThat is the most revealing detail in the passage. He stood in the presence of Allah, having just been condemned, and it did not occur to him to repent. The same recorded request appears at 15:36.\n\nAl-An'am described a reprieve as the mercy people mistake for permission. At 6:8 the demand for an angel was answered: 'the matter would have certainly been settled at once, and they would have never been given more time to repent.'\n\nIblis wants exactly that commodity — time — and he wants it for the opposite purpose.\n\n'Until the Day of their resurrection' — ila yawmi yub'athun. He asks for the maximum: the entire span of human history.\n\nThere is something worth noticing about the shape of this. He accepts the verdict without argument, and negotiates only about duration. He is not confused about his position; he simply does not want it revised.\n\nThe next verse grants the request, and the one after explains what he intends to do with it.\n\nWhat the reader should carry from this is the parallel. He was given time and used it for one purpose. So were you.",
  "15": "'Allah said, \"You are delayed ˹until the appointed Day˺.\"' (Quran 7:15). The request of 7:14, granted.\n\n'You are delayed' — innaka mina al-munzarin. You are among those given a reprieve.\n\nConsider what has just happened. A creature refused a direct command, argued with Allah about it, was sentenced, and then asked for something — and received it.\n\nThat should be startling. The request was granted to the enemy of every human being who would ever live.\n\nThe Quran specifies the limit elsewhere. In Surah al-Hijr the same exchange ends with 'until the appointed Day' (Quran 15:38) — not the Day of resurrection he asked for, but a moment known to Allah.\n\nWhy grant it at all? Because the arrangement described in 7:14 to 7:18 is the arrangement human beings live inside. There is an adversary, he has a term, and within that term there is a choice to be made.\n\nRemove him and the choice disappears. A person who cannot be tempted is not obedient; he is merely unopposed.\n\nAl-An'am put the same principle differently at 6:8: sending the sign the deniers demanded would have ended the matter, and 'they would have never been given more time to repent.'\n\nThe reprieve given to Iblis is the same interval in which every repentance in history has happened.\n\nHe asked for time to ruin people. He was given the time in which they could be saved.",
  "16": "'He said, \"For leaving me to stray I will lie in ambush for them on Your Straight Path.\"' (Quran 7:16). What he intends to do with the reprieve.\n\n'For leaving me to stray' — fa bima aghwaytani. He begins by assigning the blame elsewhere.\n\nThat is worth noting. He refused, gave his reason in 7:12, and was condemned for arrogance in 7:13. Now, with no dispute about any of it, he says the straying was done to him.\n\nThe Quran records the same opening at 15:39: 'My Lord! For allowing me to stray I will surely tempt them on earth.'\n\nBlaming the decree for one's own choice is the argument al-An'am dismantled at 6:148, where the pagans said 'Had it been Allah's Will, neither we nor our forefathers would have associated others with Him.' The reply then was: produce your knowledge.\n\n'I will lie in ambush for them' — la aq'udanna lahum. Literally: I will sit for them. The image is of someone waiting in position, not chasing.\n\n'On Your Straight Path' — siratika al-mustaqim. And this is the sharpest detail. The ambush is set on the right road, not the wrong one.\n\nHe does not wait where people are already lost. He waits where they are trying to go, because that is the only route worth blocking.\n\nAnyone who has tried to pray consistently, give regularly, or hold a resolution knows exactly where the resistance appears. It appears on the path.",
  "17": "'I will approach them from their front, their back, their right, their left, and then You will find most of them ungrateful.' (Quran 7:17). The ambush described in detail.\n\nFour directions, and they cover a person completely.\n\nThe early commentators read them as different avenues of attack. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records the reading that the front is the world ahead of them, the back is what comes after them, and the right and left are the good deeds discouraged and the bad ones made attractive.\n\nWhatever the precise mapping, the point of listing four is that no side is unguarded.\n\nNotice what is missing from the list. Not above, and not below. The scholars have long observed the gap, and the natural reading is that the direction of prostration and the direction from which mercy descends are not available to him.\n\n'Then You will find most of them ungrateful' — wa la tajidu aktharahum shakirin. Most, not all. Even in his own boast, the exception is admitted, and 15:40 makes it explicit: 'except Your chosen servants among them.'\n\nAnd look at what he predicts he will produce. Not disbelief, not idolatry — ingratitude.\n\nThat is precisely the fault named in 7:10: 'you seldom give any thanks.' Verse 7:17 tells you whose project that is.\n\nHe knows the target better than most people do. Gratitude is the hinge, and he goes for it directly.",
  "18": "'Allah said, \"Get out of Paradise! You are disgraced and rejected! I will certainly fill up Hell with you and your followers all together.\"' (Quran 7:18). The sentence completed.\n\n'Disgraced and rejected' — madh'uman madhura. Two words, both meaning driven out with contempt.\n\n'With you and your followers all together' — mimman tabi'aka minhum ajma'in. And here the sentence extends beyond him.\n\nThat single clause reframes everything he said in 7:16 and 7:17. He boasted about the people he would ambush. The reply names them as his fellow prisoners.\n\nWhatever he offers, this is the destination he is recruiting for. He is not building a rival kingdom; he is filling a cell with company.\n\nAl-An'am put it plainly at 6:128, where the jinn and their human associates admitted 'We benefited from each other's company' — and were told 'The Fire is your home.'\n\n'Your followers' — man tabi'aka. Those who follow. The word implies a choice, and it is the same word used for following the guidance in 7:3.\n\nThe Quran keeps both verbs available throughout this surah. Follow what was sent down, or follow him. Everyone follows something.\n\nNote the honesty of the arrangement. The terms are published in advance, at the very beginning of the surah, before any human being has been tempted.\n\nNobody enters this without being told.",
  "19": "'˹Allah said,˺ \"O Adam! Live with your wife in Paradise and eat from wherever you please, but do not approach this tree, or else you will be wrongdoers.\"' (Quran 7:19). The instruction given to the first human being.\n\n'Eat from wherever you please' — kula min haythu shi'tuma. The permission comes first, and it is enormous. Everything in the Garden.\n\n'But do not approach this tree' — wa la taqraba hadhihi al-shajarah. One exception.\n\nThat proportion is the whole shape of the law, and it has not changed. Al-An'am ended its food section at 6:145 with a list of four forbidden things, everything else lawful. Al-Ma'idah said at 5:87: 'Do not forbid the good things which Allah has made lawful for you.'\n\nThe permitted is vast. The forbidden is specific and small, and it is the small thing that gets the attention.\n\n'Do not approach' — la taqraba. Not 'do not eat from'. The boundary is drawn wider than the act, exactly as 6:151 said of indecencies: 'Do not come near indecencies, openly or secretly.'\n\nThat is a practical mercy. A line drawn at the act itself leaves a person standing at the edge with nothing but willpower.\n\n'Or else you will be wrongdoers' — fa takuna mina al-zalimin. The consequence is named in advance. No detail is withheld.\n\nNote also 'live with your wife' — the arrangement is a shared one, and both of them will be addressed together throughout what follows.\n\nEverything needed to succeed was supplied: the permission, the limit, the reason, and the warning about who was waiting.",
  "20": "'Then Satan tempted them in order to expose what was hidden of their nakedness. He said, \"Your Lord has forbidden this tree to you only to prevent you from becoming angels or immortals.\"' (Quran 7:20). The method of the temptation.\n\n'In order to expose what was hidden of their nakedness' — li yubdiya lahuma ma wuriya 'anhuma min saw'atihima. The purpose is stated before the speech, so the reader knows what is being aimed at before hearing the pitch.\n\nThat is a kindness to the reader. Nobody inside a temptation can see its purpose; the Quran shows it first.\n\n'Your Lord has forbidden this tree to you only to prevent you from...' — the argument is an accusation about motive. He does not deny that Allah gave the command. He supplies a reason for it, and the reason is unflattering.\n\nThat is the essential move. The prohibition of 7:19 came with no explanation attached, and into that silence he inserts one: He is holding something back from you.\n\nEvery temptation since has used the same structure. The limit is real; the suggestion is that it exists to deprive you.\n\n'From becoming angels or immortals' — malakayni aw takuna mina al-khalidin. He offers a promotion.\n\nAnd the offer is a lie about the nature of the thing. They were not in a queue for immortality. Surah Ta-Ha records the pitch as 'the Tree of Immortality and a kingdom that does not fade away' (Quran 20:120).\n\nNote that the appeal is to ambition, not appetite. It is the same fault as 7:12: wanting a higher rank than the one assigned.",
  "21": "'And he swore to them, \"I am truly your sincere advisor.\"' (Quran 7:21). The oath.\n\n'He swore to them' — wa qasamahuma. He took an oath by Allah, and the early commentators note this: he swore by the very One he had just rebelled against.\n\n'I am truly your sincere advisor' — inni lakuma la mina al-nasihin. 'Nasihah' is sincere counsel — advice given for the benefit of the one advised, with nothing in it for the adviser.\n\nConsider the claim. The being condemned in 7:18 as their enemy presents himself as the one person in the situation looking out for them.\n\nThat is why the oath is included. Adam and his wife had no experience of deception; nothing in their existence had prepared them for a sworn statement that was false.\n\nAl-An'am described the technique at 6:112: 'whispering to one another with elegant words of deception' — 'zukhruf al-qawl', gilded speech.\n\nThe gilding here is the word 'nasih'. Everything harmful arrives labelled as help.\n\nThere is a practical test buried in this and it is worth extracting. Advice that comes with an oath attached, from someone with a stake in your decision, deserves more scrutiny than advice that does not.\n\nReal counsel rarely needs to insist on its own sincerity.\n\nThe next verse gives the result, and the speed of it is part of the lesson.",
  "22": "'So he brought about their fall through deception. And when they tasted of the tree, their nakedness was exposed to them, prompting them to cover themselves with leaves from Paradise...' (Quran 7:22). The result, immediate and physical.\n\n'Brought about their fall through deception' — fa dallahuma bi ghurur. 'Ghurur' — deception by appearance, the same word al-An'am used at 6:70 and 6:130 for what the worldly life does to people.\n\n'And when they tasted of the tree' — fa lamma dhaqa al-shajarah. They tasted. The verse does not describe a feast.\n\n'Their nakedness was exposed to them' — badat lahuma saw'atuhuma. Exactly what 7:20 said was the aim. He offered them angelhood; what they received was exposure.\n\n'Prompting them to cover themselves with leaves' — the first human response to sin is the impulse to hide. Nobody taught them shame.\n\n'Did I not forbid you from that tree and did I not tell you that Satan is your sworn enemy?' — two questions, and both are reminders rather than accusations. You were told about the tree. You were told about him.\n\nThat is the whole indictment. Not that they lacked information, but that the information was available and did not survive contact with a persuasive voice.\n\nThe same account is at 20:121, which adds: 'So Adam disobeyed his Lord, and so lost his way' — followed immediately at 20:122 by 'Then his Lord chose him for His grace, accepted his repentance, and guided him rightly.'\n\nThe Quran never leaves the fall standing alone. The next verse is why.",
  "23": "'They replied, \"Our Lord! We have wronged ourselves. If You do not forgive us and have mercy on us, we will certainly be losers.\"' (Quran 7:23). The response that separates them from Iblis.\n\nCompare the two reactions, because the surah has placed them thirteen verses apart for exactly this purpose.\n\nIblis, condemned, said 'For leaving me to stray' (Quran 7:16) — the fault was Allah's decree. Adam and his wife say 'We have wronged ourselves' — zalamna anfusana.\n\nSame failure category, opposite response. One explains, one confesses.\n\n'If You do not forgive us and have mercy on us' — the plea does not claim a right. It states dependence.\n\n'We will certainly be losers' — la nakunanna mina al-khasirin. The same word used at 7:9 for those whose scale is light.\n\nAl-Baqarah records that these words were given to him: 'Then Adam was inspired with words of prayer by his Lord, so He accepted his repentance' (Quran 2:37). Even the repentance was a gift.\n\nNotice how short it is. No promises, no plan for improvement, no listing of prior good deeds. Three clauses: we did it, forgive us, otherwise we are finished.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this passage, contrasts Adam's admission with Iblis's argument and takes the difference between them as the reason one was forgiven and the other was not.\n\nThe fall is not the dividing line in this story. What is said afterwards is.",
  "24": "'Allah said, \"Descend as enemies to each other. You will find in the earth a residence and provision for your appointed stay.\"' (Quran 7:24). The sentence, after the forgiveness.\n\n'Descend as enemies to each other' — ihbitu ba'dukum li ba'din 'aduww. The hostility named is between the human beings and the one who deceived them, and 20:117 and 2:36 record it the same way.\n\n'You will find in the earth a residence' — wa lakum fi al-ard mustaqarr. 'Mustaqarr' — a settling place. The same word al-An'am used at 6:67 for the moment when every matter comes to rest, and at 6:98 for where human beings are placed.\n\n'And provision' — wa mata'. Enjoyment, the use of something.\n\n'For your appointed stay' — ila hin. Until a time.\n\nRead those three together and the earth is described precisely: a place to settle, things to enjoy, and a deadline. Nothing about it is described as punishment.\n\nThat is important. Adam was forgiven in 7:23, and still descended. Forgiveness restored the relationship; it did not reverse the consequence.\n\nMost people expect those two to move together, and they do not. A person can be entirely forgiven for a decision and still live in the world that decision produced.\n\nWhat the verse establishes is that the world we are in is not exile from mercy. It is the location where the arrangement of 7:14 to 7:18 plays out — an adversary with a term, and human beings with one too.",
  "25": "'He added, \"There you will live, there you will die, and from there you will be resurrected.\"' (Quran 7:25). Three sentences covering everything that will happen.\n\n'There you will live' — fiha tahyawn. The whole of human history, in two words.\n\n'There you will die' — wa fiha tamutun. The same ground.\n\n'And from there you will be resurrected' — wa minha tukhrajun. Literally: from it you will be brought out.\n\nThe earth appears three times, and it is doing three different jobs: a home, a grave, and a place of exit.\n\nThat last one is the answer to the whole objection al-An'am spent so long addressing. The people who said 'There is nothing beyond this worldly life' (Quran 6:29) were denying the third clause of this sentence.\n\nAnd the Quran's argument there was the same one implied here: the One who produced you from this soil in the first place is not troubled by producing you from it again. Verse 6:95 made it with a seed.\n\nNotice how the sequence is delivered. Not as a threat and not as a warning — as information, in the same tone as 'you will find in the earth a residence' in the previous verse.\n\nThis is simply what the arrangement is. Adam is told the shape of his descendants' existence before any of them exists.\n\nEverything the surah goes on to describe — seven nations, their prophets, their choices — happens between the first clause and the third.",
  "26": "'O children of Adam! We have provided for you clothing to cover your nakedness and as an adornment. However, the best clothing is righteousness...' (Quran 7:26). The story turns and addresses the reader directly.\n\n'O children of Adam' — ya bani Adam. The first of four such addresses in this passage, and the shift is deliberate. What happened to Adam is now applied to everyone descended from him.\n\n'Clothing to cover your nakedness' — libasan yuwari saw'atikum. 'Saw'ah' is the same word used in 7:20 and 7:22 for what was exposed by the tree.\n\nSo clothing is presented as a remedy for a specific event. The exposure happened; the covering was then supplied.\n\n'And as an adornment' — wa risha. 'Rish' is plumage, the fine feathers of a bird. Clothing serves the necessary function and also the beautiful one, and the Quran names both without embarrassment.\n\n'However, the best clothing is righteousness' — wa libasu al-taqwa dhalika khayr. Taqwa is described as a garment.\n\nThat image repays attention. A garment is worn, chosen, put on daily, and visible to others. It also covers what a person would rather not display.\n\nThe context makes the point sharper. This passage sits in a surah addressing people who performed pilgrimage naked — Abu Hurayrah reported the announcement made at Mina: 'No pagan is allowed to perform Hajj after this year and no naked person is allowed to perform the Tawaf around the Ka'bah' (Sahih al-Bukhari 369).\n\n'So perhaps you will be mindful' — la'allahum yadhdhakkarun.",
  "27": "'O children of Adam! Do not let Satan deceive you as he tempted your parents out of Paradise... Surely he and his soldiers watch you from where you cannot see them...' (Quran 7:27). The warning, drawn from the story just told.\n\n'Do not let Satan deceive you' — la yaftinannakumu al-shaytan. The verb is 'fitnah' — to test, to seduce into trouble.\n\n'As he tempted your parents out of Paradise' — the precedent is your own family history. Whatever technique worked then is still in service.\n\n'He and his soldiers watch you from where you cannot see them' — innahu yarakum huwa wa qabiluhu min haythu la tarawnahum.\n\nThat is the asymmetry, and it is stated without softening. He sees; you do not.\n\nIt sounds like a hopeless position, and it is not, for a reason the verse implies. You cannot outwatch him, so the defence is not vigilance against an invisible enemy. It is the covering named in the previous verse: taqwa.\n\n'We have made the devils allies of those who disbelieve' — awliya' — the same word for guardians used in 7:3: 'do not take others as guardians besides Him'.\n\nSo the alliance is contracted from the human side. He does not seize anyone. Al-An'am recorded his own admission of this at 6:112, and 14:22 has him saying: 'I did not have any authority over you. I only called you, and you responded to me.'\n\nHe is watching. He is not driving.",
  "28": "'Whenever they commit a shameful deed, they say, \"We found our forefathers doing it and Allah has commanded us to do it.\" Say, \"No! Allah never commands what is shameful. How can you attribute to Allah what you do not know?\"' (Quran 7:28). Two excuses, answered in one line.\n\n'A shameful deed' — fahishah. The word covers gross indecency, and here it refers to the naked circumambulation of the Ka'bah that some tribes practised as an act of worship.\n\nTwo justifications are offered. 'We found our forefathers doing it' — the appeal to inheritance, which al-An'am answered at 6:104: 'even if their forefathers had absolutely no knowledge or guidance?'\n\nAnd: 'Allah has commanded us to do it' — the appeal to divine authority, which is the graver claim.\n\n'No! Allah never commands what is shameful' — inna Allaha la ya'muru bi al-fahsha'. The reply is a statement about Allah's nature, and it settles the matter without examining the practice.\n\nWhatever is genuinely shameful cannot have come from Him. That gives every reader a usable test.\n\n'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'no one is more self-respecting than Allah Himself and it is because of this that He has prohibited abominable acts' (Sahih Muslim 2760a).\n\n'How can you attribute to Allah what you do not know?' — the demand for evidence, exactly as al-An'am pressed it at 6:143 and 6:148: produce your knowledge.\n\nTwo claims, and neither had anything behind it except habit.",
  "29": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"My Lord has commanded uprightness and dedication ˹to Him alone˺ in worship, calling upon Him with sincere devotion. Just as He first brought you into being, you will be brought to life again.\"' (Quran 7:29). The answer to the previous verse.\n\nVerse 7:28 asked how anyone could attribute a shameful practice to Allah. This verse states what He actually commanded.\n\n'Uprightness' — al-qist. Justice, fair dealing, the straight measure.\n\n'Dedication to Him alone in worship' — wa aqimu wujuhakum 'inda kulli masjid. Literally: set your faces straight at every place of prostration.\n\n'Calling upon Him with sincere devotion' — mukhlisina lahu al-din. 'Ikhlas' means making something pure — removing everything mixed into it. Surah al-Bayyinah uses the same phrase at 98:5: 'they were only commanded to worship Allah alone with sincere devotion to Him in all uprightness.'\n\nSo the true religion is short. Fairness, and worship with nothing else added.\n\nSet that beside what the previous verse described: naked circumambulation, inherited custom, and a false claim of divine authority. The contrast is the argument.\n\n'Just as He first brought you into being, you will be brought to life again' — kama bada'akum ta'udun. The resurrection, asserted through the first creation.\n\nThat is the same reasoning al-An'am used at 6:95 with the splitting seed. Whoever managed the beginning is not challenged by the repeat.\n\nNotice how the verse closes the loop opened at 7:25: 'from there you will be resurrected.' Adam was told it; his children are now told it again.\n\nThe whole religion, in one sentence: be fair, worship Him alone, and remember you are going back.",
  "30": "'He has guided some, while others are destined to stray. They have taken devils as their masters instead of Allah— thinking they are ˹rightly˺ guided.' (Quran 7:30). The most unsettling line in the passage.\n\n'Thinking they are rightly guided' — wa yahsabuna annahum muhtadun. They believe they are on the right road.\n\nThat is the danger the verse exists to name. A person who knows he is lost can ask for directions. A person who is certain he has arrived cannot.\n\n'They have taken devils as their masters' — awliya' — the third appearance of that word in this surah, after 7:3 and 7:27. Each time, the taking is done by them.\n\nThe Prophet, peace be upon him, quoted his Lord saying: 'I have created My servants as one having a natural inclination to the worship of Allah but it is Satan who turns them away from the right religion and he makes unlawful what has been declared lawful for them' (Sahih Muslim 2865a).\n\nSo the starting condition is sound. Surah ar-Rum calls it 'the natural Way of Allah which He has instilled in all people' (Quran 30:30). What follows is a turning away, not a defect in manufacture.\n\n'While others are destined to stray' — wa fariqan haqqa 'alayhim al-dalalah. The straying became due upon them. The wording points to something earned, and the second half of the verse says how it was earned: they took the alliance.\n\nThe practical worry the verse plants is a healthy one. If sincerity alone were the test, everyone in this verse would pass. It is not.\n\nThat is why 7:29 insisted the command be specified.",
  "31": "'O Children of Adam! Dress properly whenever you are at worship. Eat and drink, but do not waste. Surely He does not like the wasteful.' (Quran 7:31). Three instructions, and they read like ordinary advice.\n\n'Dress properly whenever you are at worship' — khudhu zinatakum 'inda kulli masjid. Take your adornment at every place of prostration.\n\nThis is the direct correction of the practice described in 7:28. They stripped for worship; they are told to dress for it.\n\nNote that the word is 'zinah' — adornment, not merely covering. The standard is not the minimum.\n\n'Eat and drink, but do not waste' — wa kulu wa ishrabu wa la tusrifu. Both halves matter. The permission is given before the limit, exactly as in 7:19.\n\n'Israf' is going past the proper measure. It is not the same as enjoyment, and the verse is careful about that: eating and drinking are commanded, not merely tolerated.\n\nMiqdam ibn Ma'dikarib reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'The human does not fill any container that is worse than his stomach. It is sufficient for the son of Adam to eat what will support his back. If this is not possible, then a third for food, a third for drink, and a third for his breath' (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 2380, graded sahih).\n\n'Surely He does not like the wasteful' — la yuhibbu al-musrifin.\n\nPut the three together and the verse is regulating the two most physical parts of life — what you wear and what you consume — inside a chapter about a Garden lost over food and clothing.",
  "32": "'Ask, \"Who has forbidden the adornments and lawful provisions Allah has brought forth for His servants?\" Say, \"They are for the enjoyment of the believers in this worldly life, but they will be exclusively theirs on the Day of Judgment...\"' (Quran 7:32). The question turned back on the questioner.\n\n'Who has forbidden' — man harrama. The challenge demands a source, in the same way 7:28 demanded one.\n\n'The adornments' — zinat Allah. The same word as the previous verse, now attributed to Allah directly. He brought them out; forbidding them is an accusation against Him.\n\nThis is the answer to a religiosity that measures itself by deprivation. Al-Ma'idah gave the same ruling at 5:87: 'Do not forbid the good things which Allah has made lawful for you.'\n\n'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud reported that when the Prophet, peace be upon him, warned against pride, a man said that he liked his dress and his shoes to be fine, and the Prophet replied: 'Verily, Allah is Graceful and He loves Grace' (Sahih Muslim 91a).\n\nSo beauty is not the problem. The verse and the hadith both locate the problem elsewhere.\n\n'They are for the enjoyment of the believers in this worldly life, but they will be exclusively theirs on the Day of Judgment' — khalisatan yawma al-qiyamah. Here they are shared with everyone; there they will not be.\n\n'This is how We make Our revelations clear for people of knowledge' — the same closing formula al-An'am used repeatedly, at 6:97, 6:98 and 6:105.\n\nThe believer is not asked to refuse the good things. He is asked to receive them from the right hand and within the stated limits.",
  "33": "'Say, \"My Lord has only forbidden open and secret indecencies, sinfulness, unjust aggression, associating ˹others˺ with Allah ˹in worship˺—a practice He has never authorized—and attributing to Allah what you do not know.\"' (Quran 7:33). The actual list.\n\nVerse 7:32 asked who forbade the good things. This verse says what was forbidden, and the list is short enough to memorise.\n\n'Open and secret indecencies' — al-fawahisha ma zahara minha wa ma batan. The same pairing al-An'am used at 6:151: 'Do not come near indecencies, openly or secretly.'\n\nPublic and private are named separately because people treat them differently and the ruling does not.\n\n'Sinfulness' — al-ithm. 'Unjust aggression' — al-baghy bi ghayri al-haqq: overstepping against others.\n\n'Associating others with Allah — a practice He has never authorized' — ma lam yunazzil bihi sultanan. No authority was ever sent down for it. The Quran repeatedly makes this the decisive point: not that idolatry is distasteful, but that there is no evidence for it.\n\n'And attributing to Allah what you do not know' — an taqulu 'ala Allahi ma la ta'lamun. The same phrase that ended 7:28, repeated deliberately five verses later.\n\nThat repetition tells you how seriously the surah takes speaking about Allah without knowledge. It is listed alongside indecency, aggression and idolatry.\n\nCompare the two lists now standing side by side: 7:32 says the good things were never forbidden, and 7:33 says exactly what was. Nothing is left to guesswork, and nobody has to invent a prohibition to feel religious.",
  "34": "'For each community there is an appointed term. When their time arrives, they can neither delay it for a moment, nor could they advance it.' (Quran 7:34). A pivot verse, and the whole second half of the surah rests on it.\n\n'For each community there is an appointed term' — li kulli ummatin ajal. Nations have deadlines, not only individuals.\n\nAl-An'am opened with two terms at 6:2, both for persons. This extends the principle to peoples.\n\n'When their time arrives' — fa idha ja'a ajaluhum. When it comes.\n\n'They can neither delay it for a moment, nor could they advance it' — la yasta'khiruna sa'atan wa la yastaqdimun. Not by an hour, in either direction.\n\nNote that both directions are excluded. A nation cannot postpone its collapse, and cannot bring it forward either — which quietly answers the people in 6:57 and 6:58 who demanded that the threatened punishment arrive immediately.\n\nSurah an-Nahl states the same rule and gives the reason for the delay at 16:61: 'If Allah were to punish people immediately for their wrongdoing, He would not have left a single living being on earth. But He delays them for an appointed term.'\n\nSo the interval is mercy, not oversight. That was the lesson of 7:15 as well, where the reprieve granted to the enemy became the window for every repentance.\n\nThis verse is placed here on purpose. From 7:59 onwards the surah will narrate one nation after another reaching its term — Nuh's people, 'Ad, Thamud, Lut's people, Madyan, and Pharaoh's Egypt.\n\nThe rule is stated before the case studies begin.",
  "35": "'O children of Adam! When messengers from among yourselves come to you reciting My revelations—whoever shuns evil and mends their ways, there will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve.' (Quran 7:35). The third address to the children of Adam, after 7:26 and 7:27.\n\n'Messengers from among yourselves' — rusulun minkum. Human beings, from your own communities.\n\nThat detail is not decoration. Every nation in the coming narratives objects to precisely this: the messenger is one of us, so why him? The objection is answered before it is raised.\n\n'Reciting My revelations' — yaqussuna 'alaykum ayati. The messenger's job is described narrowly. He recites; he does not compel. That was the limit al-An'am placed on the Prophet himself at 6:107: 'you are not their keeper.'\n\n'Whoever shuns evil and mends their ways' — fa mani ittaqa wa aslaha. Two verbs: stop the harm, then repair.\n\nBoth are required. Stopping is not the same as fixing, and the Quran asks for both.\n\n'There will be no fear for them, nor will they grieve' — la khawfun 'alayhim wa la hum yahzanun. The same promise given to Adam's descendants at 2:38, immediately after the descent.\n\nNotice what the two fears are. Fear looks forward; grief looks back. The promise clears both directions.\n\nSurah Fussilat attaches the same words to those who stay steadfast: 'Do not fear, nor grieve. Rather, rejoice in the good news of Paradise' (Quran 41:30).\n\nThe offer made to Adam at the moment of the fall is still open to everyone descended from him.",
  "36": "'But those who receive Our revelations with denial and arrogance will be the residents of the Fire. They will be there forever.' (Quran 7:36). The other half of the offer in 7:35.\n\nTwo words define the group: 'kadhdhabu' — they denied — and 'istakbaru' — they were arrogant.\n\nThat second word is the one to watch. It is the verb used of Iblis in 7:13, and this surah keeps returning to it.\n\nDenial by itself might be ignorance. Denial joined to arrogance is a refusal to be corrected, and correction is the only mechanism available.\n\n'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, defining the term precisely: 'Pride is disdaining the truth and contempt for the people' (Sahih Muslim 91a).\n\nRead that definition back into the story of 7:12. Iblis disdained the command and held Adam in contempt. Both halves are present.\n\n'Will be the residents of the Fire' — ashab al-nar. Companions of the Fire, an idiom for permanent association.\n\n'They will be there forever' — hum fiha khalidun. And the word 'khalidun' has already appeared in this surah, in 7:20, where the tree was sold as the route to being 'khalidin' — immortal.\n\nThat is a bitter echo. The permanence he was promised arrives, in the wrong place.\n\nThe pairing of 7:35 and 7:36 is the surah's basic structure: the same revelation reaches everyone, and what divides people is what they do when it arrives.",
  "37": "'Who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah or deny His revelations?... until Our messenger-angels arrive to take their souls, asking them, \"Where are those ˹false gods˺ you used to invoke besides Allah?\" They will cry, \"They have failed us\"...' (Quran 7:37). The scene at death.\n\n'Who does more wrong' — man azlamu. The question that al-An'am asked at 6:21 and 6:93 in almost identical words.\n\n'They will receive what is destined for them' — yanaluhum nasibuhum mina al-kitab. Their share of what was written: the lifespan, the provision, the enjoyment. All of it is delivered in full.\n\nThat is the point of the clause. Nothing was withheld from them. The account is settled only afterwards.\n\n'Until Our messenger-angels arrive to take their souls' — the angels of 6:61, 'never neglecting this duty.'\n\n'Where are those false gods you used to invoke besides Allah?' — the question is asked at the exact moment their help would be needed.\n\n'They have failed us' — dallu 'anna. They wandered off, went missing.\n\n'They will confess against themselves that they were indeed disbelievers' — the same confession al-An'am recorded at 6:130: 'We confess against ourselves!'\n\nNotice the timing throughout this surah. Confession at 7:5 came after the destruction. Confession here comes at death. In both cases it is accurate, complete, and useless.\n\nAl-An'am gave the rule at 6:158: 'belief will not benefit those who did not believe earlier.'\n\nThere is nothing wrong with the answer they finally give. There is only something wrong with when they give it.",
  "38": "'Allah will say, \"Enter the Fire along with the ˹evil˺ groups of jinn and humans that preceded you.\" Whenever a group enters Hell, it will curse the preceding one... \"Our Lord! They have misled us, so multiply their torment in the Fire.\" He will answer, \"It has already been multiplied for all, but you do not know.\"' (Quran 7:38). The arrival, and the argument that starts immediately.\n\n'Jinn and humans' — the same pairing al-An'am used at 6:112 and 6:128. The two responsible species share the account.\n\n'Whenever a group enters Hell, it will curse the preceding one' — kullama dakhalat ummatun la'anat ukhtaha. Each curses the one before, the one that set the example.\n\nThat is worth pausing on, because it describes something happening now. Every generation inherits its assumptions from the one before it, and 7:28 already showed people defending a practice on exactly that basis: 'We found our forefathers doing it.'\n\n'They have misled us, so multiply their torment' — the followers ask for the leaders to be punished more.\n\n'It has already been multiplied for all, but you do not know' — li kullin di'f. Everyone's is doubled. The request is granted in a form that does not help the one who made it.\n\nAnd the reply exposes the assumption behind the request. They imagined punishment was a fixed quantity to be distributed, so that more for the leaders meant less for them.\n\nSurah Saba records the same exchange at 34:31, where the followers say 'Had it not been for you, we would certainly have been believers.'\n\nBlame is the first currency spent in the Fire, and it buys nothing.",
  "39": "'Then the leaders will say to their followers, \"You were no better than us! So taste the torment for what you used to commit.\"' (Quran 7:39). The reply from the other side.\n\n'You were no better than us' — fa ma kana lakum 'alayna min fadl. You have no advantage over us.\n\nThe leaders make one argument, and it is unanswerable. Whatever we did, you chose to follow. Nobody dragged you.\n\nSurah Saba records their version at 34:32: 'Did we ever hinder you from guidance after it came to you? In fact, you were wicked on your own.'\n\nNotice that both sides in this exchange are telling the truth. The leaders did mislead, and the followers did choose. Neither statement cancels the other, and neither reduces anyone's share.\n\n'So taste the torment for what you used to commit' — bima kuntum taksibun. What you earned. The verb 'kasaba' — to earn — is the Quran's standard word for deeds, and it is used here by the leaders against their own followers.\n\nThat is the end of every arrangement built on human authority over conscience. It holds exactly as long as it is convenient, and dissolves at the first cost.\n\nAl-Baqarah described the same collapse at 2:167: 'If only we could have a second chance, we would disown them as they disowned us.'\n\nThe practical lesson is available now, not later. Anyone whose religion consists of following a person rather than following what was sent down — the command of 7:3 — should ask whether that person will be defending him in this conversation.\n\nThe leaders in this verse do not even try.",
  "40": "'Surely those who receive our revelations with denial and arrogance, the gates of heaven will not be opened for them, nor will they enter Paradise until a camel passes through the eye of a needle. This is how We reward the wicked.' (Quran 7:40). The impossibility, stated as an image.\n\n'Denial and arrogance' — the exact pair from 7:36, repeated word for word. The surah is marking the group again before describing their outcome.\n\n'The gates of heaven will not be opened for them' — la tufattahu lahum abwab al-sama'. Compare 39:73, where those who kept their duty arrive at Paradise and find its gates 'already open.'\n\nOpened for one, shut for the other. The souls of these people do not ascend, and the early commentators read the closed gates as applying both to their deeds and to their souls at death.\n\n'Until a camel passes through the eye of a needle' — hatta yalija al-jamalu fi sammi al-khiyat. Two things at the furthest extremes of size, and the condition is set between them.\n\nThe Arabic word 'jamal' is normally the camel, the largest familiar animal; some early readers pronounced it 'jummal', a thick ship's rope, which keeps the same picture of something far too big.\n\nEither way the meaning is fixed: this will not happen. The Quran states an impossibility in a form no listener could misread.\n\n'This is how We reward the wicked' — wa kadhalika najzi al-mujrimin.\n\nRecall the mustard seed of 7:8, small enough to register on the scale, and note the contrast. Arrogance has no size problem; it has an entry problem.",
  "41": "'Hell will be their bed; flames will be their cover. This is how We reward the wrongdoers.' (Quran 7:41). Two words, and both are taken from ordinary domestic life.\n\n'Bed' — mihad. Bedding, what is spread out beneath a person to make rest possible.\n\n'Cover' — ghawash. Coverings, what is laid over a sleeper.\n\nThe whole image is of a bed made up: something under, something over. And both layers are fire.\n\nThe Quran chooses this picture deliberately, because a bed is the place of relief. It is where a person goes at the end of a difficult day.\n\nSet it against 7:4, where nations were destroyed 'while sleeping at night or midday.' Rest is a theme in this surah, and here it is withdrawn entirely.\n\n'This is how We reward the wrongdoers' — wa kadhalika najzi al-zalimin. The same formula as the previous verse, with one word changed: 'mujrimin' becomes 'zalimin'.\n\n'Zulm' is the surah's own diagnosis. Adam used it of himself at 7:23 — 'We have wronged ourselves' — and was forgiven. It was applied to those who denied the signs at 7:9.\n\nSame word, two destinations, and 7:23 shows what makes the difference.\n\nNotice also how brief the verse is. The Quran does not linger over the description; it states the arrangement in ten words and moves on to the believers in the next verse.\n\nWarning here is never entertainment.",
  "42": "'As for those who believe and do good—We never require of any soul more than what it can afford—it is they who will be the residents of Paradise. They will be there forever.' (Quran 7:42). The counterpart to 7:36, and it contains an interruption.\n\nRead the verse without the middle clause and it is a straightforward promise. The middle clause is inserted into the sentence, and its placement is the lesson.\n\n'We never require of any soul more than what it can afford' — la nukallifu nafsan illa wus'aha. The same sentence closes al-Baqarah at 2:286 and appears again at 23:62.\n\nWhy place it here, in the middle of a promise about Paradise?\n\nBecause of what has just been described. Four verses of Fire, closed gates, a camel and a needle. Anyone reading carefully might reasonably wonder whether the standard is reachable.\n\nThe clause answers that before it becomes a settled fear. The requirement is calibrated to capacity — yours, not someone else's.\n\n'Wus'' is what a person can accommodate. Not their maximum under pressure; their room.\n\n'Those who believe and do good' — alladhina amanu wa 'amilu al-salihat. The Quran's standard pairing: something held, and something done.\n\n'They will be there forever' — hum fiha khalidun. Word for word the ending of 7:36. Two identical endings, two opposite destinations.\n\nThat symmetry is the point. The permanence is the same on both sides; the address differs.\n\nAnd the reassurance is not a lowering of the standard. It is a statement that the standard was set with knowledge of the one being asked.",
  "43": "'We will remove whatever bitterness they had in their hearts... And they will say, \"Praise be to Allah for guiding us to this. We would have never been guided if Allah had not guided us...\"' (Quran 7:43). What Paradise does first.\n\n'We will remove whatever bitterness they had in their hearts' — wa naza'na ma fi sudurihim min ghill. 'Ghill' is the grudge that settles in the chest: resentment, an old score.\n\nNote where the description begins. Not with the rivers, not with the thrones — with the removal of something from inside the people themselves.\n\nThat is a striking priority. The residents arrive carrying history with one another, and the first act is to take it out. Surah al-Hijr says the same at 15:47.\n\nThe believers already pray for this in the present life. Surah al-Hashr records the words: 'do not allow bitterness into our hearts towards those who believe' (Quran 59:10).\n\n'Praise be to Allah for guiding us to this' — and then the correction they add themselves: 'We would have never been guided if Allah had not guided us.'\n\nThey will not accept the credit. Standing in the reward, having done the deeds, their first statement is that the guidance was not their achievement.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'The deeds of anyone of you will not save you' — and when asked whether that included him, he said 'No, even I will not be saved unless and until Allah bestows His Mercy on me' (Sahih al-Bukhari 6463).\n\n'This is Paradise awarded to you for what you used to do' — and the announcement still ties it to deeds. Both statements are true at once.",
  "44": "'The residents of Paradise will call out to the residents of the Fire, \"We have certainly found our Lord's promise to be true. Have you too found your Lord's promise to be true?\" They will reply, \"Yes, we have!\"...' (Quran 7:44). A conversation across the divide.\n\n'We have certainly found our Lord's promise to be true' — qad wajadna ma wa'adana rabbuna haqqan. Found, by experience. Not believed — found.\n\n'Have you too found your Lord's promise to be true?' — the question is asked of the other side, and notice the wording: 'your Lord'. He was their Lord as well, and the promise was made to them too.\n\n'Yes, we have' — na'am. One word, and it is the last admission of a long argument.\n\nThis surah has now recorded four confessions: at 7:5 after destruction, at 7:37 at the moment of death, at 7:38 in mutual blame, and here. Every one is accurate. Every one arrives after the deadline set out in 7:34.\n\n'Then a caller will announce to both' — fa adhdhana mu'adhdhinun baynahum. A crier, the same root as the one who calls to prayer.\n\n'May Allah's condemnation be upon the wrongdoers' — la'natu Allahi 'ala al-zalimin. The curse is pronounced publicly, and the next verse defines who is meant.\n\nWhat should be taken from this exchange is the question itself. Both groups end up giving the same answer. The only variable was when.\n\nEverything the surah warns about was verifiable in advance — that was the argument of 7:6 to 7:9.",
  "45": "'those who hindered ˹others˺ from Allah's Way, strived to make it ˹appear˺ crooked, and disbelieved in the Hereafter.' (Quran 7:45). The definition attached to the curse of the previous verse.\n\nThree descriptions, and they escalate.\n\n'Those who hindered others from Allah's Way' — yasuddun 'an sabil Allah. Turning people away. This is not personal disbelief; it is interference with someone else's route.\n\n'Strived to make it appear crooked' — wa yabghunaha 'iwaja. They want the straight path to look bent.\n\nThat is a precise description of a real activity. Not attacking the religion openly, but presenting it as unreasonable, distorted, harsh — making the road look like something no sensible person would walk.\n\nRecall 7:16, where the enemy said he would sit in ambush 'on Your Straight Path'. The people described here have taken up the same position and the same work.\n\n'And disbelieved in the Hereafter' — wa hum bi al-akhirati kafirun. This is placed last, and it is the engine of the other two.\n\nA person who genuinely expects the meeting described in 7:44 does not spend his life obstructing the way to it. Al-An'am identified the same root at 6:29 and 6:31.\n\nNotice that the curse of 7:44 was not pronounced for private sin. It was pronounced for a public project.\n\nThe surah is building towards its nation-by-nation narratives, and in every one of them the opposition takes exactly this form: chiefs who both refuse and obstruct.",
  "46": "'There will be a barrier between Paradise and Hell. And on the heights ˹of that barrier˺ will be people who will recognize ˹the residents of˺ both by their appearance. They will call out to the residents of Paradise, \"Peace be upon you!\" They will have not yet entered Paradise, but eagerly hope to.' (Quran 7:46). The verse that gives the surah its name.\n\n'A barrier' — hijab. A screen between the two destinations.\n\n'On the heights' — wa 'ala al-a'raf. 'Al-A'raf' is the plural of 'urf' — the high edge of something, the crest. This chapter is named after it.\n\n'People who will recognize the residents of both by their appearance' — bi simahum. By marks. They can see both sides, and they know who is who.\n\nWho are they? The early authorities differ, and honesty requires saying so. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records a range of reports and views — among them that they are people whose good and bad deeds were equal, and that they are others detained for a period before entry.\n\nWhat the verse itself establishes is their position and their hope. They are not in the Fire. They have not yet entered the Garden.\n\n'Peace be upon you' — salamun 'alaykum. Notice which way they speak first, and what they say. Their greeting goes to the residents of Paradise.\n\n'They eagerly hope to' — wa hum yatma'un. Hope, still active, still unresolved.\n\nThat is the state the verse leaves them in for three verses. It is an uncomfortable place to be left, and the discomfort is instructive.",
  "47": "'When their eyes will turn towards the residents of Hell, they will pray, \"Our Lord! Do not join us with the wrongdoing people.\"' (Quran 7:47). The other direction.\n\n'When their eyes will turn' — wa idha surifat absaruhum. The verb suggests the look being turned, as if not by choice.\n\nThey have just greeted the people of the Garden. Now they see the other side, and their reaction is a prayer.\n\n'Do not join us with the wrongdoing people' — rabbana la taj'alna ma'a al-qawmi al-zalimin.\n\nLook at what this prayer contains. No claim to a rank, no request for Paradise, no listing of deeds. Only: not that.\n\nIt is a prayer made by people who can see the outcome directly, which is precisely what nobody in this life can do. And yet the content of it is available to anyone.\n\nAl-An'am recorded a similar sight at 6:27: 'If only you could see when they will be detained before the Fire! They will cry, \"Woe to us! If only we could be sent back.\"'\n\nThe difference between that scene and this one is that these people are still asking, and still able to.\n\nThe surah has arranged something careful here. Between the certainty of 7:43 and the despair of 7:50, it places a group whose situation is undecided, and shows them praying.\n\nThat is the position of the reader. Not yet in either place, able to see both described, with the same prayer available.",
  "48": "'Those on the heights will call out to some ˹tyrants in the Fire˺, who they will recognize by their appearance, saying, \"Your large numbers and arrogance are of no use ˹today˺!\"' (Quran 7:48). The people on the heights speak a second time.\n\n'Who they will recognize by their appearance' — the same phrase as 7:46. They are identified in the Fire as easily as they were identified in the world.\n\n'Your large numbers' — ma aghna 'ankum jam'ukum. What you gathered: numbers, wealth, followers, everything accumulated.\n\n'And arrogance' — wa ma kuntum tastakbirun. And there is the word again — the verb used of Iblis at 7:13, of the deniers at 7:36 and 7:40.\n\nThe surah has now named arrogance in every section it has written. It is the single thread running from the refusal in the Garden to the closed gates of heaven.\n\n'Are of no use today' — the assets are not confiscated in this scene. They are simply revealed to have never been assets.\n\nThere is a sharp irony in who is speaking. The people on the heights have not been admitted anywhere. They are in the least secure position described in the entire chapter, and they are the ones addressing the powerful.\n\nThat reversal is the point. In this life the question 'what did all of it do for you?' can be asked and shrugged off. Here it cannot.\n\n'Jam'' — the gathering — is worth one last look. It is what the leaders of every nation in this surah will rely on, and what none of them keep.",
  "49": "'\"Are these ˹humble believers˺ the ones you swore would never be shown Allah's mercy?\" ˹Finally, those on the heights will be told:˺ \"Enter Paradise! There will be no fear for you, nor will you grieve.\"' (Quran 7:49). The question, and then the resolution.\n\n'Are these the ones you swore' — a ha'ula'i alladhina aqsamtum. They took an oath about other people's fate.\n\nThat is the charge, and it is a specific one. Not that they disbelieved, but that they issued verdicts on who was excluded from mercy — usually the poor believers whose status made the claim seem obvious.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this passage, reads the objects of the oath as the weak believers whom the arrogant despised in the world.\n\nNotice the wording again: 'would never be shown Allah's mercy'. Mercy is not theirs to allocate, and swearing about it is the most confident kind of ignorance — precisely what 7:33 forbade: 'attributing to Allah what you do not know.'\n\n'Enter Paradise!' — udkhulu al-jannah. The suspense of 7:46 to 7:48 ends in one word.\n\n'There will be no fear for you, nor will you grieve' — the exact promise of 7:35, given now to the people who had been left waiting.\n\nPlace the two halves of this verse together and the lesson is complete. The people who were sworn to be beyond mercy are in the Garden; the swearers are watching from the Fire.\n\nThe safest position on anyone else's standing with Allah is silence.",
  "50": "'The residents of the Fire will then cry out to the residents of Paradise, \"Aid us with some water or any provision Allah has granted you.\" They will reply, \"Allah has forbidden both to the disbelievers,\"' (Quran 7:50). The third conversation across the barrier, and the last.\n\n'Aid us with some water' — afidu 'alayna mina al-ma'. 'Afidu' — pour it out on us. The verb is used of something flowing over.\n\nWater, and then 'any provision Allah has granted you' — the request narrows to the most basic thing there is.\n\nSet this against 7:31 to 7:32, where the same surah insisted that the good things of the world were lawful, and 'they will be exclusively theirs on the Day of Judgment.' Exclusively. This verse is that word being enforced.\n\n'Allah has forbidden both to the disbelievers' — inna Allaha harramahuma 'ala al-kafirin. Forbidden. The same verb the deniers used wrongly in 7:32 when they made lawful things unlawful.\n\nThey spent their lives forbidding what Allah permitted. Now something is genuinely forbidden to them.\n\nNotice the direction of every exchange in this passage. At 7:44 the believers speak first; here the people of the Fire do. The requests all move one way.\n\nAnd notice what is not in the reply: no gloating, no reproach. A statement of the ruling, and then in the next verse the reason for it.\n\nThe scene is an argument aimed at the living. Everything being begged for here is currently being handed out for free.",
  "51": "'those who took this faith ˹of Islam˺ as mere amusement and play and were deluded by ˹their˺ worldly life.' ˹Allah will say,˺ \"Today We will ignore them just as they ignored the coming of this Day of theirs...\"' (Quran 7:51). The reason, in their own biography.\n\n'Took this faith as mere amusement and play' — ittakhadhu dinahum lahwan wa la'iban. Al-An'am used the identical phrase at 6:70.\n\n'Lahw' is distraction, the thing that occupies you pleasantly. 'La'ib' is play. Together they describe an attitude, not a doctrine.\n\nThat is what makes this verse uncomfortable. The charge is not that they denied loudly. It is that they treated the whole matter as not serious.\n\n'Deluded by their worldly life' — gharrathum al-hayat al-dunya. 'Ghurur' again — the same deception that took Adam at 7:22.\n\n'Today We will ignore them just as they ignored the coming of this Day' — fal-yawma nansahum kama nasu liqa'a yawmihim hadha. Surah al-Jathiyah puts it the same way at 45:34.\n\nThe punishment is described as a mirror. They forgot; they are forgotten. Nothing is added that they did not first do.\n\nNote that 'ignoring' is not active hostility. They did not campaign against the Day. They simply never got round to it, and the response matches exactly.\n\n'And for rejecting Our revelations' — wa ma kanu bi ayatina yajhadun.\n\nA life can be lost without a single decision to reject anything. That is the warning here.",
  "52": "'We have certainly brought them a Book which We explained with knowledge—a guide and mercy for those who believe.' (Quran 7:52). After eight verses of the Hereafter, the argument returns to the present.\n\n'We have certainly brought them a Book' — wa laqad ji'nahum bi kitab. Brought to them. The delivery is complete.\n\n'Which We explained with knowledge' — fassalnahu 'ala 'ilm. 'Tafsil' is separating something into its parts, laying it out item by item. Al-An'am used the same word at 6:114 and 6:126.\n\n'On knowledge' — 'ala 'ilm. The explanation rests on knowledge, which stands in direct contrast to the two charges of 7:28 and 7:33: speaking about Allah without knowledge.\n\nOne side has a detailed Book grounded in knowledge. The other has inherited custom and sworn opinions.\n\n'A guide and mercy for those who believe' — hudan wa rahmatan li qawmin yu'minun.\n\nThe qualification at the end is not a restriction on availability. The Book is brought to 'them' — everyone — and functions as guidance for those who accept it. A map is a map for anyone; it only guides the person who follows it.\n\nLook at where this verse falls. Immediately after the people of the Fire have been told they were ignored because they ignored. The answer to 'how could this happen?' is: a detailed Book was delivered, in advance, and explained.\n\nThat is the same defence the surah has made from 7:2 onwards. Nobody in this chapter is punished for a lack of information.",
  "53": "'Do they only await the fulfilment ˹of its warning˺?... \"The messengers of our Lord certainly came with the truth. Are there any intercessors who can plead on our behalf? Or can we be sent back so we may do ˹good,˺ unlike what we used to do?\"' (Quran 7:53). Waiting to see, and then two requests.\n\n'Do they only await the fulfilment' — hal yanzuruna illa ta'wilah. 'Ta'wil' here is the outcome, the thing itself arriving.\n\nSome people will not accept a warning until it happens. The verse describes that as a policy, and shows where it ends.\n\n'The messengers of our Lord certainly came with the truth' — the fifth confession in this surah, and word for word what the people of Paradise said at 7:43.\n\nThen the two requests. 'Are there any intercessors' — fa hal lana min shufa'a'. Someone to speak for us.\n\nAl-An'am closed that door at 6:94: 'now you have come to Us all alone.'\n\n'Or can we be sent back so we may do good, unlike what we used to do' — the request to return, which al-An'am answered at 6:28: 'if they were to be sent back, they would certainly revert to what they were forbidden.'\n\nBoth requests are for a different arrangement than the one they were given. Neither asks for forgiveness, which was the one request that worked at 7:23.\n\n'They will have certainly ruined themselves' — khasiru anfusahum. The scale of 7:9 again: light, and lost.\n\n'Whatever gods they fabricated will fail them' — dalla 'anhum. Gone missing, exactly as at 7:37.",
  "54": "'Indeed your Lord is Allah Who created the heavens and the earth in six Days, then established Himself on the Throne... The creation and the command belong to Him ˹alone˺. Blessed is Allah—Lord of all worlds!' (Quran 7:54). The argument moves from the Hereafter to the sky overhead.\n\n'In six Days' — fi sittati ayyam. The same statement appears at 10:3, 25:59 and 32:4. 'Yawm' in Arabic is a period; the Quran does not equate these with the days measured by the sun it is about to mention.\n\n'Then established Himself on the Throne' — thumma istawa 'ala al-'arsh. The position of the early Muslims on this phrase is well known and worth stating plainly: it is affirmed as He said it, without asking how, and without likening it to anything created. Al-Tabari, commenting on this verse, reports the meaning as His rising over the Throne in a manner befitting Him.\n\n'He makes the day and night overlap in rapid succession' — yughshi al-layla al-nahara yatlubuhu hathitha. The night pursues the day, urgently. Anyone who has watched dusk arrive knows the image is exact.\n\n'All subjected by His command' — musakhkharatin bi amrih. The sun, moon and stars are described as employed, not worshipped — the direct answer to the peoples who worshipped them.\n\n'The creation and the command belong to Him alone' — ala lahu al-khalqu wa al-amr. Both. He made it, and He runs it.\n\nThat single clause is the foundation of everything the surah has argued. If the making and the ordering are His, then the rulings of 7:33 are His to issue, and no one else's.\n\n'Blessed is Allah — Lord of all worlds.'",
  "55": "'Call upon your Lord humbly and secretly. Surely He does not like the transgressors.' (Quran 7:55). Having established who He is, the surah says how to approach Him.\n\n'Call upon your Lord' — ud'u rabbakum. 'Du'a' — asking, the most direct act of worship there is.\n\n'Humbly' — tadarru'an. Lowering yourself, pressing your need.\n\n'And secretly' — wa khufyah. Quietly, out of sight.\n\nThat second instruction is the one people resist. Abu Musa al-Ash'ari reported that when the companions raised their voices in praise on a journey, the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: 'O people! Be merciful to yourselves, for you are not calling a deaf or an absent one, but One Who is with you, no doubt He is All-Hearer, ever Near' (Sahih al-Bukhari 2992).\n\nVolume is for distance. Al-Baqarah states the reason at 2:186: 'I am truly near. I respond to one's prayer when they call upon Me.'\n\n'Surely He does not like the transgressors' — al-mu'tadin. Those who overstep, and the word is placed here because supplication itself can be overstepped: by display, by asking for what is unlawful, by demanding rather than requesting.\n\nAl-An'am recorded the honest version at 6:63, where people in danger 'call upon Him with humility, openly and secretly.'\n\nNotice that the command is not to ask for less. Surah Ghafir has 'Call upon Me, I will respond to you' (Quran 40:60). It is to ask differently: lower, and quieter.\n\nThe surah will return to this at its very end, in 7:205.",
  "56": "'Do not spread corruption in the land after it has been set in order. And call upon Him with hope and fear. Indeed, Allah's mercy is always close to the good-doers.' (Quran 7:56). Two instructions and a promise.\n\n'Do not spread corruption in the land' — wa la tufsidu fi al-ard. 'Fasad' covers everything from injustice and bloodshed to ruining what works.\n\n'After it has been set in order' — ba'da islahiha. After its repair. The world was handed over functioning.\n\nThat clause is the argument. The default state of the earth described in 7:10 — established, stocked with means of living — is order. Corruption is always a later addition to something that was working.\n\nRemember the two verbs of 7:35: 'shuns evil and mends their ways' — 'aslaha', from the same root as 'islah' here. Human beings are asked to continue the repair, not to reverse it.\n\n'And call upon Him with hope and fear' — khawfan wa tama'an. The two together.\n\nFear alone produces despair; hope alone produces carelessness. Surah as-Sajdah describes the believers the same way at 32:16: 'invoking their Lord with hope and fear.'\n\nNote that 'tama'an' is the same word used of the people on the heights at 7:46, who 'eagerly hope to' enter. Their state is being recommended as a posture for the living.\n\n'Allah's mercy is always close to the good-doers' — inna rahmata Allahi qaribun mina al-muhsinin. Close. The same nearness the hadith at 7:55 insisted on.\n\nAl-An'am said He 'has taken it upon Himself to be Merciful' (Quran 6:12). Here that mercy is located: near the people who do good.",
  "57": "'He is the One Who sends the winds ushering in His mercy. When they bear heavy clouds, We drive them to a lifeless land and then cause rain to fall, producing every type of fruit. Similarly, We will bring the dead to life, so perhaps you will be mindful.' (Quran 7:57). An argument you can watch happen.\n\n'The winds ushering in His mercy' — bushran bayna yaday rahmatih. Good news arriving ahead of the mercy itself.\n\nRain is called 'mercy' here, and that naming was the Prophet's own habit. 'A'isha reported that on a windy or cloudy day anxiety showed on his face, and when rain fell he was delighted; he explained: 'I was afraid that it might be a calamity that might fall upon my Ummah' — and when he saw the rain he said: 'It is the mercy' (Sahih Muslim 899a).\n\n'Heavy clouds' — sahaban thiqalan. Loaded. Anyone who has seen a rain cloud recognises the weight in it.\n\n'We drive them to a lifeless land' — li baladin mayyit. A dead country.\n\n'And then cause rain to fall, producing every type of fruit' — the sequence is observable, repeatable, and free.\n\n'Similarly, We will bring the dead to life' — kadhalika nukhriju al-mawta. That is the conclusion, and it is the whole reason the description was given.\n\nThe argument runs: you already accept that dead ground produces living crops when water arrives. You watch it every year. Then the objection to resurrection is not about possibility.\n\nSurah Fatir puts it in one line at 35:9: 'Similar is the Resurrection.'\n\n'So perhaps you will be mindful' — la'allakum tadhakkarun. Remember. The evidence is not new information; it is information already in hand.",
  "58": "'The fertile land produces abundantly by the Will of its Lord, whereas the infertile land hardly produces anything. This is how We vary ˹Our˺ lessons to those who are thankful.' (Quran 7:58). The same rain, two results.\n\n'The fertile land' — al-baladu al-tayyib. The good land.\n\n'Produces abundantly by the Will of its Lord' — yakhruju nabatuhu bi idhni rabbih. The soil does not manufacture the crop; the permission is named.\n\n'The infertile land hardly produces anything' — wa alladhi khabutha la yakhruju illa nakida. The bad ground yields only a grudging, stunted thing.\n\nNotice what has not changed between the two halves of this verse: the rain. Verse 7:57 described it falling; nothing suggests it fell unevenly.\n\nSo this is a statement about receivers, not about the gift. The revelation reaches the fertile and the barren alike, and the difference in outcome is the ground.\n\nAbu Musa reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, making the comparison directly: 'The example of guidance and knowledge with which Allah has sent me is like abundant rain falling on the earth, some of which was fertile soil that absorbed rain water and brought forth vegetation and grass in abundance' — while another portion 'was barren which could neither hold the water nor bring forth vegetation' (Sahih al-Bukhari 79).\n\n'This is how We vary Our lessons' — kadhalika nusarrifu al-ayat. 'Tasrif' — turning the signs this way and that, presenting them from different angles. Al-An'am used the word at 6:46, 6:65 and 6:105.\n\n'To those who are thankful' — li qawmin yashkurun. And gratitude is the fault named at 7:10 and predicted at 7:17.\n\nThis verse is the hinge. The next one begins the histories, and every nation in them is a soil sample.",
  "59": "'Indeed, We sent Noah to his people. He said, \"O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him. I truly fear for you the torment of a tremendous Day.\"' (Quran 7:59). The first of the seven histories begins.\n\n'We sent Noah to his people' — laqad arsalna Nuhan ila qawmih. The first messenger sent to a people who had gone astray.\n\n'O my people' — ya qawmi. That is the first thing to notice, and it recurs in every story that follows. He addresses them as his own.\n\n'Worship Allah — you have no other god except Him' — u'budu Allaha ma lakum min ilahin ghayruh. This exact sentence will be repeated by Hud at 7:65, by Salih at 7:73, and by Shu'ayb at 7:85.\n\nFour messengers, four centuries apart, one sentence. The surah repeats it deliberately so the reader cannot miss that the message never varied.\n\n'I truly fear for you' — inni akhafu 'alaykum. He is afraid on their behalf, not of them.\n\nThat is the posture of every prophet in this chapter, and it explains why their warnings are not threats. Surah Nuh opens with the commission itself at 71:1: 'Warn your people before a painful punishment comes to them.'\n\n'The torment of a tremendous Day' — 'adhaba yawmin 'azim.\n\nPlace this against 7:34: every community has a term. The histories that follow are that verse worked out in detail, and Nuh's people are the first case.\n\nNotice what he does not bring: no miracle, no demand, no politics. A single instruction and a warning.",
  "60": "'But the chiefs of his people said, \"We surely see that you are clearly misguided.\"' (Quran 7:60). The first response, and it sets the pattern for the whole surah.\n\n'The chiefs' — al-mala'. The notables, the assembly of leading men. This word will appear again at 7:66, 7:75, 7:88, 7:90, 7:103 and 7:109.\n\nTrack it and the structure of the chapter becomes visible. In every single nation, the opposition is led by the same social class.\n\nThat is not a coincidence, and the surah has already explained it. Those with the most invested in the current arrangement have the most to lose from a message that reorders it.\n\n'We surely see that you are clearly misguided' — inna la naraka fi dalalin mubin. Note the confidence: we see it, and it is obvious.\n\nThe accusation is the exact reverse of the truth. He came to correct their straying; they diagnose him as the one who is lost.\n\nNotice also that they offer no argument. There is no counter-evidence, no theological objection, no alternative account. Just a verdict delivered with certainty.\n\nVerse 7:30 already described this condition: people who take devils as their masters 'thinking they are rightly guided.' The certainty is the symptom.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, notes that this is the standard reply of every rejecting nation to its prophet — the accusation of misguidance thrown at the one sent to guide.\n\nThe reply comes next, and its tone is worth waiting for.",
  "61": "'He replied, \"O my people! I am not misguided! But I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds,\"' (Quran 7:61). The answer to the insult.\n\n'O my people' — ya qawmi. He says it again. They have just called him lost, and he still claims them.\n\n'I am not misguided' — laysa bi dalalatun. A flat denial, and nothing more. No counter-accusation.\n\nRead the exchange as a whole: they called him misguided, and he does not call them anything. That restraint is not weakness. It is the discipline of someone who is delivering a message rather than winning an argument.\n\n'But I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds' — wa lakinni rasulun min rabbi al-'alamin.\n\nHe answers the charge by naming the source. The question is not whether Nuh is impressive; it is who sent him.\n\n'Rabb al-'alamin' — the Lord of all worlds. That title was used at 7:54 for the One who created the heavens and the earth and runs them. Hud will use it in identical words at 7:67.\n\nThe claim being made is therefore very specific. He is not offering an opinion for their consideration. He is reporting.\n\nNotice what happens to the conversation. They made it about his character; he returns it to its subject.\n\nAnyone who has tried to say something true and been answered with a comment about themselves will recognise the move — and this verse shows the correct response to it.",
  "62": "'conveying to you my Lord's messages and giving you ˹sincere˺ advice. And I know from Allah what you do not know.' (Quran 7:62). The job description.\n\nThree clauses, and together they define what a prophet is.\n\n'Conveying to you my Lord's messages' — uballighukum risalati rabbi. Delivery. The message is not his, and he does not adjust it.\n\n'And giving you sincere advice' — wa ansahu lakum. 'Nush' — the same root as 'nasih', sincere counsel given purely for the benefit of the one receiving it.\n\nThat word should stop the reader, because it has appeared before in this surah. At 7:21 the enemy of mankind swore: 'I am truly your sincere advisor.'\n\nThe claim is identical; the speakers could not be more different. And the surah gives the reader a way to tell them apart: one swore an oath and offered a promotion, the other simply delivers and expects nothing.\n\n'And I know from Allah what you do not know' — wa a'lamu mina Allahi ma la ta'lamun. This is the one advantage he claims, and it is not personal superiority.\n\nIt is the answer to 'we see that you are misguided' in 7:60. Their seeing is limited to what is visible; his knowledge came from outside it.\n\nCompare 7:33, which forbade 'attributing to Allah what you do not know.' The prophet is the one person entitled to speak, and only because he was told.\n\nNothing in this description involves force, and nothing involves personal gain.",
  "63": "'Do you find it astonishing that a reminder should come to you from your Lord through one of your own, warning you, so you may beware and perhaps be shown mercy?' (Quran 7:63). The question that answers the real objection.\n\n'Do you find it astonishing' — a wa 'ajibtum. Are you amazed?\n\n'Through one of your own' — 'ala rajulin minkum. A man from among you.\n\nThere is the actual complaint. Not the content of the message — the identity of the messenger. They knew him, knew his family, remembered him as a boy.\n\nVerse 7:35 already stated the principle: 'messengers from among yourselves come to you.' Here a nation is shown objecting to precisely that.\n\nAnd it is the same objection Iblis made at 7:12: the recipient of the honour does not deserve it. Fire above clay, chief above ordinary man.\n\n'A reminder' — dhikr. Not new information; a reminder of what was already known and forgotten.\n\n'So you may beware and perhaps be shown mercy' — wa li tattaqu wa la'allakum turhamun. The purpose of the warning is stated: mercy, not punishment.\n\nThat is worth pausing on. The warning exists in order to be heeded. It is not an announcement of a fixed verdict; it is an intervention offered in advance.\n\n'Perhaps you will be shown mercy' — the same 'la'alla' construction that has run through the surah at 7:26, 7:57 and 7:69.\n\nThe whole verse is one question, and it is a fair one. Why would a message from your Lord arrive by any other route?",
  "64": "'But they rejected him, so We saved him and those with him in the Ark, and drowned those who rejected Our signs. They were certainly a blind people.' (Quran 7:64). The entire flood in one verse.\n\n'But they rejected him' — fa kadhdhabuh. The verb that names the whole chapter's subject.\n\nNotice the compression. Surah Hud gives this story thirty verses; Surah Nuh gives it a whole chapter. Al-A'raf gives it one.\n\nThat is because the surah is not narrating for its own sake. It is building a series, and the series needs each case stated in the same shape so the pattern shows.\n\n'We saved him and those with him in the Ark' — the rescue is mentioned before the drowning. That order is consistent throughout this chapter.\n\n'And drowned those who rejected Our signs' — wa aghraqna alladhina kadhdhabu bi ayatina. The charge is named as denial of signs — the same charge laid at 7:9, 7:36, 7:37 and 7:40.\n\n'They were certainly a blind people' — innahum kanu qawman 'amin. Blind.\n\nThe word is chosen with care, because their complaint in 7:60 was 'we surely see'. They claimed sight. The verdict is blindness.\n\nAl-An'am gave the principle at 6:104: 'whoever chooses to see, it is for their own good. But whoever chooses to be blind, it is to their own loss.'\n\nBlindness here is not a disability. It is a verdict on people who had eyes, had a warning, had a man they knew telling them the truth for a very long time, and looked away.",
  "65": "'And to the people of 'Ad We sent their brother Hud. He said, \"O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him. Will you not then fear Him?\"' (Quran 7:65). The second history, and the sentence repeats.\n\n'Their brother Hud' — akhahum Hudan. Brother — from their own tribe, exactly as 7:63 argued a messenger should be.\n\n'Worship Allah — you have no other god except Him' — the identical words of Nuh at 7:59. Generations apart, a different nation, a different civilisation, and not one word changed.\n\nThat repetition is the surah's central evidence. If these messages were invented, they would differ. They do not.\n\n'Ad were a real people in southern Arabia, and the Quran describes them concretely. Surah al-Fajr calls them 'the people of Iram — with their great stature, unmatched in any other land' (Quran 89:7 and 89:8).\n\nSurah Fussilat records their slogan at 41:15: 'Who is superior to us in might?'\n\nSo this is a nation defined by strength and building. Their reply to Hud will be shaped entirely by that.\n\n'Will you not then fear Him?' — a fa la tattaqun. Nuh warned of a tremendous Day; Hud asks a question.\n\nThe difference in approach is worth noticing. Each prophet speaks to his own people in their own register, and the core instruction stays fixed while the framing adapts.\n\nThat is a working model for anyone who has to say something true to someone who does not want to hear it.",
  "66": "'The disbelieving chiefs of his people responded, \"We surely see you as a fool, and we certainly think you are a liar.\"' (Quran 7:66). The second rejection, from the same social class.\n\n'The chiefs' — al-mala' again, exactly as at 7:60. The pattern is now visible.\n\n'We surely see you as a fool' — inna la naraka fi safahah. 'Safah' is lightness of mind — foolishness, poor judgement, the opposite of sound sense.\n\nCompare their opening words to those of Nuh's chiefs: 'We surely see that you are clearly misguided' (Quran 7:60). Same construction, same confidence, different insult.\n\nTwo nations, separated by centuries, produce nearly the same sentence. The surah lets the repetition make its own argument.\n\n'And we certainly think you are a liar' — wa inna la nazunnuka mina al-kadhibin. Note the verb: 'nazunnu' — we think, we suppose.\n\nThat is an admission buried in the accusation. They are not claiming to know. They are stating a suspicion with the force of a verdict.\n\nThe Quran is precise about this distinction. Verse 7:33 forbade 'attributing to Allah what you do not know', and the whole argument of the surah rests on the difference between knowing and supposing.\n\nNotice also what the accusation of foolishness reveals about them. To a people whose identity was strength and stature — 'who is superior to us in might?' (Quran 41:15) — a man calling them to humility before an unseen Lord would look exactly like a fool.\n\nEvery nation's insult exposes what that nation values.",
  "67": "'Hud replied, \"O my people! I am no fool! But I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds,\"' (Quran 7:67). The same reply Nuh gave.\n\nSet the two side by side. Nuh: 'O my people! I am not misguided! But I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds' (Quran 7:61). Hud: 'O my people! I am no fool! But I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds.'\n\nOnly the insult changes. The structure of the answer is identical: deny the charge in a few words, then name the source.\n\n'O my people' — again. Called a fool and a liar to his face, and he still opens with the word that claims kinship.\n\n'I am no fool' — laysa bi safahah. Four words in Arabic, and then he moves on.\n\nThere is a lesson in the brevity that the surah is clearly making by repeating it. He does not defend his intelligence, list his qualities, or demand an apology.\n\nAnyone whose case rests on personal credibility has to fight over personal credibility. Anyone carrying a message from elsewhere does not.\n\n'Rabb al-'alamin' — the Lord of all worlds. The same title, and it is the answer to their boast at 41:15. They asked who is superior in might; he names Him.\n\nNotice how the surah is building. Two histories in, and the reader can already predict the shape: message, insult from the chiefs, calm reply, warning, refusal, term arrives.\n\nThat predictability is deliberate. Verse 7:34 stated the rule; these are the demonstrations.",
  "68": "'conveying to you my Lord's messages. And I am your sincere advisor.' (Quran 7:68). Hud's job description, matching Nuh's at 7:62.\n\n'Conveying to you my Lord's messages' — uballighukum risalati rabbi. Word for word what Nuh said.\n\n'And I am your sincere advisor' — wa ana lakum nasihun amin. And here Hud adds a word Nuh did not use: 'amin'.\n\n'Amin' means trustworthy — one who keeps what is entrusted to him and does not tamper with it.\n\nThat single word answers the accusation of 7:66 exactly. They said 'we certainly think you are a liar'; he says he is 'amin'.\n\nAnd it answers something else. At 7:21 the enemy swore he was 'min al-nasihin' — a sincere advisor. He offered counsel; he was not trustworthy. Hud claims both together.\n\nThis is the test the surah has been constructing. Everyone offering advice claims sincerity. The question is whether the adviser can be trusted with what he was given.\n\nNotice what Hud does not claim: not clever, not powerful, not deserving. Trustworthy, and a deliverer of messages.\n\nThose were the two qualities his own people had already observed in him for a lifetime, before he said a word about religion. That is why the claim could be made at all.\n\nThe Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him, was known in Mecca by the same word before revelation came, and his people did not dispute it even while rejecting his message.\n\nCharacter precedes the argument, and often survives it.",
  "69": "'Do you find it astonishing that a reminder should come to you from your Lord through one of your own so he may warn you? Remember that He made you successors after the people of Noah and increased you greatly in stature. So remember Allah's favours, so you may be successful.' (Quran 7:69). Hud's argument, and it is built from their own history.\n\nThe opening question is Nuh's question at 7:63, repeated almost exactly.\n\nThen Hud adds something Nuh did not have available: a precedent.\n\n'He made you successors after the people of Noah' — khulafa'a min ba'di qawmi Nuh. You are the replacements. Al-An'am described the process at 6:6: 'Then We destroyed them for their sins and replaced them with other peoples.'\n\nThink about what that means for 'Ad. Their position exists because a previous nation was removed. The vacancy they occupy was created by a rejection exactly like the one they are considering.\n\n'And increased you greatly in stature' — wa zadakum fi al-khalqi bastah. Extension in build — the great stature of 89:7.\n\nHud takes the very thing they boast about and reassigns its ownership. Your strength is a favour, not an achievement.\n\n'So remember Allah's favours' — fadhkuru ala'a Allah. Remember, the same verb as 7:57.\n\n'So you may be successful' — la'allakum tuflihun. 'Falah' — the word from the balance at 7:8, where those whose scale is heavy are 'al-muflihun'.\n\nThe whole argument is one sentence: everything you are proud of was given to you, by the One you are refusing, after He removed the last people who refused Him.",
  "70": "'They said, \"Have you come to us so that we would worship Allah alone and abandon what our forefathers used to worship? Then bring us what you threaten us with, if what you say is true!\"' (Quran 7:70). Their answer, in two parts.\n\n'Abandon what our forefathers used to worship' — ma kana ya'budu aba'una. The appeal to inheritance, which is the third time this surah has recorded it after 7:28 and 7:38.\n\nNotice what this reveals. Hud's argument in 7:69 was also about their past — but about what Allah did for them. Their reply is about what their fathers did.\n\nTwo versions of history, and each side is arguing from it. One points to the favours; the other to the customs.\n\n'Then bring us what you threaten us with' — fa'tina bima ta'iduna. The demand to see the punishment.\n\nThis is the most reckless sentence a nation can say, and the Quran records it more than once. Nuh's people said it in almost identical words at 11:32.\n\nAl-An'am answered the same demand at 6:57: 'That torment you seek to hasten is not within my power. It is only Allah Who decides its time.'\n\n'If what you say is true' — in kunta mina al-sadiqin. They frame it as a test of his honesty.\n\nBut look at the position it puts them in. They have made the arrival of the punishment their own evidence standard. When it comes, the case will be proved — and there will be nobody left to concede it.\n\nThat is the trap of 7:53: waiting for the outcome as a substitute for weighing the argument.",
  "71": "'He said, \"You will certainly be subjected to your Lord's torment and wrath. Do you dispute with me regarding the so-called gods which you and your forefathers have made up—a practice Allah has never authorized? Then wait! I too am waiting with you.\"' (Quran 7:71). Hud's last words to 'Ad.\n\n'Torment and wrath' — rijsun wa ghadab. 'Rijs' is filth, defilement; here it names the punishment as something foul.\n\n'Do you dispute with me regarding the so-called gods... which you and your forefathers have made up' — asma'in sammaytumuha. Literally: names which you named.\n\nThat is the whole argument against their religion in three words. The gods are names. Somebody chose them, and the choosing is the only thing behind them.\n\n'A practice Allah has never authorized' — ma nazzala Allahu biha min sultan. No authority was sent down for it. This is the exact phrase used at 7:33 when the surah listed what was actually forbidden.\n\nThe demand throughout al-A'raf is consistent: produce the evidence. Al-An'am pressed it repeatedly, at 6:143 and 6:148.\n\n'Then wait! I too am waiting with you' — fantaziru inni ma'akum mina al-muntazirin.\n\nThey asked him at 7:70 to bring the punishment. He does not bring it, because it is not his to bring, and he says so by simply agreeing to wait.\n\nAl-An'am ends the same way at 6:158: 'Keep waiting! We too are waiting.'\n\nThere is nothing left for a messenger to say to people who have made the punishment their evidence standard. He has delivered, advised, and been called a liar. Now both parties wait for the same event.",
  "72": "'So We saved him and those with him by Our mercy and uprooted those who denied Our signs. They were not believers.' (Quran 7:72). The second nation reaches its term.\n\n'So We saved him and those with him' — the rescue first, exactly as with Nuh at 7:64.\n\n'By Our mercy' — bi rahmatin minna. And that phrase is added here. The survivors are not credited with saving themselves.\n\nRecall 7:43, where the people of Paradise refuse the credit: 'We would have never been guided if Allah had not guided us.' The same correction is applied to a rescue in this world.\n\n'And uprooted those who denied Our signs' — wa qata'na dabira alladhina kadhdhabu bi ayatina. 'Qata'na dabir' — We cut off their last trace. Nothing remained to continue the line.\n\nIbn 'Abbas reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'I was granted victory with As-Saba and the nation of 'Ad was destroyed by Ad-Dabur' — the westerly wind (Sahih al-Bukhari 1035).\n\nSo a people whose boast was 'who is superior to us in might?' (Quran 41:15) were finished by moving air. Surah al-Ahqaf describes them mistaking the approaching punishment for a rain cloud at 46:24.\n\n'They were not believers' — wa ma kanu mu'minin. The verdict is stated last, flatly, with no elaboration.\n\nNote the contrast the surah builds into every one of these accounts: the strength each nation trusted is the thing that fails, and the mercy they dismissed is what saves the few.",
  "73": "'And to the people of Thamud We sent their brother Salih. He said, \"O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him. A clear proof has come to you from your Lord: this is Allah's she-camel as a sign to you...\"' (Quran 7:73). The third history, and the sentence appears a third time.\n\n'Their brother Salih' — one of their own, as with Hud at 7:65.\n\n'O my people! Worship Allah — you have no other god except Him' — identical to 7:59 and 7:65. Three nations, three messengers, one line.\n\n'A clear proof has come to you' — qad ja'atkum bayyinah. And this time a visible sign is given, which the previous two did not have.\n\n'This is Allah's she-camel' — hadhihi naqatu Allah. The camel is attributed to Allah directly, which is why harming her is framed as defiance rather than theft.\n\n'So leave her to graze freely on Allah's land and do not harm her' — the whole obligation is to leave something alone.\n\nConsider how light that is. Not a tax, not labour, not a pilgrimage. One animal, unmolested.\n\nSurah ash-Shu'ara adds the arrangement at 26:155: 'She will have her turn to drink as you have yours, each on an appointed day.'\n\nSo the cost was a shared water rota. That is the entire test.\n\n'Or else you will be overcome by a painful punishment' — the consequence stated in advance, as it was to Adam at 7:19.\n\nThamud lived in the rocky north-west of Arabia. Their dwellings are still there, which is why the next verse can speak about them so concretely.",
  "74": "'Remember when He made you successors after 'Ad and established you in the land—˹and˺ you built palaces on its plains and carved homes into mountains. So remember Allah's favours, and do not go about spreading corruption in the land.' (Quran 7:74). Salih's argument, built exactly like Hud's.\n\n'Successors after 'Ad' — khulafa'a min ba'di 'Ad. The same sentence Hud made to 'Ad at 7:69, with one name changed.\n\nRead the two together and a chain appears: 'Ad replaced Nuh's people, Thamud replaced 'Ad. Each nation occupies a space cleared by the previous refusal, and each is reminded of it.\n\n'You built palaces on its plains and carved homes into mountains' — tattakhidhuna min suhuliha qusuran wa tanhituna al-jibala buyutan. Two building techniques, one for soft ground and one for rock.\n\nThis is unusually specific, and it is checkable. The carved rock facades of al-Hijr still stand in northern Arabia.\n\nIbn 'Umar reported that when the Prophet, peace be upon him, passed al-Hijr during the expedition to Tabuk, he ordered his companions not to drink from its well or take water from it, and when they said they had already kneaded dough with that water, he ordered the dough thrown away (Sahih al-Bukhari 3378).\n\nHe also said, as Ibn 'Umar reported: 'Do not enter the places of these people where Allah's punishment had fallen unless you do so weeping' (Sahih al-Bukhari 433).\n\n'So remember Allah's favours' — the same instruction as 7:69.\n\n'And do not go about spreading corruption in the land' — the command of 7:56, repeated to a specific nation.\n\nTheir skill was real. Its ownership was the question.",
  "75": "'The arrogant chiefs of his people asked the lowly who believed among them, \"Are you certain that Salih has been sent by his Lord?\" They replied, \"We certainly believe in what he has been sent with.\"' (Quran 7:75). The social shape of belief, laid bare.\n\n'The arrogant chiefs' — al-mala'u alladhina istakbaru. The word 'istakbaru' again, from Iblis at 7:13 through the deniers at 7:36, 7:40 and 7:48.\n\n'The lowly who believed among them' — lilladhina istud'ifu liman amana minhum. Those who were made weak — the word describes a condition imposed on them, not a quality they had.\n\nSo the division is now explicit. The leaders reject; the powerless believe.\n\nThat is the arrangement in every one of these histories, and it was already anticipated at 7:49, where the arrogant had sworn that certain people would never receive mercy — and those people entered the Garden.\n\n'Are you certain that Salih has been sent by his Lord?' — the question is asked in a tone. It is not an inquiry; it is pressure applied by people with power over the ones being questioned.\n\nLook at the answer. 'We certainly believe in what he has been sent with' — inna bima ursila bihi mu'minun.\n\nThey do not argue, do not produce proofs, do not match the sneer. They restate their position and stop.\n\nAnd notice the precision of their words: they believe in what he was sent with. Not in him personally — in the message. That is exactly the distinction Nuh drew at 7:61 and Hud at 7:67.",
  "76": "'The arrogant said, \"We surely reject what you believe in.\"' (Quran 7:76). Eight words, and they are the whole of the reply.\n\n'We surely reject' — inna billadhi amantum bihi kafirun. We are deniers of the very thing you believe in.\n\nThere is no argument here at all. The believers gave an answer in 7:75; this is not a response to it.\n\nThat absence is the point of recording the exchange. Across three nations now, the surah has documented the rejections in full — 'clearly misguided' at 7:60, 'a fool' and 'a liar' at 7:66, and this — and not one of them contains a reason.\n\nSet that against what the messengers offer: a stated source, a stated message, a checkable history, and in Salih's case a visible sign.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this passage, observes that the arrogant answer with nothing but their own refusal, having no counter-proof to bring.\n\nNotice also who is being addressed. The chiefs do not answer Salih; they answer the weak believers. The declaration is aimed downward, at the people they have authority over.\n\nThat tells you what the statement is for. It is not persuasion; it is a warning to the rest of the community about which side is safe.\n\nSurah ash-Shams describes Thamud's condition in one line at 91:11: 'Thamud rejected the truth out of arrogance.'\n\nThe Quran keeps naming the same disease. Three nations, three insults, one root.",
  "77": "'Then they killed the she-camel—defying their Lord's command—and challenged ˹Salih˺, \"Bring us what you threaten us with, if you are ˹truly˺ one of the messengers.\"' (Quran 7:77). The one prohibition, broken.\n\n'Then they killed the she-camel' — fa 'aqaru al-naqah. 'Aqr' is hamstringing — cutting the leg tendons, a specific and cruel method.\n\nRecall what was asked of them at 7:73: leave her alone. Nothing else.\n\nSurah al-Qamar notes that the deed was done by one man acting for all: 'But they roused a companion of theirs, so he dared to kill her' (Quran 54:29). One hand, and the whole nation owns the act.\n\nThat is worth sitting with. The Quran assigns the killing to 'they', because the community incited it and approved it. Standing by while something is done in your name is participation.\n\n'Defying their Lord's command' — wa 'ataw 'an amri rabbihim. They went beyond it, deliberately.\n\n'Bring us what you threaten us with, if you are truly one of the messengers' — and this is now the second time this surah has recorded that exact demand, after 'Ad at 7:70.\n\nThe wording is almost identical, from a different nation in a different century. The surah keeps showing the reader that rejection is not creative; it recycles.\n\nNotice the escalation across the three histories. Nuh's people insulted him. 'Ad insulted and demanded. Thamud insulted, destroyed the sign, and demanded.",
  "78": "'Then an ˹overwhelming˺ earthquake struck them, and they fell lifeless in their homes.' (Quran 7:78). The answer to the demand of the previous verse.\n\n'An overwhelming earthquake' — al-rajfah. A violent shaking.\n\nNote how immediately it follows. Verse 7:77 ends with 'bring us what you threaten us with'; verse 7:78 begins with the arrival. The surah places no gap between the challenge and the answer.\n\nSurah Hud describes it as 'the mighty blast' at 11:67 and gives them three days' notice at 11:65. Surah Fussilat calls it 'the blast of a disgracing punishment' (Quran 41:17). The accounts describe the same event from different angles.\n\n'They fell lifeless in their homes' — fa asbahu fi darihim jathimin. 'Jathimin' — collapsed, fallen forward and still, the way a body lies where it dropped.\n\n'In their homes' — and there is the sharpest detail. The homes are the ones described in 7:74: palaces on the plains and dwellings carved into the mountain rock.\n\nThey cut those houses out of solid stone. The stone was still standing; they were not.\n\nRecall 7:4, where nations were struck 'while sleeping at night or in the afternoon.' The theme returns: destruction arrives in the place of safety.\n\nThe verse is eight words long in Arabic. The Quran spent five verses on the warning and one line on the outcome, and that proportion is itself the message.\n\nWarnings are given at length because they are meant to work.",
  "79": "'So he turned away from them, saying, \"O my people! Surely I conveyed to you my Lord's message and gave you ˹sincere˺ advice, but you do not like ˹sincere˺ advisors.\"' (Quran 7:79). Salih speaks to a dead city.\n\n'So he turned away from them' — fa tawalla 'anhum. He leaves.\n\n'O my people' — ya qawmi. He is standing among their bodies, and he still calls them his people.\n\nThat single word carries the whole grief of the scene. Three prophets in this surah have used it — Nuh at 7:61, Hud at 7:67, Salih here — and this is the only time it is said to people who cannot hear.\n\n'Surely I conveyed to you my Lord's message and gave you sincere advice' — laqad ablaghtukum risalata rabbi wa nasahtu lakum. The two verbs of 7:62 and 7:68, now in the past tense.\n\nHe is not gloating. He is closing the account: this is what I was sent to do, and I did it.\n\n'But you do not like sincere advisors' — wa lakin la tuhibbuna al-nasihin. The final diagnosis, and it is about affection, not argument.\n\nThey did not lose a debate. They disliked being advised, and that dislike was enough.\n\nLook back at 7:21, where the enemy called himself a 'nasih' and was believed instantly, and at 7:68, where Hud called himself a trustworthy 'nasih' and was called a liar.\n\nThe counsel people accept is the counsel that flatters them. That is the mechanism this surah keeps exposing, and it did not stop working when Thamud fell.",
  "80": "'And ˹remember˺ when Lot scolded ˹the men of˺ his people, ˹saying,˺ \"Do you commit a shameful deed that no man has ever done before?\"' (Quran 7:80). The fourth history, and it breaks the pattern.\n\nEvery previous story opened the same way: 'We sent X to his people... Worship Allah, you have no other god except Him.' This one does not.\n\nLut is not introduced as being sent to them at all — the Quran presents him as living among them. And the first thing he addresses is not their worship.\n\nThat difference is deliberate. The three earlier nations were called away from false gods. This nation is confronted about conduct.\n\n'A shameful deed' — al-fahishah. The word the surah used at 7:28 for the practice defended by ancestral custom, and listed at 7:33 among the things actually forbidden.\n\n'That no man has ever done before' — ma sabaqakum biha min ahadin mina al-'alamin. Surah al-'Ankabut records the same phrase at 29:28.\n\nSo the charge includes originality. They were not maintaining an inherited practice, which was the excuse offered at 7:28 and 7:70. This one they began.\n\nNotice that Lut opens with a question, not a sentence. 'Do you commit' — a fa'tuna. He asks them to look at what they are doing.\n\nThat has been the method of every messenger in this chapter: Hud asked 'will you not then fear Him?' at 7:65, and 'do you find it astonishing?' at 7:69.\n\nQuestions leave room to answer. The next two verses show what these people did with that room.",
  "81": "'You lust after men instead of women! You are certainly transgressors.' (Quran 7:81). The charge stated plainly.\n\n'You lust after men instead of women' — innakum lata'tuna al-rijala shahwatan min duni al-nisa'. Surah ash-Shu'ara puts it at 26:165 and adds at 26:166: 'leaving the wives that your Lord has created for you.'\n\nThe Quran states this without euphemism and without excess. One sentence of description, one of judgement.\n\n'You are certainly transgressors' — bal antum qawmun musrifun. And that word deserves attention: 'musrifun' — from 'israf', going past the limit.\n\nIt is the same word used at 7:31: 'Eat and drink, but do not waste. Surely He does not like the wasteful.' Overstepping the measure.\n\nThe surah is consistent. Every ruling it has given has the same shape — a wide permission with a boundary — and every failure it records is a boundary crossed.\n\nNote also that 7:31 was about food and clothing, ordinary things. The vocabulary does not change when the subject becomes grave. The category is the same: a limit set by the One who created the arrangement, and a decision to exceed it.\n\nLut says this to their faces, in their own town, as a resident. He has no army, no tribe behind him, and no protection.\n\nThat is the position of anyone who has to name something in a place where naming it is unwelcome. The Quran preserves his words as an example of how it is done: accurate, brief, and unashamed.",
  "82": "'But his people's only response was to say, \"Expel them from your land! They are a people who wish to remain chaste!\"' (Quran 7:82). The reply, and it is astonishing.\n\n'His people's only response' — wa ma kana jawaba qawmihi illa an qalu. There was no other answer. That construction means the following sentence is all they had.\n\n'Expel them from your land' — akhrijuhum min qaryatikum. Deport them.\n\n'They are a people who wish to remain chaste' — innahum unasun yatatahharun. People who keep themselves pure.\n\nRead that again, because the Quran is recording it as an accusation. The stated grounds for expulsion are the purity of the ones being expelled.\n\nThe mask is entirely off. There is no charge of lying here, no claim that Lut is a fool or misguided — the insults offered at 7:60, 7:66 and 7:76. The complaint is that these people will not participate.\n\nThat is what makes this the most honest rejection in the surah. Every other nation dressed its refusal in an accusation. This one simply states that decency is intolerable.\n\nNotice also who is to be expelled: 'them', not 'him'. Lut and the few with him, the same small group of believers who appear in every one of these accounts.\n\nSurah al-'Ankabut records another response from them at 29:29: 'Bring Allah's punishment upon us, if what you say is true' — the third nation in this chapter to say it.\n\nExpulsion is a familiar tactic. When an argument cannot be won, the arguer can still be removed.",
  "83": "'So We saved him and his family except his wife, who was one of the doomed.' (Quran 7:83). The rescue, with one exception.\n\n'So We saved him and his family' — fa anjaynahu wa ahlahu. The pattern holds: the believers are removed before the punishment arrives, as at 7:64 and 7:72.\n\n'Except his wife' — illa imra'atahu. And here the surah does something it has not done before.\n\nIn the three previous histories the line ran between the messenger's people and the messenger's followers. Here it runs through his household.\n\n'Who was one of the doomed' — kanat mina al-ghabirin. Among those who stayed behind.\n\nSurah at-Tahrim states the principle openly at 66:10, naming 'the wife of Noah and the wife of Lot' — each married to a righteous servant, each unfaithful to him — and concludes: 'So their husbands were of no benefit to them against Allah whatsoever.'\n\nThat is a hard verse, and it is meant to be. Proximity to a prophet saves no one. It did not save her, living in his house, hearing everything he said.\n\nCompare 7:53, where people in the Fire ask for intercessors. The answer given here is a fact rather than an argument: the closest possible relationship to a messenger of Allah produced nothing.\n\nNotice how little is said about her. No name, no speech, one clause.\n\nThe surah has recorded chiefs and nations and civilisations. It also records that the count is individual, and that the smallest unit of this reckoning is one person standing alone.",
  "84": "'We poured upon them a rain ˹of brimstone˺. See what was the end of the wicked!' (Quran 7:84). The fourth destruction, and a direct instruction to the reader.\n\n'We poured upon them a rain' — wa amtarna 'alayhim matara. Rain, and the word is chosen for its irony.\n\nVerse 7:57 called rain 'His mercy' — the winds ushering it in, the dead land revived. Twenty-seven verses later the same sky delivers the opposite.\n\nSurah al-Hijr specifies what fell: 'We turned the cities upside down and rained upon them stones of baked clay' (Quran 15:74).\n\n'See what was the end of the wicked' — fanzur kayfa kana 'aqibatu al-mujrimin. Look. An imperative, addressed to whoever is reading.\n\nThis is the first time in the sequence that the reader is told to look, and it changes the function of the whole passage. These are not stories being narrated. They are sites being pointed at.\n\nSurah al-Hijr makes the same point at 15:76: 'Their ruins still lie along a known route.' The places were on the trade road the Meccan audience travelled.\n\nThat is the argument al-An'am made at 6:11: 'Travel throughout the land and see the fate of the deniers.'\n\nFour nations are now on the record: drowned, blown away, shaken to the ground, and buried under stone. Different endings, one cause.\n\nAnd each was warned by a man from among them who asked nothing in return, and was disliked for it.",
  "85": "'And to the people of Midian We sent their brother Shu'aib. He said, \"O my people! Worship Allah—you have no other god except Him... So give just measure and weight, do not defraud people of their property, nor spread corruption in the land...\"' (Quran 7:85). The fifth history, and the sin is commercial.\n\n'Their brother Shu'aib' — from among them, as with Hud at 7:65 and Salih at 7:73.\n\n'Worship Allah — you have no other god except Him' — the fourth appearance of this exact sentence, after 7:59, 7:65 and 7:73.\n\nThen something new. Nuh, Hud and Salih each followed the call to worship with a warning. Shu'aib follows it with a rule about scales.\n\n'Give just measure and weight' — fa awfu al-kayla wa al-mizan. Fill the measure. Midian sat on the caravan routes; their livelihood was trade.\n\n'Do not defraud people of their property' — wa la tabkhasu al-nasa ashya'ahum. 'Bakhs' is shaving value off something — giving less than what was agreed while appearing to give it.\n\nSurah al-Mutaffifin opens with the same offence at 83:1: 'Woe to the defrauders!'\n\nIbn 'Abbas reported that when the Prophet, peace be upon him, came to Madinah, its people were among the worst in weights and measures, and after that surah was revealed 'they were fair in weights and measures after that' (Sunan Ibn Majah 2223, graded hasan).\n\n'Nor spread corruption in the land after it has been set in order' — the exact command of 7:56, now applied to a marketplace.\n\nThat connection is the lesson. Worship and the weighing scale are named in one breath, as one religion.",
  "86": "'And do not lie in ambush on every road—threatening and hindering those who believe in Allah from His Path and striving to make it ˹appear˺ crooked. Remember when you were few, then He increased you in number. And consider the fate of the corruptors!' (Quran 7:86). The second charge.\n\n'Do not lie in ambush on every road' — wa la taq'udu bi kulli siratin tu'idun. Do not sit on every road, threatening.\n\nThat phrasing should ring a bell. At 7:16 the enemy of mankind said: 'I will lie in ambush for them on Your Straight Path.' The verb is the same: sitting, waiting, on a road.\n\nMidian's chiefs have taken up his position. The surah does not say so; it simply uses his words and lets the reader notice.\n\n'Hindering those who believe in Allah from His Path and striving to make it appear crooked' — and this is a direct quotation of 7:45, which defined the people cursed at the barrier: 'those who hindered others from Allah's Way, strived to make it appear crooked.'\n\nSo the description given of the damned in the Hereafter is here found in a living community. That is how the surah connects its two halves.\n\n'Remember when you were few, then He increased you in number' — the same argument from history that Hud made at 7:69 and Salih at 7:74. Your numbers were given.\n\n'And consider the fate of the corruptors' — wanzuru kayfa kana 'aqibatu al-mufsidin. Look at how it ended for them — the instruction of 7:84, now spoken by a prophet to his own people.\n\nShu'aib is asking them to read the histories the reader has just read.",
  "87": "'If some of you do believe in what I have been sent with while others do not, then be patient until Allah judges between us. He is the Best of Judges.' (Quran 7:87). Instructions for a divided town.\n\n'If some of you do believe... while others do not' — the community has split. Shu'aib names the situation without pretending it away.\n\n'Then be patient' — fasbiru. Wait it out.\n\nThis is a remarkable instruction and worth dwelling on. A prophet with a mandate from Allah, facing people who defraud in the market and intimidate believers on the roads, tells his followers to be patient.\n\nNot to retaliate, not to seize the town, not to leave. To wait.\n\n'Until Allah judges between us' — hatta yahkuma Allahu baynana. The verdict belongs to Him, and it will be issued at a time He chooses — which is exactly the rule of 7:34: every community has a term, and it cannot be advanced.\n\n'He is the Best of Judges' — wa huwa khayru al-hakimin. Al-An'am used the same title at 6:57 when answering the demand to hasten the punishment.\n\nNotice the discipline being taught. The believers of Midian are told to endure people who are actively harming them, on the grounds that the case is already filed.\n\nCompare 7:71, where Hud said 'wait, I too am waiting with you' to his opponents. Here the same word is addressed to the believers.\n\nPatience in this surah is never passivity. It is the behaviour of someone who knows a judgement is coming and does not need to issue his own.",
  "88": "'The arrogant chiefs of his people threatened, \"O Shu'aib! We will certainly expel you and your fellow believers from our land, unless you return to our faith.\" He replied, \"Even if we hate it?\"' (Quran 7:88). The chiefs respond, and their method is familiar.\n\n'The arrogant chiefs' — al-mala'u alladhina istakbaru. The same phrase used of Thamud's leaders at 7:75.\n\n'We will certainly expel you' — la nukhrijannaka. Expulsion, exactly as Lut's people proposed at 7:82.\n\nTwo nations, two different sins, one tactic. When the argument cannot be won, remove the person making it.\n\n'Unless you return to our faith' — aw lata'udunna fi millatina. Convert or leave.\n\nNote the word 'return'. They assume he was one of them and defected, which reveals how they understand religion: as membership in a community rather than a matter of truth.\n\n'Even if we hate it?' — a wa law kunna karihin. Shu'aib's reply is a question, and it exposes the emptiness of what they are demanding.\n\nThey are asking for outward conformity from people who are known to find the thing repellent. What exactly would that be worth?\n\nSurah Hud records more of their pressure at 11:91: 'we see you powerless among us. Were it not for your clan, we would have certainly stoned you.'\n\nSo the threat was credible, and Shu'aib knew it. His answer is still a question about principle rather than a negotiation about safety.\n\nEverything he says next builds on this one word: 'karihin' — hating it.",
  "89": "'We would surely be fabricating a lie against Allah if we were to return to your faith after Allah has saved us from it... In Allah we trust. Our Lord! Judge between us and our people with truth. You are the best of those who judge.' (Quran 7:89). The full answer to the ultimatum.\n\n'We would surely be fabricating a lie against Allah' — qadi iftarayna 'ala Allahi kadhiban. Going back would not merely be weakness; it would be a false statement about Allah.\n\n'Iftira'' is the charge al-An'am laid repeatedly, at 6:21, 6:93 and 6:144. And 7:33 listed it: 'attributing to Allah what you do not know.'\n\n'After Allah has saved us from it' — ba'da idh najjana Allahu minha. He describes his own past religion as something he was rescued from.\n\n'Unless it is the Will of Allah, our Lord' — illa an yasha'a Allahu rabbuna. Even in refusing absolutely, he does not claim independence from the divine will. The exception costs him nothing and keeps the statement honest.\n\n'Our Lord has encompassed everything in His knowledge' — wasi'a rabbuna kulla shay'in 'ilma.\n\n'In Allah we trust' — 'ala Allahi tawakkalna. Trust, at the exact moment of maximum exposure — facing expulsion, with stoning openly discussed.\n\nSurah at-Talaq attaches a promise to that posture at 65:3: 'whoever puts their trust in Allah, then He alone is sufficient for them.'\n\n'Judge between us and our people with truth' — and note 'our people'. Threatened with deportation by them, he still calls them his own, exactly as at 7:61, 7:67 and 7:79.\n\nThe prayer asks for a verdict, not for revenge.",
  "90": "'The disbelieving chiefs of his people threatened, \"If you follow Shu'aib, you will surely be losers!\"' (Quran 7:90). The chiefs change tactics.\n\nThe threat of 7:88 was aimed at Shu'aib. This one is aimed at everyone else.\n\n'If you follow Shu'aib' — la ini ittaba'tum Shu'ayban. The verb 'follow' is the surah's own word, from the command at 7:3: 'Follow what has been sent down to you from your Lord.'\n\n'You will surely be losers' — innakum idhan la khasirun. 'Khasirun' — the word from the scales at 7:9, from Adam's plea at 7:23, and from 7:53.\n\nThey have taken the Quran's own category and applied it to the believers. Loss, in their usage, means loss of trade — Midian was a merchant town, and following a man who insists on full measure has an obvious cost.\n\nThat is the honest core of their warning. Fair scales are expensive.\n\nHakim ibn Hizam reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying of buyer and seller: 'if they speak the truth and mention the defects, then their bargain will be blessed, and if they tell lies and conceal the defects, they might gain some financial gain but they will deprive their sale of Allah's blessings' (Sahih al-Bukhari 2114).\n\nGain and blessing are separated there, exactly as this verse separates them.\n\nRecall 7:75, where Thamud's chiefs pressured the weak believers with a question. Here Midian's chiefs pressure them with a forecast.\n\nThe next two verses settle which side the word 'losers' belongs to.",
  "91": "'Then an ˹overwhelming˺ earthquake struck them and they fell lifeless in their homes.' (Quran 7:91). Word for word, the fate of Thamud at 7:78.\n\n'Al-rajfah' — the same shaking. 'Jathimin' — the same collapsed stillness. 'In their homes' — the same place.\n\nThe surah repeats the sentence exactly, and the repetition is the argument. Two nations with nothing in common — one carved palaces from rock, the other ran caravans and cheated on scales — receive the identical line.\n\nTheir sins were different. Thamud killed a sign; Midian shaved weights. The outcome is printed the same way.\n\nThat is a warning against ranking sins by how respectable they look. Fraud in a marketplace does not sound like the crime of a doomed civilisation. Here it is filed under the same verdict.\n\nSurah Hud describes their end as 'the mighty blast' at 11:94, and Surah ash-Shu'ara mentions 'the day of the deadly cloud' at 26:189 — different aspects of one event, reported for different audiences.\n\n'In their homes' — again the place of safety. Verse 7:4 set this theme at the start: destruction arriving 'while sleeping at night or midday.'\n\nThe chiefs of 7:90 warned the believers that following Shu'aib would ruin them. Within one verse, the warners are gone and the followers are not.\n\nAnd the surah does not pause to point that out. It simply places the two verses next to each other.",
  "92": "'Those who rejected Shu'aib were ˹wiped out˺ as if they had never lived there. Those who rejected Shu'aib were the true losers.' (Quran 7:92). The reversal, stated twice.\n\n'As if they had never lived there' — ka an lam yaghnaw fiha. As though they had never prospered in it, never been rich there.\n\nThe verb 'ghaniya' carries the sense of dwelling in ease. Midian was a wealthy trading town. That wealth is described as leaving no mark at all.\n\n'Those who rejected Shu'aib were the true losers' — alladhina kadhdhabu Shu'ayban kanu humu al-khasirin.\n\nRead that against 7:90. The chiefs said: 'If you follow Shu'aib, you will surely be losers.' The verse answers with their own word, aimed the other way, and adds 'humu' — they, specifically, are the ones.\n\nNothing else is added. No commentary, no moral drawn. The sentence simply returns the label to its owners.\n\nNotice that the name 'Shu'aib' appears twice in one short verse. The surah is deliberate about this: the man they threatened to expel is the fixed point around which the outcome is described.\n\nRecall 7:82, where Lut's people wanted the believers expelled from 'your land'. In both stories the land in question outlives no one who claimed it.\n\nAl-An'am put the pattern at 6:6: 'We had made them more established in the land than you... Then We destroyed them for their sins.'\n\nProsperity is not evidence of approval. That is the point 7:95 is about to make explicitly.",
  "93": "'He turned away from them, saying, \"O my people! Indeed, I have delivered to you the messages of my Lord and gave you ˹sincere˺ advice. How can I then grieve for those who chose to disbelieve?\"' (Quran 7:93). Shu'aib's closing words, matching Salih's at 7:79.\n\n'He turned away from them' — fa tawalla 'anhum. The same verb, the same moment: a prophet standing in an emptied town.\n\n'O my people' — for the last time.\n\n'I have delivered to you the messages of my Lord and gave you sincere advice' — ablaghtukum risalati rabbi wa nasahtu lakum. Word for word what Salih said at 7:79.\n\nTwo prophets, two nations, one closing statement. The account is settled the same way: delivery made, counsel given.\n\nThen the difference. Salih ended with a diagnosis of them — 'you do not like sincere advisors.' Shu'aib ends with a question about himself.\n\n'How can I then grieve for those who chose to disbelieve?' — fa kayfa asa 'ala qawmin kafirin. How should I mourn?\n\nThe question is not cold. It comes from a man who has just used the words 'my people' over a graveyard, and who prayed at 7:89 for a judgement 'with truth' rather than for their destruction.\n\nWhat it settles is the limit of a caller's responsibility. He was accountable for the delivering and the advising. He was never accountable for the outcome.\n\nThat is the same limit al-An'am placed on the Prophet himself at 6:107: 'you are not their keeper.'\n\nAnyone who carries a message and watches it refused should learn this sentence.",
  "94": "'Whenever We sent a prophet to a society, We afflicted its ˹disbelieving˺ people with suffering and adversity, so perhaps they would be humbled.' (Quran 7:94). The histories pause, and the method behind them is explained.\n\nFive nations have now been described. This verse steps back and states the rule they all followed.\n\n'Whenever We sent a prophet to a society' — wa ma arsalna fi qaryatin min nabiyyin. The pattern is universal, not occasional.\n\n'We afflicted its people with suffering and adversity' — akhadhna ahlaha bil-ba'sa'i wal-darra'. 'Ba'sa'' is hardship in outward circumstances — poverty, loss, defeat. 'Darra'' is affliction in the body — illness, hunger.\n\n'So perhaps they would be humbled' — la'allahum yadarra'un. From the same root as 'tadarru'' in 7:55: 'Call upon your Lord humbly.'\n\nSo the purpose of the hardship is named openly, and it is not punishment. It is to bring people to the posture the surah has already commanded.\n\nAl-An'am states the identical rule at 6:42, in almost identical words, and then asks the sharp question at 6:43: 'Why did they not humble themselves when We made them suffer?'\n\nThere is something merciful in this verse that is easy to miss. Difficulty arriving alongside a message is not evidence of abandonment. It is part of the delivery.\n\nSuhayb reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'Strange are the ways of a believer for there is good in every affair of his... if he gets into trouble and shows resignation, there is a good for him in it' (Sahih Muslim 2999).\n\nHardship is a question being asked. The next verse describes what happens when the answer is no.",
  "95": "'Then We changed their adversity to prosperity until they flourished and argued ˹falsely˺, \"Our forefathers ˹too˺ had been visited by adversity and prosperity.\" So We seized them by surprise, while they were unaware.' (Quran 7:95). The second method, and it is more dangerous than the first.\n\n'Then We changed their adversity to prosperity' — thumma baddalna makana al-sayyi'ati al-hasanah. The hardship of 7:94 is replaced with ease.\n\n'Until they flourished' — hatta 'afaw. They increased, multiplied, grew comfortable.\n\nAnd then the reasoning that ruins them: 'Our forefathers too had been visited by adversity and prosperity.'\n\nLook closely at what they are saying. Good times and bad times happen; they happened to our fathers; they are simply how life goes. There is no message in any of it.\n\nThat is a modern-sounding argument, and the Quran records it from an ancient people. Every signal has been reinterpreted as weather.\n\nNote also that it is a third appeal to the forefathers, after 7:28 and 7:70. Here the ancestors are used not to justify a practice but to dismiss a warning.\n\n'So We seized them by surprise, while they were unaware' — fa akhadhnahum baghtatan wa hum la yash'urun. Al-An'am describes exactly this at 6:44: 'We showered them with everything they desired. But just as they became prideful of what they were given, We seized them by surprise.'\n\nThat is the mechanism the scholars call 'istidraj' — being drawn in gradually by ease.\n\nHardship at least asks a question. Comfort does not, and comfort is therefore the harder test to pass.",
  "96": "'Had the people of those societies been faithful and mindful ˹of Allah˺, We would have overwhelmed them with blessings from heaven and earth. But they disbelieved, so We seized them for what they used to commit.' (Quran 7:96). The alternative history.\n\n'Had the people of those societies been faithful and mindful' — wa law anna ahla al-qura amanu wattaqaw. Two conditions: belief, and the mindfulness that keeps a person inside the limits.\n\n'We would have overwhelmed them with blessings from heaven and earth' — la fatahna 'alayhim barakatin mina al-sama'i wal-ard. 'Fatahna' — We would have opened.\n\nThat verb is worth noticing. At 7:40 the gates of heaven were shut for the arrogant. Here the same sky opens.\n\n'From heaven and earth' — rain from above, growth from below. The two halves of the argument made at 7:57 and 7:58.\n\nSo the surah is offering a counterfactual for five destroyed nations. The outcome was not fixed. Belief would have produced the opposite result, in this world, in the same places.\n\nThat matters for how the histories are read. These were not stories of people created to be destroyed. Every one of them had an open door, described here in one line.\n\n'But they disbelieved, so We seized them for what they used to commit' — bima kanu yaksibun. What they earned — the vocabulary of the scale at 7:8.\n\nNotice what is not promised: wealth in exchange for religion. 'Barakah' is blessing — the quality that makes a small thing sufficient — not accumulation.\n\nMidian had accumulation. It did not have this.",
  "97": "'Did the people of those societies feel secure that Our punishment would not come upon them by night while they were asleep?' (Quran 7:97). A question aimed at everyone still living.\n\n'Did they feel secure' — a fa amina. From 'amn' — safety. The question is about a feeling, not a fact.\n\n'By night while they were asleep' — bayatan wa hum na'imun. This is the exact scenario of 7:4, where nations were destroyed 'while sleeping at night or midday.'\n\nThe surah opened with that image and returns to it now, ninety-three verses later, after five demonstrations that it is not hypothetical.\n\nConsider what is being asked. Not 'did they know', but 'did they feel safe'. The nations described did feel safe. That feeling is what the verse identifies as the error.\n\nThamud carved houses out of a mountain, and 7:78 records them dead inside them. Midian was rich, and 7:92 says it was as if they had never lived there.\n\nSecurity is exactly the state in which these things arrived.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this passage, reads the two questions of 7:97 and 7:98 as addressing the sense of safety from Allah's plan — a sense the surah treats as itself dangerous.\n\nThe verse does not tell anyone to live in fear. Verse 7:56 already gave the balance: 'call upon Him with hope and fear.'\n\nWhat it removes is the third option — the assumption that the matter is settled and requires no thought at all.",
  "98": "'Or did they feel secure that Our punishment would not come upon them by day while they were at play?' (Quran 7:98). The second half of the question.\n\nVerse 7:97 covered the night. This covers the day, and between them nothing is left out.\n\n'While they were at play' — wa hum yal'abun. Playing.\n\nThat word has appeared before in this surah. At 7:51 the people of the Fire were described as 'those who took this faith as mere amusement and play' — la'iban.\n\nThe same root, used twice, and the connection is deliberate. Play as an attitude towards religion at 7:51; play as the activity interrupted at 7:98.\n\nThe verse is not condemning recreation. What it describes is a whole society occupied with its diversions, which is what 7:95 said prosperity produces.\n\nSet the two questions side by side. Asleep, or playing. Neither state involves any wrongdoing at that moment.\n\nThat is the sharpest part of the image. The punishments in these histories did not arrive while anyone was committing the sin. They arrived during rest and during leisure — the ordinary hours, the ones nobody thinks about.\n\n'A fa amina' — did they feel secure. The question is repeated word for word, which in Arabic makes it heavier, not lighter.\n\nTwo verses, two times of day, one point: the arrival is not scheduled around anyone's awareness of it.\n\nAnd the next verse names the feeling itself and calls it what it is.",
  "99": "'Did they feel secure against Allah's planning? None would feel secure from Allah's planning except the losers.' (Quran 7:99). The feeling of 7:97 and 7:98 is now named and judged.\n\n'Allah's planning' — makr Allah. 'Makr' in Arabic means a hidden arrangement, a plan that works unseen. Used of human beings it means scheming; used of Allah it means His response to their scheming, which they cannot detect.\n\nThe Quran uses it precisely that way. Surah Al 'Imran says at 3:54: 'And the disbelievers made a plan, but Allah also planned — and Allah is the best of planners.'\n\nSurah al-Anfal records the same at 8:30, about the night the Prophet's enemies plotted against him.\n\nSo the word arrives as a counter-move. They had a plan; there was another one running.\n\n'Did they feel secure' — a fa aminu. The third time in three verses.\n\n'None would feel secure from Allah's planning except the losers' — illa al-qawmu al-khasirun. And there is the verdict.\n\nNotice how strong that is. Feeling safe is not described as a mistake or an oversight. It is made the identifying mark of the ruined.\n\nSet this against the other extreme, which the Quran forbids just as firmly. Surah Yusuf at 12:87: 'do not lose hope in the mercy of Allah, for no one loses hope in Allah's mercy except those with no faith.'\n\nTwo states are declared off limits: certainty of safety, and despair of mercy. Verse 7:56 already gave the position between them — 'call upon Him with hope and fear.'\n\n'Istidraj' — the drawing-in of 7:95 — works only on people who feel secure.",
  "100": "'Is it not clear to those who take over the land after ˹the destruction of˺ its former residents that—if We will—We can punish them ˹too˺ for their sins and seal their hearts so they will not hear ˹the truth˺?' (Quran 7:100). A question for the inheritors.\n\n'Those who take over the land after its former residents' — alladhina yarithuna al-arda min ba'di ahliha. The heirs.\n\nEvery nation in this surah was an heir. 'Ad inherited from Nuh's people (Quran 7:69); Thamud inherited from 'Ad (Quran 7:74). Each was reminded of it, and each repeated the fault of the people it replaced.\n\n'Is it not clear' — a wa lam yahdi lahum. Has it not become plain to them? The evidence is the empty land they now occupy.\n\n'That if We will, We can punish them too for their sins' — the succession does not come with immunity. Occupying the house of a destroyed people proves the house can be emptied.\n\n'And seal their hearts so they will not hear' — wa natba'u 'ala qulubihim fa hum la yasma'un. The second consequence, and it is worse than the first.\n\n'Tab'' is stamping or sealing, as a seal is pressed into wax. Al-Baqarah uses it at 2:7 and al-An'am describes the same condition at 6:25: 'We have cast veils over their hearts — leaving them unable to comprehend it.'\n\nNotice the order: the sins come first, then the seal. Al-An'am states the sequence plainly at 6:110: 'We turn their hearts and eyes away from the truth as they refused to believe at first.'\n\nThe sealing is not arbitrary. It is a refusal that has been made permanent by repetition.\n\nHearing is the faculty named because it is the one this surah depends on. A sealed heart can read every history in this chapter and take nothing from it.",
  "101": "'We have narrated to you ˹O Prophet˺ some of the stories of those societies. Surely, their messengers came to them with clear proofs, but still they would not believe in what they had already denied. This is how Allah seals the hearts of the disbelievers.' (Quran 7:101). The five histories are closed and summed up.\n\n'Some of the stories' — min anba'iha. Some, not all. The five were a selection.\n\n'Their messengers came to them with clear proofs' — bil-bayyinat. Clear evidence. Salih brought a she-camel; each of the others brought a message that could be checked against their own history.\n\n'But still they would not believe in what they had already denied' — fa ma kanu li yu'minu bima kadhdhabu min qablu.\n\nThat clause repays slow reading. They had denied it already, and after that they could not believe it. The earlier refusal fixed the later position.\n\nThis is the mechanism behind the sealing of 7:100. A person who has publicly rejected something has an investment in it being false. Every further proof now threatens him rather than helps him.\n\n'This is how Allah seals the hearts of the disbelievers' — kadhalika yatba'u Allahu 'ala qulubi al-kafirin. 'Kadhalika' — in this way, by this process.\n\nThe verse describes a door that closes from the inside and is then locked from outside.\n\nAl-An'am gives the same account at 6:110 and adds the timing: 'as they refused to believe at first.'\n\nThe practical urgency is obvious. A conviction is cheapest to change before it has been announced.\n\n'To you, O Prophet' — the histories were told to a man being rejected in Mecca by chiefs of exactly the type described in every one of them.",
  "102": "'We did not find most of them true to their covenant. Rather, We found most of them truly rebellious.' (Quran 7:102). The final verdict on those nations, and it introduces a word the surah will build on.\n\n'True to their covenant' — min 'ahd. A binding agreement.\n\nWhich covenant? The surah answers this seventy verses later, at 7:172, where all the descendants of Adam are brought forth and asked 'Am I not your Lord?' and reply 'Yes, You are! We testify.'\n\nSo the agreement predates every nation described. It was not a treaty any of them signed in history; it is the acknowledgement built into being a human creature.\n\n'Most of them' — aktharahum. Most, twice in one verse, which means not all. In each of the five histories a small group believed and was saved.\n\nThe Quran is careful with this word throughout the surah: 'most of them are unaware' at 7:187, 'most of them ungrateful' at 7:17.\n\n'Rebellious' — fasiqin. From 'fisq' — stepping outside, breaking away from obedience.\n\nNote what the verdict is not. They are not called stupid, or unlucky, or badly taught. They are described as having broken an agreement they were party to.\n\nThat framing changes what the histories mean. These were not people who failed to find the truth. They were people who had it, at the deepest level, and did not keep to it.\n\nAnd with that, the surah closes the ancient files and turns to the longest history of all.",
  "103": "'Then after them We sent Moses with Our signs to Pharaoh and his chiefs, but they wrongfully rejected them. See what was the end of the corruptors!' (Quran 7:103). The sixth history begins, and it will run for more than sixty verses.\n\n'Then after them' — thumma ba'athna min ba'dihim. After the five.\n\n'We sent Moses with Our signs' — bi ayatina. Signs, plural, and Musa will produce them one after another. This is the first mission in the surah supported by a sustained series of miracles.\n\n'To Pharaoh and his chiefs' — ila Fir'awna wa mala'ih. There is the word again: 'al-mala''. The same class that opposed Nuh at 7:60, Hud at 7:66, Salih at 7:75 and Shu'aib at 7:88.\n\nBut something has changed. In the earlier stories the chiefs are anonymous. Here they have a head, and he has a name.\n\n'They wrongfully rejected them' — fa zalamu biha. They wronged by them — the injustice is done through their treatment of the signs themselves.\n\n'See what was the end of the corruptors' — fanzur kayfa kana 'aqibatu al-mufsidin. The imperative from 7:84, addressed to the reader again.\n\nNotice that the outcome is announced in the opening verse. The surah tells you how it ends before it begins, which removes suspense and leaves attention where it belongs: on the arguments.\n\n'Mufsidin' — corruptors, from 'fasad'. The word of 7:56 and 7:74 and 7:86. Pharaoh's Egypt is filed under the same heading as Midian's crooked scales.",
  "104": "'And Moses said, \"O Pharaoh! I am truly a messenger from the Lord of all worlds,\"' (Quran 7:104). The opening line, and it is the same one the others used.\n\n'I am truly a messenger from the Lord of all worlds' — inni rasulun min rabbi al-'alamin. Word for word what Nuh said at 7:61 and Hud at 7:67.\n\nBoth of them said it in reply to an insult. Musa says it first, before anything has been said to him.\n\nThat difference marks the man he is addressing. Pharaoh claimed divinity — Surah an-Nazi'at records his words at 79:24: 'I am your lord, the most high!' — and Surah al-Qasas has him telling his chiefs at 28:38: 'I know of no other god for you but myself.'\n\nSo 'the Lord of all worlds' is not a general title here. It is a direct contradiction of the man in front of him, delivered in his own court.\n\nSurah ash-Shu'ara records Pharaoh's response to exactly this phrase at 26:23: 'And what is \"the Lord of all worlds\"?'\n\n'O Pharaoh' — ya Fir'awn. Notice what is absent from the address: no titles, no flattery, and also no abuse.\n\nSurah Ta-Ha preserves the instruction Musa was given at 20:44: 'Speak to him gently, so perhaps he may be mindful or fearful.'\n\nThat command is one of the most striking in the Quran. The most tyrannical ruler in these narratives was to be addressed gently, because the purpose was still his benefit.\n\nThe messenger's tone belongs to the mission, not to the recipient's behaviour.",
  "105": "'obliged to say nothing about Allah except the truth. Indeed, I have come to you with clear proof from your Lord, so let the children of Israel go with me.' (Quran 7:105). Three clauses: a constraint, a credential, and a demand.\n\n'Obliged to say nothing about Allah except the truth' — haqiqun 'ala an la aqula 'ala Allahi illa al-haqq. It is a duty upon me.\n\nMusa binds himself before making any claim. That is the discipline 7:33 required — not 'attributing to Allah what you do not know' — and it is stated to a king who says whatever suits him.\n\n'I have come to you with clear proof from your Lord' — bi bayyinatin min rabbikum. And there is the sharpest word in the sentence: 'your Lord'.\n\nPharaoh has just been told he has one. In front of his court.\n\n'So let the children of Israel go with me' — fa arsil ma'iya bani Isra'il. Release them.\n\nThis is new in the surah. Nuh, Hud, Salih and Shu'aib asked for worship and honest dealing. Musa asks for that and adds a political demand: free the enslaved population.\n\nThe two are not separate errands. A man who claims to be lord of the people will naturally own them, and ending the claim means ending the ownership.\n\nNote the order of the three clauses. First the standard he holds himself to, then the evidence, then the request. Nothing is asked before the ground is laid.\n\nAnd nothing personal is requested. Musa asks for the freedom of others, and for himself nothing at all — the same absence of self-interest 7:62 and 7:68 described as 'nasihah'.",
  "106": "'Pharaoh said, \"If you have come with a sign, then bring it if what you say is true.\"' (Quran 7:106). The demand for evidence.\n\n'Then bring it' — fa'ti biha. Produce it.\n\nReaders of this surah have met this sentence twice already. 'Ad said at 7:70: 'Then bring us what you threaten us with, if what you say is true.' Thamud said at 7:77: 'Bring us what you threaten us with, if you are truly one of the messengers.'\n\nThe wording is nearly identical, and the difference matters. Those two nations demanded the punishment. Pharaoh demands the proof.\n\nHis request is, on its face, entirely reasonable. A man walks into court claiming to represent the Lord of all worlds; asking for credentials is fair.\n\nThat is what makes this history different from the five before it. Pharaoh's rejection cannot be explained by lack of evidence, because he asks for evidence and receives it immediately, twice, in the next two verses.\n\n'If what you say is true' — in kunta mina al-sadiqin. The same closing clause used by 'Ad at 7:70.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this exchange, notes that the demand exposes Pharaoh's position: he has framed the matter as a test he intends to judge.\n\nAnd that is the flaw hidden inside a reasonable request. He is not asking in order to find out. He is asking in order to preside.\n\nThe signs arrive anyway.",
  "107": "'So Moses threw down his staff and—behold!—it became a real snake.' (Quran 7:107). The first sign.\n\n'His staff' — 'asah. An ordinary shepherd's stick. Surah Ta-Ha records Allah asking him about it at 20:17: 'And what is that in your right hand, O Moses?'\n\n'It became a real snake' — thu'banun mubin. A clear, evident serpent. The Quran uses 'mubin' — the word for something obvious beyond dispute.\n\nThat single adjective does the work of an argument. This was not a trick performed at a distance in poor light. It was plain.\n\nSurah Ta-Ha describes the movement at 20:20: 'behold! — it became a serpent, slithering.'\n\nNotice how brief the verse is. Ten words for the most dramatic moment so far in the surah.\n\nThe Quran consistently refuses to dwell on miracles. Compare 7:64, where the flood took half a line, and 7:78, where the destruction of Thamud took eight words.\n\nThere is a reason for that restraint. Verse 7:106 shows a man who wants a spectacle to judge; the surah will not turn the sign into one.\n\nRecall also 6:109, where the deniers swore that a sign would convince them, and 6:111 answered that even angels arriving would not.\n\nThe staff is a real object, in a real hand, and it does something impossible. What happens next is not a debate about whether it happened.\n\nIt is an argument about how to describe it.",
  "108": "'Then he drew his hand ˹out of his collar˺ and it was ˹shining˺ white for all to see.' (Quran 7:108). The second sign, and it is the opposite kind.\n\n'He drew his hand out' — naza'a yadah. From inside his garment.\n\n'It was shining white for all to see' — bayda'u lil-nazirin. White, for the onlookers. Surah an-Naml adds the detail at 27:12: 'it will come out shining white, unblemished' — unblemished, so nobody could call it a disease.\n\nTwo signs, and they are deliberately unalike. The staff became something dangerous; the hand becomes something luminous. One outside the body, one from within it.\n\nSurah an-Naml also counts them at 27:12 as two of nine signs given to Pharaoh and his people. This is a mission equipped with more evidence than any other in the surah.\n\n'Lil-nazirin' — for those looking. The verse specifies an audience. Pharaoh's court is full of people, and everything happens in front of them.\n\nThat detail becomes important immediately. The response in 7:109 does not come from Pharaoh; it comes from the chiefs who watched.\n\nNote that Musa produces both signs without a word of commentary. He does not explain, boast, or press an advantage.\n\nHe was asked for a sign at 7:106. He gives two, and stops.\n\nEverything after this point in the story is about what people do with evidence they cannot deny, which is the question this surah has been asking since 7:9.",
  "109": "'The chiefs of Pharaoh's people said, \"He is indeed a skilled magician,\"' (Quran 7:109). The verdict, and notice who delivers it.\n\n'The chiefs' — al-mala'. The same class, for the fifth time in this surah.\n\nPharaoh asked the question at 7:106. Pharaoh watched both signs. And it is his chiefs who speak.\n\nSurah ash-Shu'ara records the same words from Pharaoh's own mouth at 26:34, which the commentators reconcile easily: he said it, and his court repeated it, or they said it and he adopted it. Either way the verdict is a collective product.\n\n'A skilled magician' — sahirun 'alim. A knowledgeable sorcerer.\n\nLook at what this explanation concedes. Nobody disputes that the staff became a serpent or that the hand shone. The events are accepted as facts.\n\nWhat is disputed is the category. They agree something impossible happened and file it under a heading that keeps their world intact.\n\nThat is the most sophisticated rejection in the surah, and the most instructive. Denial does not require denying what you saw.\n\nAl-An'am described the same manoeuvre at 6:7: 'Had We sent down to you O Prophet a revelation in writing... the disbelievers would still have said, \"This is nothing but pure magic.\"'\n\nVerse 7:101 has already explained why: 'they would not believe in what they had already denied.' The position came first; the explanation was fitted to it.",
  "110": "'\"who seeks to drive you from your land.\" ˹So Pharaoh asked,˺ \"What do you propose?\"' (Quran 7:110). The accusation turns political in one line.\n\n'Who seeks to drive you from your land' — yuridu an yukhrijakum min ardikum. Expulsion.\n\nThat word again. Lut's people said 'Expel them from your land' at 7:82. Midian's chiefs said 'We will certainly expel you' at 7:88.\n\nHere the same fear is used as propaganda: he will do to us what we would do to him.\n\nNotice how quickly the frame shifts. Verse 7:109 called Musa a magician — a claim about the signs. This half-verse says nothing about the signs at all. It is about territory.\n\nThat is the move to watch. When the evidence cannot be beaten, the argument is changed into a question of loyalty and threat.\n\nAnd it works, because everyone in the room owns land in Egypt. A theological dispute concerns few people; a warning that outsiders will take your property concerns all of them.\n\nRecall the real demand Musa made at 7:105: 'let the children of Israel go with me.' He asked to leave, taking the enslaved with him.\n\nThe accusation is therefore the exact inversion of the request. The man asking permission to depart is described as an invader.\n\n'What do you propose?' — madha ta'murun. Pharaoh, who told his court at 28:38 that he knew of no god but himself, asks them for advice.\n\nSurah ash-Shu'ara has the same question at 26:35. The self-declared lord needs a committee.",
  "111": "'They replied, \"Let him and his brother wait and send mobilizers to all cities\"' (Quran 7:111). The plan, part one.\n\n'Let him and his brother wait' — arjih wa akhah. Postpone them, hold them off.\n\nThe first instinct is delay. Not arrest, not execution — a pause.\n\nThat is worth noticing in a court with unlimited power. They have just watched two undeniable signs, and their instinct is to buy time, because they need to organise a response and know they do not have one yet.\n\n'And his brother' — Harun. He appears here for the first time in this surah, mentioned in passing as part of the problem.\n\n'Send mobilizers to all cities' — wa arsil fi al-mada'ini hashirin. Gatherers, people who round others up.\n\nThe scale of that instruction is the real information. Not a local expert — every city in Egypt.\n\nThey are treating this as a national emergency, which tells you exactly how convincing the signs were. Nobody mounts a country-wide search to answer a fraud they consider obvious.\n\nSo their private assessment contradicts their public verdict of 7:109. If Musa were merely 'a skilled magician', an equally skilled magician would be easy to find.\n\nThis is the pattern of 7:101 in action: the denial was fixed first, and now enormous effort is spent making it hold.\n\nNote also the shape of the plan. It is a public relations exercise. The problem is not that Musa might be right; it is that the crowd was watching, as 7:108 specified.",
  "112": "'to bring you every clever magician.' (Quran 7:112). The plan, part two, and the sentence that decides the outcome.\n\n'Every clever magician' — bi kulli sahirin 'alim. The same two words used of Musa at 7:109.\n\nThat repetition is the argument. They labelled him a skilled magician; now they will fetch skilled magicians. By their own reasoning the matter should be settled easily.\n\n'Every' — kulli. All of them. The best in Egypt, a nation whose priestly craft was famous throughout the ancient world.\n\nConsider what Pharaoh has just done. He has arranged a public test, chosen the arena, selected the judges, recruited the champions, and set the terms.\n\nAnd he has done it in front of the whole country, which is the detail that will destroy him. Verse 7:113 onwards shows the assembly he called turning into the largest audience any prophet in this surah addresses.\n\nThere is a principle here that the Quran states elsewhere and this narrative demonstrates. Surah Al 'Imran at 3:54: 'the disbelievers made a plan, but Allah also planned — and Allah is the best of planners.'\n\nThat is 'makr', the word of 7:99. Pharaoh's plan is running; another plan is running through it.\n\nThe magicians are being gathered to defeat Musa. They will be the first people in Egypt to believe him, and they will say so in public, at the event Pharaoh organised.\n\nNothing about that outcome required a single extra miracle. It required only that the experts be honest about what they were looking at.",
  "113": "'The magicians came to Pharaoh, saying, \"Shall we receive a ˹suitable˺ reward if we prevail?\"' (Quran 7:113). The experts arrive, and the first thing they discuss is payment.\n\n'Shall we receive a suitable reward' — a inna lana la ajran. Is there a fee in this?\n\nThat question tells you everything about where these men are standing at the start of the story. They have been summoned across a country to face a man claiming to speak for the Lord of all worlds, and their opening concern is the contract.\n\nSurah ash-Shu'ara records the same negotiation at 26:41.\n\n'If we prevail' — in kunna nahnu al-ghalibin. If we are the winners. They assume a contest with two sides and one prize.\n\nNotice how ordinary this is. They are professionals being hired for a job, and they behave exactly as professionals do.\n\nSet that beside every prophet in this surah. Nuh, Hud, Salih and Shu'aib all delivered messages and asked for nothing — that was the meaning of 'nasihah' at 7:62 and 7:68.\n\nThe difference between the two positions is money, and the surah has put it in the opening line of the episode on purpose.\n\nRemember this verse, because these same men will be threatened with mutilation in eleven verses and will not move. The distance they travel between 7:113 and 7:126 is the whole point of the story.\n\nNobody in this surah changes as completely as they do.",
  "114": "'He replied, \"Yes, and you will certainly be among those closest to me.\"' (Quran 7:114). The offer, improved.\n\n'Yes' — na'am. The fee is agreed without discussion, which is what a ruler does when the price is irrelevant to him.\n\n'And you will certainly be among those closest to me' — wa innakum la mina al-muqarrabin. Access to the throne.\n\nThat is the larger payment, and Pharaoh knows it. Money can be earned elsewhere; proximity to power cannot.\n\nSurah ash-Shu'ara records the identical exchange at 26:42.\n\nConsider what has been assembled here. A ruler who claims divinity, a court that manufactures verdicts, experts recruited nationally, and now a promise of status.\n\nEverything in the room is arranged around one outcome, and the arrangement is public.\n\nNote also what this promise reveals about Pharaoh's understanding of people. He assumes that men who asked about a fee can be bought with something better than a fee, and on the evidence of 7:113 he is right.\n\nWhat he cannot price is the possibility that they will see something at the event that changes what they want.\n\nRecall 7:99: 'None would feel secure from Allah's planning except the losers.' Pharaoh has just secured his outcome, in his own view completely.\n\nThe word 'muqarrabin' — those brought near — will not be heard again in this story. What the magicians end up asking for at 7:126 is something else entirely.",
  "115": "'They asked, \"O Moses! Will you cast, or shall we be the first to cast?\"' (Quran 7:115). The contest opens with a courtesy.\n\n'Will you cast, or shall we be the first' — imma an tulqiya wa imma an nakuna nahnu al-mulqin.\n\nThey offer him the choice of order. Whatever else these men are, they are not thugs; they observe the manners of their profession.\n\nAnd the offer is not innocent. Going first is an advantage in a display contest: the first performance sets the standard the second must beat, and the crowd's expectations are shaped by it.\n\n'O Moses' — ya Musa. They address him by name, without insult.\n\nCompare the words thrown at the earlier prophets: 'clearly misguided' at 7:60, 'a fool' and 'a liar' at 7:66, 'we surely reject' at 7:76.\n\nThe magicians say none of that. They came to defeat him, and they speak to him with respect.\n\nThat is not a small detail. It is the first sign in the episode that these men are capable of dealing honestly with what is in front of them.\n\nSurah Ta-Ha records Musa's reply at 20:66: 'No, you go first.'\n\nNotice the position he is in. He is one man, in the capital of the empire that enslaved his people, facing every magician in Egypt, and he lets them choose their advantage.\n\nThat confidence has a source, and it is not in himself. It was stated at 7:104: he is a messenger from the Lord of all worlds.",
  "116": "'Moses said, \"You first.\" So when they did, they deceived the eyes of the people, stunned them, and made a great display of magic.' (Quran 7:116). The performance.\n\n'You first' — alqu. One word.\n\n'They deceived the eyes of the people' — saharu a'yuna al-nas. They bewitched the eyes.\n\nThat phrase is the Quran's verdict on what magic is, and it is precise. The target is the eyes of the audience, not the ropes.\n\nSurah Ta-Ha describes the effect at 20:66: 'their ropes and staffs appeared to him — by their magic — to be slithering.' Appeared.\n\nNothing changed. The perception changed.\n\nSet that against 7:107, where the staff 'became a real snake' — 'thu'banun mubin', with 'mubin' insisting on the reality.\n\nThe surah is drawing a line between two things that look similar to a spectator: an event, and an impression.\n\n'Stunned them' — istarhabuhum. They terrified them.\n\n'And made a great display of magic' — wa ja'u bi sihrin 'azim. The Quran does not minimise it. It was a great display, by the best practitioners in the world.\n\nSurah Ta-Ha even records at 20:67 that 'Moses concealed fear within himself.' The Quran preserves the prophet's human reaction rather than tidying it away.\n\nEverything about this moment favours Pharaoh: the venue, the crowd, the fear, and a spectacle that worked.",
  "117": "'Then We inspired Moses, \"Throw down your staff,\" and—behold!—it devoured the objects of their illusion!' (Quran 7:117). One instruction, and the contest ends.\n\n'We inspired Moses' — awhayna ila Musa. The command comes at the moment of maximum pressure, immediately after 7:116 described the fear in the arena.\n\n'Throw down your staff' — an alqi 'asak. The same shepherd's stick from 7:107.\n\n'It devoured' — talqafu. Swallowed them up. The verb suggests speed and completeness.\n\n'The objects of their illusion' — ma ya'fikun. What they were falsifying. Not 'their snakes' — the Quran refuses to grant the ropes the status of the thing they resembled.\n\nSurah Ta-Ha gives the reason in advance at 20:69: 'it will swallow up what they have made, for what they have made is no more than a magic trick.'\n\nNotice what did not happen. Musa did not produce a bigger illusion, or a more impressive show. His staff removed theirs.\n\nThat is a different kind of victory from the one the crowd expected. They came for a contest between two performances and watched one performance eat the other.\n\nAnd the magicians, standing closest, are the only people present who know exactly what their own ropes were.\n\nSurah Yunus records what Musa said as they cast at 10:81: 'What you have produced is mere magic, Allah will surely make it useless.'\n\nThe staff is the same object it always was. The difference between 7:116 and 7:117 is not skill; it is who is behind the hand.",
  "118": "'So the truth prevailed and their illusions failed.' (Quran 7:118). Seven words in Arabic, and the episode is decided.\n\n'So the truth prevailed' — fa waqa'a al-haqq. Literally: the truth fell into place. The verb is used of something landing, becoming established, taking effect.\n\n'And their illusions failed' — wa batala ma kanu ya'malun. What they were doing became void.\n\n'Batil' is the Quran's word for the empty, the null, the thing with nothing inside it. It is the opposite of 'haqq'.\n\nNotice how the sentence is built. The truth arriving and the falsehood collapsing are described as one event, not two.\n\nThat is the pattern this surah has shown from the beginning. The false gods of 7:71 were 'names which you named'. The illusions here were images in the eyes of a crowd. Neither had to be defeated; each had only to be exposed.\n\nSurah Yunus states the principle at 10:82: 'And Allah establishes the truth by His Words — even to the dismay of the wicked.'\n\nNote also the tense of 'ma kanu ya'malun' — what they used to do. Their entire profession, not just this afternoon's performance, is included in the collapse.\n\nFor the magicians this is not a lost competition. It is the end of everything they knew how to do, and the discovery of what it was actually worth.\n\nThat discovery is what the next two verses are about, and it happens faster than anyone in the arena expects.",
  "119": "'So Pharaoh and his people were defeated right there and put to shame.' (Quran 7:119). The plan of 7:111 and 7:112 comes apart in public.\n\n'Defeated right there' — fa ghulibu hunalika. On the spot, in that place.\n\n'Ghulibu' — they were overcome. And the word is worth tracing: the magicians asked at 7:113 whether they would be rewarded 'if we prevail' — 'al-ghalibin'. The same root, now applied to Pharaoh in the passive.\n\n'And put to shame' — wanqalabu saghirin. They turned back, made small.\n\n'Saghirin' — the exact word used of Iblis at 7:13, when he was expelled 'utterly humiliated'. The surah keeps its vocabulary consistent: arrogance ends in smallness.\n\nConsider precisely what has been lost here, because it is not a magic contest.\n\nPharaoh chose the venue, set the date, summoned the participants and guaranteed their reward. He made the event as large and as public as possible, because 7:108 says the signs had been seen 'for all to see' and he needed the correction seen by the same crowd.\n\nEvery element of that decision now works against him. The larger the audience, the larger the defeat.\n\n'Pharaoh and his people' — the loss is collective, exactly as the verdict of 7:109 was collective.\n\nRecall 7:99: 'Did they feel secure against Allah's planning?' This verse is the answer, delivered in front of a nation.\n\nAnd the humiliation is not yet complete. It gets worse in the next verse, from the direction he least expected.",
  "120": "'And the magicians fell down, prostrating.' (Quran 7:120). Five words, and they are the turning point of the entire history.\n\n'Fell down' — ulqiya al-saharatu sajidin. The Arabic is passive: the magicians were cast down in prostration.\n\nThat construction is deliberate and the commentators have long noticed it. The same verb 'alqa' — to throw down — has run through the whole scene: they were told to cast at 7:115, they cast at 7:116, Musa was told to cast at 7:117.\n\nNow they themselves are what gets cast down.\n\n'Prostrating' — sajidin. Faces to the ground, in the arena, in front of Pharaoh and the whole assembled country.\n\nThink about who these people are. They are the experts. They spent their lives producing the effects the crowd had just watched, and they alone knew, with professional certainty, what could and could not be done with rope.\n\nEveryone else in that arena saw something amazing. The magicians saw something impossible.\n\nThat is why they are the first to believe, and it is the argument the surah is making. The people best equipped to detect a fraud detected none.\n\nRecall 7:113, where their first words were about a fee. Eight verses later they are on the ground, and no fee has been paid.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this passage, observes that they had been brought to strengthen Pharaoh's position and became the proof against him.",
  "121": "'They declared, \"We ˹now˺ believe in the Lord of all worlds—\"' (Quran 7:121). They say it out loud.\n\n'We now believe' — amanna. One word in Arabic, and it is spoken in the worst possible place to say it.\n\n'In the Lord of all worlds' — bi rabbi al-'alamin. The exact title Musa used in his first sentence to Pharaoh at 7:104.\n\nThat is the phrase Pharaoh queried in Surah ash-Shu'ara at 26:23 — 'And what is \"the Lord of all worlds\"?' — and it has now been declared in his court by the men he hired.\n\nNotice the precision of what they say. They do not say 'Musa is right' or 'we lost'. They name the One they now believe in.\n\nThat distinction matters because Pharaoh's next accusation, at 7:123, will be that they are Musa's accomplices. Their words at 7:121 and 7:122 already refute it: their allegiance is stated to Allah, not to the man.\n\nThe prostration of 7:120 was an action. This is the statement that interprets it, and the two together leave nothing ambiguous.\n\nConsider the speed of it. Between the display of 7:116 and this declaration, perhaps a minute has passed.\n\nConversion is not always slow. When the obstacle is not ignorance but interest, removing the obstacle can take one moment — and these men had just watched their interest swallowed by a stick.\n\nSurah ash-Shu'ara records the identical words at 26:47.",
  "122": "'\"the Lord of Moses and Aaron.\"' (Quran 7:122). The clarification, and every word of it is chosen.\n\nThey have just said 'the Lord of all worlds'. Now they specify, and the specification closes a loophole.\n\n'Rabbi Musa wa Harun' — the Lord of Musa and Harun. In a country where the king claimed to be lord, the phrase 'Lord of all worlds' could conceivably have been twisted into a compliment to Pharaoh himself.\n\nBy naming the two men who came against him, they make that impossible.\n\nSurah Ta-Ha preserves the same declaration with the names in the other order at 20:70: 'We believe in the Lord of Aaron and Moses.'\n\nThe commentators note the order there is for the rhyme of that surah; both are the same statement.\n\nNotice something else. They name Harun, who has said nothing in this entire episode. He was mentioned once at 7:111 as 'his brother', and he is included here as an equal.\n\nThat is fidelity to the facts. The mission was given to two men, and the magicians credit both.\n\n'Musa' — and not 'our master Musa'. They do not enrol as his followers or his students. They identify whose Lord it is.\n\nEverything about this declaration is designed to be unmistakable, which is exactly what a public confession should be.\n\nThey are about to find out what it costs.",
  "123": "'Pharaoh threatened, \"How dare you believe in him before I give you permission? This must be a conspiracy you devised in the city to drive out its people, but soon you will see.\"' (Quran 7:123). The reaction of a man who has lost control of his own event.\n\n'How dare you believe in him before I give you permission' — amantum bihi qabla an adhana lakum.\n\nRead that literally. He is claiming the right to license belief.\n\nThat is the clearest statement of what tyranny actually wants. Not obedience in action — permission to be required before conviction.\n\n'This must be a conspiracy you devised in the city' — inna hadha la makrun makartumuhu fi al-madinah. 'Makr' — the word of 7:99, now used by Pharaoh about other people.\n\nThe man running the plan accuses others of plotting. And the accusation is transparently false: he summoned these men himself, from cities across Egypt, at his own initiative.\n\n'To drive out its people' — li tukhriju minha ahlaha. Expulsion again, the same charge levelled at Musa at 7:110, and before that at Lut in 7:82 and at Shu'aib in 7:88.\n\nFour times in this surah the threat of displacement is used against people who threaten no one. It is the standard accusation of those defending a position they cannot argue for.\n\n'But soon you will see' — fa sawfa ta'lamun. You will know.\n\nNotice that he offers no rebuttal of what happened. The staff, the swallowed ropes, the prostration of his own experts — none of it is addressed.\n\nHe moves straight from losing the argument to punishing the people who conceded it.",
  "124": "'\"I will certainly cut off your hands and feet on opposite sides, then crucify you all.\"' (Quran 7:124). The threat, in detail.\n\n'On opposite sides' — min khilaf. The right hand and the left foot, or the reverse.\n\nThe specificity is the cruelty. This is not rage; it is a described procedure, announced calmly, designed to be imagined by everyone listening.\n\n'Then crucify you all' — thumma la usallibannakum ajma'in. Public display of the bodies afterwards.\n\nSurah ash-Shu'ara records the identical sentence at 26:49, and Surah Ta-Ha adds at 20:71: 'crucify you on the trunks of palm trees.'\n\nConsider the audience for this. It is not really the magicians. It is the crowd that has just watched them prostrate.\n\nThe signs were public, so the punishment must be public. Pharaoh cannot unmake what the country saw, so he sets out to make the cost of agreeing with it unbearable.\n\nThat is what force is for at this point in the story. It is the argument of last resort, deployed exactly when the arguments have run out — which is the sequence 7:118 described: 'the truth prevailed and their illusions failed.'\n\nNote also that the threat is directed at everyone: 'ajma'in', all of you. No offer of pardon, no attempt to peel off individuals.\n\nHe has abandoned persuasion entirely.\n\nAnd the extraordinary thing — the thing the surah is building towards — is that it does not work. Not on one of them.",
  "125": "'They responded, \"Surely to our Lord we will ˹all˺ return.\"' (Quran 7:125). Eight words in reply to mutilation and crucifixion.\n\n'Surely to our Lord we will all return' — inna ila rabbina munqalibun. 'Munqalibun' — those who are turning back, returning.\n\nLook at the word Pharaoh's court supplied only six verses earlier. At 7:119 they 'turned back' defeated — 'wanqalabu saghirin', from the identical root.\n\nThe magicians take the same verb and give it a destination. Everyone turns back; the question is to whom.\n\nNotice that they do not argue with the sentence. They do not deny the conspiracy charge of 7:123, plead, bargain, or ask for mercy from the man who has it to give.\n\nThey answer the threat by identifying its ceiling. Pharaoh can send them somewhere; the place he can send them is where they were going.\n\nSurah Ta-Ha records the fuller version at 20:72: 'So do whatever you want! Your authority only covers the fleeting life of this world.'\n\nThat single sentence is the correct measurement of every tyrant in history, and it was worked out by men who had been believers for under an hour.\n\nRecall their first recorded words, at 7:113: 'Shall we receive a suitable reward if we prevail?'\n\nSame men. Same day. The reward they were negotiating for has become irrelevant, and so has the punishment.\n\nNothing in between changed except what they knew to be true.",
  "126": "'\"Your rage towards us is only because we believed in the signs of our Lord when they came to us. Our Lord! Shower us with perseverance, and let us die while submitting ˹to You˺.\"' (Quran 7:126). Their last words, and half of it is a prayer.\n\n'Your rage towards us is only because we believed' — wa ma tanqimu minna illa an amanna. You resent us for one thing only.\n\nThat is a precise reply to the accusation of 7:123. Pharaoh charged them with a plot against the city; they name the real offence.\n\n'In the signs of our Lord when they came to us' — lamma ja'atna. When they arrived. They responded to evidence at the moment it appeared, which is exactly what 7:101 says the destroyed nations would not do.\n\nThen they stop speaking to Pharaoh entirely and turn to Allah.\n\n'Our Lord! Shower us with perseverance' — rabbana afrigh 'alayna sabra. 'Afrigh' — pour it over us, the way water is poured. Al-Baqarah has the same words at 2:250, from an army facing Goliath.\n\nThey do not ask to be rescued. They ask to be able to bear it.\n\n'And let us die while submitting to You' — wa tawaffana muslimin. Take us as Muslims.\n\nKhabbab ibn al-Aratt, tortured in Mecca, complained to the Prophet, peace be upon him, and was told of believers before them: 'a saw would be put over his head and he would be cut into two pieces; yet that torture would not make him give up his religion' (Sahih al-Bukhari 3612).\n\nThe magicians came for a fee. They left asking only for endurance and a good ending.",
  "127": "'The chiefs of Pharaoh's people protested, \"Are you going to leave Moses and his people free to spread corruption in the land and abandon you and your gods?\" He responded, \"We will kill their sons and keep their women. We will completely dominate them.\"' (Quran 7:127). The court regroups.\n\n'Are you going to leave Moses and his people free' — a tadharu Musa wa qawmah. The chiefs push their king, who has just been humiliated in public, towards violence.\n\n'To spread corruption in the land' — li yufsidu fi al-ard. And there is the accusation, inverted as usual.\n\n'Fasad' is the charge the surah lays against Pharaoh himself at 7:103: 'See what was the end of the corruptors.' Surah al-Qasas states it directly at 28:4: 'He was truly one of the corruptors.'\n\nThe man enslaving a nation calls the man asking for their release a corruptor.\n\n'And abandon you and your gods' — wa yadharaka wa alihatak. A revealing slip. Pharaoh claimed at 28:38 to know of no god but himself, yet his court speaks of his gods, plural.\n\n'We will kill their sons and keep their women' — sanuqattilu abna'ahum wa nastahyi nisa'ahum. The policy is restarted. Al-Baqarah records the original at 2:49, and 28:4 too.\n\nMusa survived that policy as an infant. Pharaoh's response to Musa is to run it again.\n\n'We will completely dominate them' — wa inna fawqahum qahirun. We are above them, crushing.\n\nNotice that no argument is offered against anything Musa said or did. The reply to a proof is a threat against children.",
  "128": "'Moses reassured his people, \"Seek Allah's help and be patient. Indeed, the earth belongs to Allah ˹alone˺. He grants it to whoever He chooses of His servants. The ultimate outcome belongs ˹only˺ to the righteous.\"' (Quran 7:128). What he tells people who have just heard that their sons will be killed.\n\n'Seek Allah's help and be patient' — ista'inu billahi wasbiru. Two commands, in that order.\n\n'Isti'anah' before 'sabr'. Ask for help first, because patience without help is only endurance, and endurance runs out.\n\nThe instruction is the same one Shu'aib gave his divided town at 7:87: 'be patient until Allah judges between us.'\n\n'The earth belongs to Allah alone' — inna al-arda lillah. That sentence is the direct answer to 7:127.\n\nPharaoh's court spoke of 'the land' as theirs to protect and of the Israelites as theirs to crush. Musa says the ownership is elsewhere, and always was.\n\n'He grants it to whoever He chooses of His servants' — yurithuha man yasha'u min 'ibadih. He gives it as an inheritance.\n\nThe surah has already shown this happening five times: 'Ad inherited from Nuh's people at 7:69, Thamud from 'Ad at 7:74, and 7:100 asked the inheritors whether they had learned anything.\n\n'The ultimate outcome belongs only to the righteous' — wal-'aqibatu lil-muttaqin. 'Aqibah' — the end of the matter. The word used at 7:84, 7:86 and 7:103 when the reader was told to look at how things finished.\n\nHe does not promise them rescue by a certain date. He tells them who owns the ground and how endings work.",
  "129": "'They complained, \"We have always been oppressed—before and after you came to us.\" He replied, \"Perhaps your Lord will destroy your enemy and make you successors in the land to see what you will do.\"' (Quran 7:129). An honest complaint, and a warning disguised as good news.\n\n'Before and after you came to us' — min qabli an ta'tiyana wa min ba'di ma ji'tana. Their point is blunt: nothing improved when you arrived; it got worse.\n\nThe Quran records the complaint without rebuking it. These are enslaved people whose children are again being killed, and their frustration is preserved as it was spoken.\n\nKhabbab ibn al-Aratt made a similar complaint to the Prophet, peace be upon him, in Mecca, asking him to pray for relief, and was answered with the account of believers sawn in two who did not abandon their religion, and then: 'By Allah, this religion will prevail... but you people are hasty' (Sahih al-Bukhari 3612).\n\n'Perhaps your Lord will destroy your enemy and make you successors in the land' — that is the promise, and Surah al-Qasas confirms it at 28:5.\n\nThen the clause that changes everything: 'to see what you will do' — fa yanzura kayfa ta'malun.\n\nThe rescue is not the end of the test. It is the next question.\n\nThat single line governs the rest of this surah. Every failure of the Israelites from 7:138 onwards — the demand for an idol, the calf, the covenant — happens inside 'to see what you will do'.\n\nPower removed from an oppressor is power handed to somebody else.",
  "130": "'Indeed, We afflicted Pharaoh's people with famine and shortage of crops so they might come back ˹to their senses˺.' (Quran 7:130). The first pressure applied to Egypt.\n\n'With famine' — bil-sinin. Literally: with years. In Arabic usage, 'the years' means the years of drought.\n\n'And shortage of crops' — wa naqsin mina al-thamarat. A decrease in fruits.\n\nNote the scale of it. Not a plague, not an army — a poor harvest, repeated. Egypt was the granary of the ancient world, and what it is given is a shortfall.\n\n'So they might come back to their senses' — la'allahum yadhdhakkarun. That they might remember.\n\nThe purpose is stated, and it is the same purpose named at 7:94: 'We afflicted its people with suffering and adversity, so perhaps they would be humbled.'\n\nSo Egypt is now being treated exactly as the five destroyed nations were treated. The general rule of 7:94 to 7:95 is being demonstrated on the largest empire in the story.\n\nAbu Sa'id al-Khudri and Abu Hurayrah reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'No fatigue, nor disease, nor sorrow, nor sadness, nor hurt, nor distress befalls a Muslim, even if it were the prick he receives from a thorn, but that Allah expiates some of his sins for that' (Sahih al-Bukhari 5641).\n\nDifficulty is not, by itself, a verdict. What it becomes depends on the response, and the next verse records Egypt's.",
  "131": "'In times of prosperity, they said, \"This is what we deserve,\" but in adversity, they blamed it on Moses and those with him. Surely all is destined by Allah. Yet most of them did not know.' (Quran 7:131). Two responses, both wrong.\n\n'In times of prosperity, they said, \"This is what we deserve\"' — hadhihi lana. This is ours.\n\nGood years are treated as earnings. No question is asked about where a harvest comes from, because nothing seems to need explaining when things go well.\n\n'But in adversity, they blamed it on Moses and those with him' — yattayyaru bi Musa wa man ma'ah. The verb is from 'tiyarah' — reading omens from birds, treating someone as a source of bad luck.\n\nSo the bad years are not theirs. Those belong to the newcomers with the strange message.\n\nThis is one of the most recognisable habits the Quran describes. Success is merit; failure is somebody else.\n\nThe same accusation was thrown at Salih. Surah an-Naml records it at 27:47: 'You and your followers are a bad omen for us' — and the reply: 'Your omens are destined by Allah. In fact, you are only a people being tested.'\n\nSurah Ya-Sin gives the sharper answer at 36:19: 'Your bad omen lies within yourselves.'\n\n'Surely all is destined by Allah' — ala innama ta'iruhum 'inda Allah. Their omen is with Allah — the good years and the bad ones come from the same hand.\n\n'Yet most of them did not know' — the word 'most' again, as at 7:102 and 7:187.",
  "132": "'They said, \"No matter what sign you may bring to deceive us, we will never believe in you.\"' (Quran 7:132). The position stated openly.\n\n'No matter what sign you may bring' — mahma ta'tina bihi min ayah. Whatever you produce.\n\n'We will never believe in you' — fa ma nahnu laka bi mu'minin.\n\nThis is the most honest sentence anyone speaks in the whole Egyptian episode, and it should be read carefully, because it settles the question the surah has been asking since 7:9.\n\nThey are not saying the evidence is insufficient. They are announcing in advance that no evidence will be sufficient.\n\nRecall 7:106, where Pharaoh demanded a sign 'if what you say is true'. That demand is now exposed. It was never a request for proof.\n\nAl-An'am predicted exactly this at 6:109 and 6:111: even if angels came down and the dead spoke to them, 'they still would not believe unless Allah so willed.'\n\nAnd 7:101 gave the reason: 'they would not believe in what they had already denied.'\n\n'To deceive us' — li tasharana biha. To bewitch us with it. They still use the vocabulary of 7:109, calling the signs magic while conceding that the signs keep coming.\n\nNote the practical consequence for the reader. Argument is not always the missing ingredient. A person who has decided in advance is not short of information.\n\nVerse 7:133 records what was sent anyway.",
  "133": "'So We plagued them with floods, locusts, lice, frogs, and blood—all as clear signs, but they persisted in arrogance and were a wicked people.' (Quran 7:133). Five signs, sent to people who had just said no sign would work.\n\n'Floods' — al-tufan. 'Locusts' — al-jarad. 'Lice' — al-qummal. 'Frogs' — al-dafadi'. 'Blood' — al-dam.\n\nLook at the list. Every one of them is a natural thing, and every one arrives in the wrong quantity or the wrong place.\n\nThat is a deliberate design. These are not thunderbolts from a clear sky; they are ordinary features of the Nile valley, turned into an affliction.\n\nWhich means each one could still be explained away as a bad season, and that is precisely what makes them a test rather than a compulsion.\n\n'All as clear signs' — ayatin mufassalat. Detailed, distinct, separated one from another. They came in sequence, with gaps.\n\nThose gaps matter, and 7:134 shows what happened in them.\n\n'But they persisted in arrogance' — fastakbaru. The word again — Iblis at 7:13, the deniers at 7:36 and 7:40, Thamud's chiefs at 7:75, Midian's at 7:88.\n\nThe surah has now used it of every rejecting party in its pages. One disease, many centuries.\n\n'And were a wicked people' — wa kanu qawman mujrimin.\n\nNote that the number of signs is never the issue. Five arrive here; nine are counted at 27:12. The count changes nothing.",
  "134": "'When tormented, they pleaded, \"O Moses! Pray to your Lord on our behalf, by virtue of the covenant He made with you. If you help remove this torment from us, we will certainly believe in you and let the Children of Israel go with you.\"' (Quran 7:134). The offer, made under pressure.\n\n'Pray to your Lord on our behalf' — ud'u lana rabbak. They ask the man they called a magician to intercede for them.\n\nNotice what that request concedes. They know whose Lord answers, and they know Musa has access.\n\n'By virtue of the covenant He made with you' — bima 'ahida 'indak. They even argue from Musa's standing with Allah, using theology they officially reject.\n\n'We will certainly believe in you and let the Children of Israel go with you' — the two things Musa asked for at 7:104 and 7:105, offered freely.\n\nSo the entire mission succeeds, on paper, in a single sentence.\n\nAnd it is worthless, because of one word: 'if'. The belief is conditional on the relief.\n\nThat is the mechanism al-An'am described at 6:63, where people in danger 'call upon Him with humility... \"If You rescue us from this, we will be ever grateful\"' — and 6:64 records what happens next: they associate others with Him again.\n\nPressure produces sincerity that lasts exactly as long as the pressure.\n\nNote also that Musa prays anyway. The removal in 7:135 happens because he asked, which tells you something about the man: he interceded for the court that had threatened to crucify its own magicians.",
  "135": "'But as soon as We removed the torment from them—until they met their inevitable fate—they broke their promise.' (Quran 7:135). The promise of the previous verse, tested.\n\n'As soon as We removed the torment' — fa lamma kashafna 'anhumu al-rijz. The relief is granted immediately, every time.\n\n'They broke their promise' — idha hum yankuthun. 'Nakth' is untwisting a rope that was spun — undoing something that had been made.\n\nThe word choice is exact. A promise is a thing constructed; breaking it is unpicking it.\n\n'Until they met their inevitable fate' — ila ajalin hum balighuh. Up to a term they were going to reach.\n\nThere is 'ajal' again, the appointed term of 7:34: 'For each community there is an appointed term.' Egypt has one too, and this verse says the cycle of plague, promise and betrayal simply filled the time until it arrived.\n\nConsider how many chances that represents. Five signs at 7:133, each with an appeal, a prayer, a relief and a broken word.\n\nNobody in this story can claim they were given one opportunity.\n\nAnd the pattern is not exotic. Anyone who has made promises during an illness, or a financial crisis, or a period of fear, knows this cycle from the inside.\n\nThe Quran records it here at national scale so it can be recognised at personal scale.\n\nWhat ends it is not another sign. It is the term.",
  "136": "'So We inflicted punishment upon them, drowning them in the sea for denying Our signs and being heedless of them.' (Quran 7:136). The end of Pharaoh.\n\n'Drowning them in the sea' — aghraqnahum fi al-yamm. Water again, and the surah does not narrate the parting or the pursuit here. It states the outcome.\n\nThat compression matches 7:64, where the flood of Nuh took half a verse, and 7:78, where Thamud fell in eight words.\n\nSurah Yunus supplies the detail this verse omits, at 10:90: as Pharaoh was drowning he cried, 'I believe that there is no god except that in whom the Children of Israel believe.'\n\nAnd the answer at 10:91: 'Now? But you always disobeyed and were one of the corruptors.'\n\nThat is the sixth confession recorded in this surah's territory, after 7:5, 7:37, 7:44 and 7:53, and it arrives at the same moment as all the others: too late.\n\nSurah an-Nisa states the rule at 4:18: repentance is not accepted from those who persist 'until they start dying, and then cry, \"Now I repent!\"'\n\n'For denying Our signs and being heedless of them' — ghafilin. Heedless. The charge is not only denial but inattention.\n\nIbn 'Abbas reported that the Prophet, peace be upon him, came to Madinah and found the Jews fasting on 'Ashura, saying it was 'the day on which Allah rescued Bani Israel from their enemy'; he said 'We have more claim over Moses than you', and fasted it and ordered it to be fasted (Sahih al-Bukhari 2004).\n\nThe rescue is still marked.",
  "137": "'And ˹so˺ We made the oppressed people successors of the eastern and western lands, which We had showered with blessings... And We destroyed what Pharaoh and his people constructed and what they established.' (Quran 7:137). The inheritance, exactly as promised.\n\n'The oppressed people' — al-qawma alladhina kanu yustad'afun. Those who were made weak — the same description used of Thamud's believers at 7:75.\n\n'We made them successors' — awrathna. We gave them the inheritance, the verb Musa used at 7:128: 'He grants it to whoever He chooses of His servants.'\n\nVerse 7:129 promised this: 'Perhaps your Lord will destroy your enemy and make you successors in the land.' Eight verses later it has happened.\n\n'The noble Word of your Lord was fulfilled... for what they had endured' — bima sabaru. Because they were patient.\n\nThat is the payoff of the instruction at 7:128: 'Seek Allah's help and be patient.' The Quran connects the endurance to the outcome explicitly.\n\n'And We destroyed what Pharaoh and his people constructed and what they established' — ma kana yasna'u Fir'awnu wa qawmuhu wa ma kanu ya'rishun.\n\nEgypt's monuments were the most permanent things human beings had built. Surah ad-Dukhan describes what was left behind at 44:25: 'how many gardens and springs the tyrants left behind' — and at 44:28: 'We awarded it all to another people.'\n\nRemember 7:129, though: 'to see what you will do.' The inheritance has arrived, and the test attached to it starts in the very next verse.",
  "138": "'We brought the Children of Israel across the sea and they came upon a people devoted to idols. They demanded, \"O Moses! Make for us a god like their gods.\" He replied, \"Indeed, you are a people acting ignorantly!\"' (Quran 7:138). The test of 7:129 begins immediately.\n\n'We brought the Children of Israel across the sea' — the rescue is one clause long.\n\n'And they came upon a people devoted to idols' — 'ala asnamin lahum. Statues.\n\n'Make for us a god like their gods' — ij'al lana ilahan kama lahum alihah.\n\nRead that with the timing in mind. The sea has just closed behind them. The water that drowned Pharaoh is still in view.\n\nAnd their request is for an idol, because the neighbours have some.\n\nNotice the reason embedded in 'kama lahum' — like theirs. Not an argument, not a doctrine. Other people have one and we do not.\n\nAbu Waqid al-Laythi reported that on a journey the companions passed a tree the idolaters hung their weapons on and said: 'O Messenger of Allah! Make a Dhat Anwat for us as they have a Dhat Anwat.' The Prophet, peace be upon him, said: 'Subhan Allah! This is like what Musa's people said: Make for us a god like their gods. By the One in Whose Hand is my soul! You shall follow the way of those who were before you' (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 2180, graded sahih).\n\nThat hadith is the reason this verse matters to every later community. The impulse is not ancient; it is human.\n\n'You are a people acting ignorantly' — qawmun tajhalun. Not wicked — ignorant.",
  "139": "'\"What they follow is certainly doomed to destruction and their deeds are in vain.\"' (Quran 7:139). Musa's assessment of the religion they admired.\n\n'What they follow is certainly doomed to destruction' — inna ha'ula'i mutabbarun ma hum fih. 'Tabar' is ruin, being smashed to pieces.\n\nHe is not describing a future punishment. He is describing the thing itself: what these people are engaged in is already broken.\n\n'And their deeds are in vain' — wa batilun ma kanu ya'malun. 'Batil' — void, empty.\n\nThat exact phrase appeared twenty-one verses earlier, at 7:118: 'and their illusions failed' — 'wa batala ma kanu ya'malun'.\n\nThe surah is repeating itself deliberately. The magicians' ropes and the idol-worshippers' rituals get the same verdict, in the same words.\n\nAnd the people asking for the idol at 7:138 had watched the first of those collapses in person. They saw 'batil' exposed in an arena, and are now requesting some of their own.\n\nNotice what Musa does not do. He does not mock the neighbours or call for their statues to be smashed. He explains what is wrong with the request.\n\n'Ma hum fih' — what they are in. Their whole situation, not just their objects.\n\nThat is the accurate way to describe idolatry: not a set of stones, but a condition people live inside.\n\nThe answer given here is an argument, offered to people who had just made an ignorant demand — and it is offered without contempt.",
  "140": "'He added, \"Shall I seek for you a god other than Allah, while He has honoured you above the others?\"' (Quran 7:140). One question, and it closes the matter.\n\n'Shall I seek for you a god other than Allah' — a ghayra Allahi abghikum ilaha. Am I to go looking for one?\n\nThe absurdity is in the verb. Gods, on this model, are things you shop for — which is exactly what 7:138 requested: one like the neighbours have.\n\n'While He has honoured you above the others' — wa huwa faddalakum 'ala al-'alamin. He preferred you over the peoples.\n\nThat is the argument, and it is made from their own recent history. Al-Baqarah puts it at 2:47 and ad-Dukhan at 44:32.\n\nConsider the sequence they had just lived through. Enslaved, their sons killed, then rescued in front of the empire that owned them, and given the inheritance of 7:137.\n\nThe favour is not a claim requiring proof. It is the previous six verses.\n\nAnd the request of 7:138 is therefore not merely wrong; it is a failure to notice what had happened to them.\n\nRecall 7:10: 'We have established you on the earth and provided you with a means of livelihood. ˹Yet˺ you seldom give any thanks.' The surah named this fault in its opening pages, and here is the demonstration.\n\nNote also that Musa answers three times — 7:138, 7:139 and 7:140 — with an assessment, an argument, and a question. Not one threat.\n\nThe next verse reminds them what they were rescued from.",
  "141": "'And ˹remember˺ when We rescued you from the people of Pharaoh, who afflicted you with dreadful torment—killing your sons and keeping your women. That was a severe test from your Lord.' (Quran 7:141). The reminder attached to the demand for an idol.\n\n'Remember when We rescued you' — the address turns from Musa's speech to a direct word to the audience.\n\n'Killing your sons and keeping your women' — yuqattiluna abna'akum wa yastahyuna nisa'akum. The policy announced at 7:127, described here from the side that suffered it.\n\nAl-Baqarah records the same sentence at 2:49, and al-Qasas at 28:4.\n\nThe reminder is placed exactly where it belongs. Three verses ago they asked for an idol; this is what they were saved from, and how recently.\n\n'That was a severe test from your Lord' — bala'un min rabbikum 'azim. And this is the word to notice: 'bala''.\n\n'Bala'' in Arabic covers both trial and favour, because both are tests. The Quran uses it for the affliction and for the rescue alike.\n\nVerse 7:129 already stated the principle: 'to see what you will do.' The slavery tested them, and so did the deliverance.\n\nThat is a difficult idea and the surah keeps insisting on it. Verse 7:94 described hardship as a summons; 7:95 described ease as a trap.\n\nNeither condition is a verdict. Each is a question.\n\nSo the demand of 7:138 was not a small lapse. It was the first answer given to the second half of the test, and it came within days of the sea closing.",
  "142": "'We appointed for Moses thirty nights then added another ten—completing his Lord's term of forty nights. Moses commanded his brother Aaron, \"Take my place among my people, do what is right, and do not follow the way of the corruptors.\"' (Quran 7:142). An appointment, and a handover.\n\n'Thirty nights then added another ten' — the extension is mentioned as part of the plan, not as a delay.\n\nThat detail matters for what follows. The people will treat the extra ten nights as an absence that went on too long — Surah Ta-Ha records Musa asking them at 20:86: 'Has my absence been too long for you?'\n\nAl-Baqarah refers to the same forty nights at 2:51.\n\n'Take my place among my people' — ukhlufni fi qawmi. Be my deputy.\n\n'Do what is right' — wa aslih. Repair, put right. The verb of 7:56 and 7:35.\n\n'And do not follow the way of the corruptors' — wa la tattabi' sabila al-mufsidin. 'Mufsidin' — the word used of Pharaoh at 7:103 and of Midian's chiefs at 7:86.\n\nMusa says this to Harun, a prophet in his own right. It is not an accusation; it is the standing instruction given to anyone left in charge.\n\nNotice the shape of the arrangement. A community is left without its main teacher for forty days, with a deputy, a clear instruction, and everything they had just witnessed.\n\nThat is the setting. What they do with it is at 7:148, and the surah tells you about Musa's mountain first.",
  "143": "'When Moses came at the appointed time and his Lord spoke to him, he asked, \"My Lord! Reveal Yourself to me so I may see You.\" Allah answered, \"You cannot see Me! But look at the mountain...\"' (Quran 7:143). The request no one else has made.\n\n'His Lord spoke to him' — wa kallamahu rabbuh. Directly. Surah an-Nisa states it at 4:164: 'And to Moses Allah spoke directly.'\n\n'Reveal Yourself to me so I may see You' — rabbi arini anzur ilayk.\n\nThe request comes from a man who has just been spoken to. Hearing produced the desire to see, and the Quran records the desire without condemning it.\n\n'You cannot see Me' — lan tarani. Not in this life, not in this frame.\n\n'But look at the mountain. If it remains firm in its place, only then will you see Me' — the answer is turned into a demonstration.\n\n'When his Lord appeared to the mountain, He levelled it to dust' — ja'alahu dakka. Crushed flat.\n\n'And Moses collapsed unconscious' — wa kharra Musa sa'iqa. He fell, struck down.\n\nAbu Musa reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'His veil is the light. If He withdraws it, the splendour of His countenance would consume His creation so far as His sight reaches' (Sahih Muslim 179a).\n\n'Glory be to You! I turn to You in repentance and I am the first of the believers' — subhanaka tubtu ilayk.\n\nHe repents. Not for a sin, but for asking beyond his capacity — which is what 'subhanaka' means: You are far above what I imagined.\n\nAl-An'am states the rule at 6:103: 'No vision can encompass Him, but He encompasses all vision.'",
  "144": "'Allah said, \"O Moses! I have ˹already˺ elevated you above all others by My messages and speech. So hold firmly to what I have given you and be grateful.\"' (Quran 7:144). The answer to a request that was refused.\n\n'I have already elevated you above all others' — istafaytuka 'ala al-nas. I have chosen you.\n\nNotice the timing of this. It comes immediately after 'You cannot see Me' — a refusal followed at once by a statement of what was given.\n\nThat order is a kindness, and it is worth learning from. Musa asked for something and did not get it; the reply reminds him of what he already has.\n\n'By My messages and speech' — bi risalati wa bi kalami. Two things: the messages entrusted to him, and being spoken to directly.\n\nThe second is unique. No one else in the Quran is described this way, which is why later tradition calls him Kalim Allah — the one Allah spoke to.\n\n'So hold firmly to what I have given you' — fa khudh ma ataytuka. Take it with strength.\n\n'And be grateful' — wa kun mina al-shakirin. Gratitude, again.\n\nThat instruction connects this scene to the whole surah. Verse 7:10 said 'you seldom give any thanks'; 7:17 recorded the enemy's prediction of ingratitude; 7:58 said the signs are varied 'to those who are thankful'.\n\nA prophet on a mountain is told the same thing his people are told.\n\nAnd there is a quiet lesson in that. The correct response to a boundary is not resentment about what lies beyond it, but gratitude for what lies inside it.",
  "145": "'We wrote for him on the Tablets ˹the fundamentals˺ of everything; commandments and explanations of all things. ˹We commanded,˺ \"Hold to this firmly and ask your people to take the best of it...\"' (Quran 7:145). The Torah is given.\n\n'We wrote for him on the Tablets' — katabna lahu fi al-alwah. Written, and given as an object.\n\n'Commandments and explanations of all things' — maw'izatan wa tafsilan li kulli shay'. Two categories: exhortation, which moves a person, and detail, which tells him what to do.\n\nBoth are needed. Rules without motive are dead weight; motive without rules is sentiment.\n\n'Tafsil' is the word used of the Quran at 7:52: 'a Book which We explained with knowledge.'\n\n'Hold to this firmly' — fa khudhha bi quwwah. The same instruction Musa personally received one verse earlier at 7:144. What was said to the prophet is said about the Book.\n\n'And ask your people to take the best of it' — ya'khudhu bi ahsaniha. Take the best of it.\n\nThe commentators explain this as taking the higher option where the law offers a choice — pardoning rather than retaliating, patience rather than a permitted response.\n\nSo the standard set is not the minimum the text allows.\n\n'I will soon show all of you the home of the rebellious' — sa'urikum dara al-fasiqin. The ruins again, as at 7:84 and 7:86.\n\nNotice the order of the whole passage: a refused request, a reminder of favour, then a Book. The mountain scene ends with something tangible in his hands.",
  "146": "'I will turn away from My signs those who act unjustly with arrogance in the land. And even if they were to see every sign, they still would not believe in them...' (Quran 7:146). The description of a closed mind.\n\n'Those who act unjustly with arrogance' — alladhina yatakabbaruna fi al-ardi bi ghayri al-haqq. There is 'takabbur' again, the surah's central diagnosis, running from 7:13 to 7:133.\n\n'I will turn away from My signs' — sa'asrifu 'an ayati. I will divert them from them.\n\nThe verb is from the same root as 'tasrif' at 7:58, where the signs are turned this way and that for people who are thankful. Here the people themselves are turned away.\n\n'Even if they were to see every sign, they still would not believe' — the exact statement al-An'am makes at 6:25.\n\nAnd Egypt has just demonstrated it. Verse 7:132: 'No matter what sign you may bring to deceive us, we will never believe in you.' Verse 7:133 sent five more anyway.\n\n'If they see the Right Path, they will not take it. But if they see a crooked path, they will follow it' — sabila al-rushd, sabila al-ghayy.\n\nBoth roads are visible to them. That is the point: this is not blindness but preference.\n\n'This is because they denied Our signs and were heedless of them' — and the causation is given last, deliberately.\n\nThe turning away is the consequence, not the cause. Verse 7:101 described the same door: denial first, sealing after.\n\nAl-An'am says it plainly at 6:110: 'as they refused to believe at first.'",
  "147": "'The deeds of those who deny Our signs and the meeting ˹with Allah˺ in the Hereafter will be in vain. Will they be rewarded except for what they have done?' (Quran 7:147). What happens to the good works of a denier.\n\n'Will be in vain' — habitat a'maluhum. 'Hubut' is the collapse of something that had swelled — the word is used of an animal that eats until it bloats and dies.\n\nThe image is exact. The deeds had volume; they had no substance.\n\n'Batil' was the verdict on the magicians' ropes at 7:118 and on the idols at 7:139. This is the same category applied to actions.\n\n'Those who deny Our signs and the meeting with Allah in the Hereafter' — the two denials are named together, as at 7:45.\n\nWhy does denial of the meeting void the deeds? Because the deeds were never addressed to anyone. A gift sent to no address is not a gift.\n\n'Will they be rewarded except for what they have done?' — hal yujzawna illa ma kanu ya'malun. The question is the whole justice of the arrangement.\n\nNothing is taken from them. They receive precisely what they built, and what they built was aimed at this world, which is where they received it.\n\nRecall 7:37: 'They will receive what is destined for them' — their full share, delivered in life.\n\nThe verse is not saying that a denier's kindness was worthless to those who received it. It is saying that on the scale of 7:8 it registers nothing, because weight there is measured by intention towards the One being met.",
  "148": "'In the absence of Moses, his people made from their ˹golden˺ jewellery an idol of a calf that made a lowing sound. Did they not see that it could neither speak to them nor guide them to the ˹Right˺ Path? Still they took it as a god and were wrongdoers.' (Quran 7:148). The forty nights, spent.\n\n'From their golden jewellery' — min huliyyihim. From ornaments they carried out of Egypt.\n\n'A calf that made a lowing sound' — 'ijlan jasadan lahu khuwar. A body, with a sound. Surah Ta-Ha describes it at 20:88 and names the man responsible, the Samiri, at 20:85.\n\n'A lowing sound' — that is the whole of its evidence, and it was enough.\n\n'Did they not see that it could neither speak to them nor guide them' — annahu la yukallimuhum wa la yahdihim sabila.\n\nConsider the argument the Quran uses here. It does not call the calf ugly or unworthy. It applies two tests: can it speak, and can it guide.\n\nBoth tests come straight from the previous verses. At 7:143 Musa's Lord spoke to him. At 7:145 He gave a Book of guidance.\n\nSo the community had a live demonstration of what the real thing does, and accepted a noise instead.\n\n'Still they took it as a god and were wrongdoers' — wa kanu zalimin.\n\nNotice the timing once more. Verse 7:138 was the request; 7:141 was the reminder of the rescue; 7:148 is the act itself, while Musa is on the mountain receiving the law against it.\n\nNothing here required a persuasive argument. It required only an absence.",
  "149": "'Later, when they were filled with remorse and realized they had gone astray, they cried, \"If our Lord does not have mercy on us and forgive us, we will certainly be losers.\"' (Quran 7:149). The turn.\n\n'When they were filled with remorse' — wa lamma suqita fi aydihim. A striking Arabic idiom: literally, when it was dropped into their hands.\n\nThe picture is of someone whose hands are suddenly full of what he did. Regret arrives as an object he cannot put down.\n\n'And realized they had gone astray' — wa ra'aw annahum qad dallu. They saw it.\n\nThen the words, and they should be familiar: 'If our Lord does not have mercy on us and forgive us, we will certainly be losers.'\n\nCompare 7:23, where Adam and his wife said: 'Our Lord! We have wronged ourselves. If You do not forgive us and have mercy on us, we will certainly be losers.'\n\nAlmost the same sentence, 126 verses apart, at the two ends of the surah's argument.\n\nThat parallel is the point. The surah opened by contrasting two responses to failure: Iblis, who explained, and Adam, who confessed. Here a whole nation takes Adam's option.\n\nNotice what the confession does not include. No blame on the Samiri, no complaint about Musa's long absence, no mention of the pressure described at 7:150.\n\n'Al-khasirin' — the losers. The word from the scale at 7:9, and from 7:23, and from 7:90 where Midian's chiefs used it as a threat.\n\nA person who says it about himself, in time, is not one.",
  "150": "'Upon Moses' return... he said, \"What an evil thing you committed in my absence!...\" Then he threw down the Tablets and grabbed his brother by the hair... Aaron pleaded, \"O son of my mother! The people overpowered me and were about to kill me...\"' (Quran 7:150). The return.\n\n'Furious and sorrowful' — ghadbana asifa. Both at once, and the Quran does not soften either.\n\n'Did you want to hasten your Lord's torment?' — the question refers to 7:142: he was gone forty nights, and they could not wait.\n\n'Then he threw down the Tablets' — wa alqa al-alwah. He puts down the Book he has just been told at 7:145 to 'hold firmly'.\n\nThe Quran records this without comment. A prophet in the grip of grief and anger acts, and the act is preserved rather than tidied away.\n\n'And grabbed his brother by the hair, dragging him closer' — Surah Ta-Ha has Harun pleading at 20:94: 'Do not seize me by my beard or the hair of my head.'\n\n'O son of my mother' — ya bna umm. He appeals by their shared mother, the most intimate form of address available to him.\n\n'The people overpowered me and were about to kill me' — istad'afuni wa kadu yaqtulunani. The verb 'istad'afu' is the surah's word for the oppressed at 7:75 and 7:137. Harun, the deputy in charge, was the one made weak.\n\nSurah Ta-Ha confirms he had warned them, at 20:90: 'O my people! You are only being tested by this.'\n\n'Nor count me among the wrongdoing people' — the one thing he asks not to be.",
  "151": "'Moses prayed, \"My Lord! Forgive me and my brother! And admit us into Your mercy. You are the Most Merciful of the merciful.\"' (Quran 7:151). The anger ends in a prayer.\n\n'Forgive me and my brother' — ighfir li wa li akhi. And notice the order: himself first.\n\nHe has just heard Harun's defence and accepted it completely. The man he seized by the hair is now included in his prayer as an equal, and Musa asks pardon for himself before asking it for him.\n\nThat sequence is the measure of the man. There is no attempt to justify the grip, the shouting, or the thrown Tablets.\n\n'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud reported that when a man questioned the Prophet's distribution of goods, the Prophet, peace be upon him, became visibly angry and then said: 'May Allah bestow His Mercy on Moses, for he was harmed more than this; yet he endured patiently' (Sahih al-Bukhari 3405).\n\n'And admit us into Your mercy' — wa adkhilna fi rahmatik. Into it, as into a place.\n\n'You are the Most Merciful of the merciful' — arhamu al-rahimin.\n\nThat title is chosen deliberately, standing over a community that has just worshipped a metal calf. The prayer of a leader for people who have failed badly appeals to the widest attribute available.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying that when Allah created the creation He wrote in His Book: 'Verily, My mercy predominates My wrath' (Sahih Muslim 2751a).\n\nVerse 7:152 is the wrath. Verse 7:153 is the mercy, and the surah puts them in that order for a reason.",
  "152": "'Those who worshipped the calf will certainly be afflicted with Allah's wrath as well as disgrace in the life of this world. This is how We reward those who invent falsehood.' (Quran 7:152). The consequence, stated in two parts.\n\n'Allah's wrath' — ghadabun min rabbihim. And 'disgrace in the life of this world' — dhillatun fi al-hayati al-dunya.\n\nTwo penalties, one in each world. The verse does not merge them.\n\n'Those who invent falsehood' — al-muftarin. 'Iftira'' — the fabricating that 7:33 forbade and 7:89 named: 'We would surely be fabricating a lie against Allah.'\n\nThat is how the calf is classified. Not as bad taste in worship, but as a claim about Allah with nothing behind it.\n\n'Dhillah' — lowness, humiliation. The opposite of the honour named at 7:140: 'He has honoured you above the others.'\n\nThe favour and its reverse are described in the same terms, which shows what was actually lost.\n\nAl-Baqarah records the immediate consequence at 2:54, where Musa tells them to turn in repentance to their Creator, and adds: 'Then He accepted your repentance.'\n\nSo this verse and that one are not in conflict. Punishment and accepted repentance both occurred.\n\nNotice the tense here: 'sayanaluhum' — it will reach them. The verse is a description of what such an act incurs, and the next verse states the standing exception.\n\nNothing about the wrath in this verse is presented as final.",
  "153": "'But those who commit evil, then repent and become ˹true˺ believers, your Lord will certainly be All-Forgiving, Most-Merciful.' (Quran 7:153). The door, left open one verse after the sentence.\n\n'Those who commit evil' — 'amilu al-sayyi'at. The plural covers everything, including a golden calf.\n\n'Then repent' — thumma tabu min ba'diha. After it. The order is fixed: the deed, then the turning.\n\n'And become true believers' — wa amanu. Repentance and belief together, because a turning without faith is only regret.\n\n'Your Lord will certainly be All-Forgiving, Most-Merciful' — la ghafurun rahim.\n\nLook at the placement of this verse. It comes directly after the harshest sentence in the Musa narrative, and it is addressed to the very people described there.\n\nThat is the pattern the surah has used from the beginning. At 7:23 Adam confessed and at 7:24 was sent down forgiven. At 7:149 the people confessed. Here the rule behind both is stated in the open.\n\nIbn Kathir, commenting on this verse, notes that it establishes a general principle: repentance is accepted from anyone who turns, whatever the sin, provided the turning is real.\n\n'Min ba'diha' — after it. There is no offence in this surah that is described as too late while a person still lives.\n\nThe only deadline the chapter recognises is the one at 7:34, the term, and the one at 7:37, the moment of death.\n\nEverything before that is inside the offer of this verse.",
  "154": "'When Moses' anger subsided, he took up the Tablets whose text contained guidance and mercy for those who stand in awe of their Lord.' (Quran 7:154). The Tablets are picked up.\n\n'When Moses' anger subsided' — wa lamma sakata 'an Musa al-ghadab. The Arabic is unusual and beautiful: literally, when the anger fell silent in Musa.\n\nAnger is described as something that was speaking. It had a voice, it said what it had to say, and then it stopped.\n\nThat is an accurate description of how anger works, and the Quran uses it about a prophet, without embarrassment.\n\n'He took up the Tablets' — akhadha al-alwah. The ones he threw down at 7:150.\n\nNothing is said about damage, and nothing is said about apology. The action itself is the resolution: the Book is back in his hands.\n\n'Whose text contained guidance and mercy' — hudan wa rahmah. The same pairing used of the Quran at 7:52: 'a guide and mercy for those who believe.'\n\n'For those who stand in awe of their Lord' — lilladhina hum li rabbihim yarhabun. Those who hold Him in awe.\n\nNotice who the guidance is said to be for. Not for the clever, or the strong, or the unblemished — for people with a particular posture towards their Lord.\n\nThat is the same qualification given at 7:52 and at 7:56, where mercy is 'close to the good-doers'.\n\nThe scene ends quietly. A leader recovers himself, picks up what he dropped, and continues.\n\nAnd the Book is the same Book it was before the calf, before the anger, and before the fall.",
  "155": "'Moses chose seventy men from among his people for Our appointment and, when they were seized by an earthquake, he cried, \"My Lord!... Will You destroy us for what the foolish among us have done? This is only a test from You...\"' (Quran 7:155). A delegation, and a prayer.\n\n'Seventy men' — sab'ina rajulan. A representative body, chosen for an appointed meeting.\n\n'When they were seized by an earthquake' — al-rajfah. The same word used for the destruction of Thamud at 7:78 and Midian at 7:91.\n\nThe surah uses its own vocabulary consistently, which makes the moment more frightening: this is the shaking that ended two civilisations.\n\n'Will You destroy us for what the foolish among us have done?' — a tuhlikuna bima fa'ala al-sufaha'u minna. 'Sufaha'' — the fools, from 'safah', the very word 'Ad threw at Hud at 7:66.\n\nMusa is arguing that a community should not be destroyed for the acts of some within it. That is a plea, not a doctrine, and the surah is about to answer it.\n\n'This is only a test from You' — in hiya illa fitnatuk. He names the whole episode as a trial, which is the reading 7:141 gave: 'a severe test from your Lord.'\n\n'You are our Guardian' — anta waliyyuna. 'Wali' — the word this surah forbade applying to anyone else, at 7:3, 7:27 and 7:30.\n\n'So forgive us and have mercy on us. You are the best forgiver' — khayru al-ghafirin.\n\nHe asks for exactly what Adam asked at 7:23 and what the calf-worshippers asked at 7:149. Three times, the same two words: forgiveness and mercy.",
  "156": "'\"Ordain for us what is good in this life and the next...\" Allah replied, \"I will inflict My torment on whoever I will. But My mercy encompasses everything. I will ordain mercy for those who shun evil, pay alms-tax, and believe in Our revelations.\"' (Quran 7:156). The answer to the prayer, and it is one of the great verses of the Quran.\n\n'Ordain for us what is good in this life and the next' — waktub lana fi hadhihi al-dunya hasanatan wa fi al-akhirah. Write it for us.\n\n'My mercy encompasses everything' — wa rahmati wasi'at kulla shay'. There is no exception in that sentence. Everything.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying that when Allah created the creation He wrote in His Book: 'Verily, My mercy predominates My wrath' (Sahih Muslim 2751a).\n\nAl-An'am states it twice as something He took upon Himself, at 6:12 and 6:54.\n\nNote the structure. The punishment is described as 'whoever I will' — undefined. The mercy is described as encompassing everything, and then given a definite address.\n\n'I will ordain mercy for those who shun evil, pay alms-tax, and believe in Our revelations' — sa aktubuha lilladhina yattaqun.\n\nThree conditions, and the middle one is striking: 'yu'tuna al-zakah'. Between mindfulness and belief sits an act of giving.\n\nThe Quran repeatedly places money inside the definition of faith, exactly as Shu'aib placed scales inside worship at 7:85.\n\nThe verse answers Musa's request literally: he asked for it to be written, and the reply uses the same verb twice.",
  "157": "'\"˹They are˺ the ones who follow the Messenger, the unlettered Prophet, whose description they find in their Torah and the Gospel... and relieves them from their burdens and the shackles that bound them...\"' (Quran 7:157). The sentence continues, and it arrives somewhere unexpected.\n\nVerse 7:156 promised mercy to a described group. This verse identifies them, and the identification reaches across centuries to Muhammad, peace be upon him.\n\n'The unlettered Prophet' — al-nabiyya al-ummiyy. One who did not read or write. Surah al-'Ankabut treats that as evidence at 29:48.\n\n'Whose description they find in their Torah and the Gospel' — maktuban 'indahum. Written with them.\n\nSurah as-Saff records Jesus giving the same notice at 61:6, and Al 'Imran describes a covenant taken from the prophets at 3:81 to believe in and support a messenger confirming what they have.\n\n'He commands them to do good and forbids them from evil' — al-ma'ruf and al-munkar. The known good, the rejected wrong.\n\n'Permits for them what is lawful and forbids to them what is impure' — al-tayyibat and al-khaba'ith. The good things and the foul ones — the exact distinction of 7:32 and 7:33.\n\n'And relieves them from their burdens and the shackles that bound them' — wa yada'u 'anhum israhum wal-aghlala. He puts down the load and removes the chains.\n\nAl-Baqarah records the prayer that anticipates this at 2:286: 'Do not place a burden on us like the one you placed on those before us.'\n\n'Those who believe in him, honour and support him, and follow the light sent down to him will be successful' — al-muflihun, the word of the scale at 7:8.",
  "158": "'Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"O humanity! I am Allah's Messenger to you all...\" So believe in Allah and His Messenger, the unlettered Prophet, who believes in Allah and His revelations. And follow him, so you may be ˹rightly˺ guided.' (Quran 7:158). The Prophet is told to announce it himself.\n\n'O humanity! I am Allah's Messenger to you all' — inni rasulu Allahi ilaykum jami'a. To all of you.\n\nThat word 'jami'a' is the difference between this mission and every other one in the surah. Nuh went to his people, Hud to 'Ad, Salih to Thamud, Shu'aib to Midian, Musa to Pharaoh and the Israelites.\n\nEach was 'their brother', sent to one nation. This messenger is sent to everyone, and Surah Saba states it at 34:28: 'We have sent you only as a deliverer of good news and a warner to all of humanity.'\n\n'To Him alone belongs the kingdom of the heavens and the earth... He gives life and causes death' — the credentials are about Allah, not about the messenger. Exactly the move Nuh made at 7:61 and Hud at 7:67.\n\n'Who believes in Allah and His revelations' — wa yu'minu billahi wa kalimatih. The messenger is described as a believer first.\n\nThat is worth noticing. He is not placed above the people he calls; he is the first one following what he delivers.\n\n'And follow him, so you may be rightly guided' — wattabi'uh la'allakum tahtadun.\n\n'Follow' — the verb from the opening command of this surah at 7:3: 'Follow what has been sent down to you from your Lord.' One hundred and fifty-five verses later, the instruction returns.",
  "159": "'There are some among the people of Moses who guide with the truth and establish justice accordingly.' (Quran 7:159). A short verse, and a necessary one.\n\n'There are some among the people of Moses' — wa min qawmi Musa ummatun. A community among them.\n\nLook at where this sits. The surah has just described the calf, the changed words, the earthquake and the Sabbath is coming. And here, in one line, it refuses to let the account become a blanket verdict.\n\nThat refusal is a habit of the Quran. It says 'most of them' at 7:102, and 'some among them' here, and it means both precisely.\n\n'Who guide with the truth' — yahduna bil-haqq. They guide others, not just themselves.\n\n'And establish justice accordingly' — wa bihi ya'dilun. And by it they deal justly. The truth is not only what they teach; it is what they judge by.\n\nAl-Ma'idah gives the same instruction to the believers at 5:8: 'Do not let the hatred of a people lead you to injustice. Be just! That is closer to righteousness.'\n\nThat is the practical use of this verse. A reader who has just gone through fifteen verses of failure is being taught how to think about a group: by evidence, individually, and without collapsing a people into a single sentence.\n\n'Ummah' — the word means a community defined by something shared, not by descent.\n\nThe verse also prepares 7:168, where the same nation is described as 'some were righteous, others were less so.'\n\nGuidance is never the property of a bloodline.",
  "160": "'We divided them into twelve tribes—each as a community... \"Strike the rock with your staff.\" Then twelve springs gushed out... We shaded them with clouds and sent down to them manna and quails... They ˹certainly˺ did not wrong Us, but wronged themselves.' (Quran 7:160). The favours listed, and the verdict attached.\n\n'Twelve tribes' — ithnatay 'ashrata asbata. Al-Ma'idah mentions twelve leaders appointed for them at 5:12.\n\n'Strike the rock with your staff' — the same staff from 7:107 and 7:117. Al-Baqarah records the event at 2:60.\n\n'Then twelve springs gushed out. Each tribe knew its drinking place' — the provision is organised. Nobody has to fight for water, because the arrangement removes the competition.\n\nThat detail is easy to pass over and it is a mercy in itself. Order is part of the gift.\n\n'We shaded them with clouds' — al-ghamam. In a desert, shade is not a luxury.\n\n'And sent down to them manna and quails' — al-manna wal-salwa. Food that required no farming. Al-Baqarah gives the same list at 2:57.\n\nCount what has been provided: water, shade, and food, delivered without labour, to a people newly out of slavery.\n\n'Eat from the good things We have provided for you' — al-tayyibat. The word of 7:32 and 7:157.\n\n'They did not wrong Us, but wronged themselves' — wa ma zalamuna wa lakin kanu anfusahum yazlimun.\n\nThat is the sentence the whole surah has been building. Al-An'am said it at 6:12 of those who ruin themselves; Adam said it of himself at 7:23.\n\nHarm done to a command returns to the one who did it.",
  "161": "'And ˹remember˺ when it was said to them, \"Enter this city ˹of Jerusalem˺ and eat from wherever you please. Say, 'Absolve us,' and enter the gate with humility. We will forgive your sins, ˹and˺ We will multiply the reward for the good-doers.\"' (Quran 7:161). An offer with three easy conditions.\n\n'Enter this city and eat from wherever you please' — kulu minha haythu shi'tum.\n\nThose words are not new. They are almost exactly what Adam was told at 7:19: 'Live with your wife in Paradise and eat from wherever you please.'\n\nThe surah is repeating its opening scene with a different cast. A permission that is vast, and a single specified condition attached.\n\n'Say, \"Absolve us\"' — hittah. One word, a request for the lifting of sin.\n\n'And enter the gate with humility' — sujjadan. Bowing, lowering yourselves.\n\nSo the terms are: enter, eat freely, say one word, and come in low. Al-Baqarah records the same at 2:58.\n\n'We will forgive your sins' — naghfir lakum khatayakum.\n\n'We will multiply the reward for the good-doers' — sanazidu al-muhsinin. And the reward is not merely given; it is increased.\n\nRead the whole verse as a transaction and it is absurdly generous. Everything is offered; one syllable and a posture are asked.\n\nThat proportion is the surah's argument about the law itself, first made at 7:19 and repeated at 7:32 and 7:33: the permitted is enormous, the required is small.\n\nWhich makes what happens next difficult to explain by anything except the will to refuse.",
  "162": "'But the wrongdoers among them changed the words they were commanded to say. So We sent down a punishment from the heavens upon them for their wrongdoing.' (Quran 7:162). The one condition, altered.\n\n'The wrongdoers among them' — alladhina zalamu minhum. Among them. The surah is careful again, exactly as at 7:159.\n\n'Changed the words they were commanded to say' — baddalu qawlan ghayra alladhi qila lahum. They substituted a different statement.\n\nThey were told to say 'hittah' — absolve us. The early commentators report that they said something else, mocking the word by twisting its sound.\n\nConsider the size of the offence measured against the size of the act. One word, changed.\n\nAnd then consider what the change means. The word was a request for forgiveness; replacing it with a joke is a refusal to be in the position of someone asking.\n\nThat is the same refusal that runs through this entire surah. Iblis would not lower himself at 7:12. The chiefs would not accept a message from one of their own at 7:63 and 7:69. The instruction here was to enter 'with humility', and humility is precisely what was withheld.\n\n'So We sent down a punishment from the heavens' — rijzan mina al-sama'. 'Rijz' — the word used of the plagues on Egypt at 7:134 and 7:135.\n\n'For their wrongdoing' — bima kanu yazlimun. The 'zulm' of 7:160: harm that returns to the doer.\n\nAl-Baqarah records the identical episode at 2:59.",
  "163": "'Ask them ˹O Prophet˺ about ˹the people of˺ the town which was by the sea, who broke the Sabbath. During the Sabbath, ˹abundant˺ fish would come to them clearly visible, but on other days the fish were never seen. In this way We tested them for their rebelliousness.' (Quran 7:163). A test designed with precision.\n\n'Ask them about the town which was by the sea' — the Prophet is told to raise the case. It is a matter of record among them.\n\n'Who broke the Sabbath' — idh ya'duna fi al-sabt. The Sabbath was their one required abstention, and 4:154 records the covenant: 'Do not break the Sabbath.'\n\n'During the Sabbath, abundant fish would come to them clearly visible' — shurra'an. On the surface, in plain sight.\n\n'But on other days the fish were never seen' — la ta'tihim.\n\nLook at the design of this. The fish arrive precisely when they are forbidden and vanish precisely when they are permitted.\n\nNothing about it is subtle. Anyone could see it, which is why the verse says 'clearly visible'.\n\n'In this way We tested them' — kadhalika nablawhum. A test, and the word is 'bala'', the same as at 7:141 where the rescue from Pharaoh was called a severe test.\n\n'For their rebelliousness' — bima kanu yafsuqun. The 'fisq' of 7:102: stepping outside an agreement.\n\nThe commentators record that the community devised ways around the rule — trapping the fish on the Sabbath and collecting them afterwards — keeping the letter while emptying the intent.\n\nThat manoeuvre needs no ancient setting to be recognised.",
  "164": "'When some of ˹the righteous among˺ them questioned ˹their fellow Sabbath-keepers˺, \"Why do you ˹bother to˺ warn those who will either be destroyed or severely punished by Allah?\" They replied, \"Just to be free from your Lord's blame, and so perhaps they may abstain.\"' (Quran 7:164). Three groups, not two.\n\nThe town divides into the offenders, those who warned them, and those who asked what the point was.\n\n'Why do you bother to warn' — lima ta'izuna qawman. The question is not hostile. It is weary.\n\nThese are people who have watched the warnings fail and concluded that speaking is pointless. Anyone who has tried to correct something repeatedly will recognise the feeling exactly.\n\n'Just to be free from your Lord's blame' — ma'dhiratan ila rabbikum. So that we have an excuse before Him.\n\nThat is the first reason, and it is about the warner, not the warned. The duty is discharged regardless of the result.\n\n'And so perhaps they may abstain' — wa la'allahum yattaqun. And the second reason keeps hope alive without promising anything: perhaps.\n\nAbu Sa'id al-Khudri reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'He who amongst you sees something abominable should modify it with the help of his hand; and if he has not strength enough to do it, then he should do it with his tongue, and if he has not strength enough to do it, then he should abhor it from his heart, and that is the least of faith' (Sahih Muslim 49a).\n\nThe next verse states which of the three groups was saved, and the answer settles the debate.",
  "165": "'When they ignored the warning they were given, We rescued those who used to warn against evil and overtook the wrongdoers with a dreadful punishment for their rebelliousness.' (Quran 7:165). The verdict on the three groups.\n\n'We rescued those who used to warn against evil' — anjayna alladhina yanhawna 'ani al-su'. The warners are saved.\n\n'And overtook the wrongdoers' — the offenders are punished.\n\nAnd the third group — the ones who asked 'why do you bother?' at 7:164 — are not mentioned.\n\nThat silence has occupied the commentators for centuries. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this passage, records the disagreement among the early scholars about their fate, and reports Ibn 'Abbas's view that they were saved along with the warners.\n\nWhat the verse establishes beyond dispute is the safest position. Only one group is explicitly named as rescued, and it is the group that kept speaking.\n\n'Those who used to warn' — the tense is continuous. They kept at it, after it stopped working.\n\nThat is the answer to the question of 7:164. The value of the warning was never only its success rate.\n\n'With a dreadful punishment for their rebelliousness' — bi 'adhabin ba'isin bima kanu yafsuqun. The same word 'fisq' from 7:163.\n\nNotice how this closes the surah's long argument about messengers. Nuh, Hud, Salih, Shu'aib and Musa all delivered and were refused, and each one said afterwards: I conveyed, I advised.\n\nThis verse says what happens to people who do that.",
  "166": "'But when they stubbornly persisted in violation, We said to them, \"Be disgraced apes!\"' (Quran 7:166). The punishment named.\n\n'When they stubbornly persisted' — fa lamma 'ataw 'an ma nuhu 'anh. When they went beyond what they were forbidden.\n\n'Ataw' — the same verb used of Thamud at 7:77: 'defying their Lord's command.'\n\n'Be disgraced apes' — kunu qiradatan khasi'in. Al-Baqarah records the same words at 2:65, and al-Ma'idah refers to it at 5:60.\n\n'Khasi'in' — driven off, despised, the word used for chasing away a dog.\n\nThe early scholars differ over whether the transformation was physical or a description of their state, and the honest position is that the Quran states it without elaborating.\n\nWhat the wording does make clear is the humiliation. Whatever else happened, the point is 'khasi'in'.\n\nAnd there is a logic in the image that the surah has prepared. These were people who kept the outward form of the Sabbath while emptying it of meaning — imitating obedience without obeying.\n\nImitation without understanding is exactly what an ape does. The commentators have long noted that the punishment matches the offence in kind.\n\nRecall 7:146: 'if they see the Right Path, they will not take it. But if they see a crooked path, they will follow it.'\n\nThe crooked path here was a legal device. It was not ignorance that ruined them; it was cleverness applied against the purpose of a command they fully understood.",
  "167": "'And ˹remember, O Prophet,˺ when your Lord declared that He would send against them others who would make them suffer terribly until the Day of Judgment. Indeed, your Lord is swift in punishment, but He is certainly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.' (Quran 7:167). A long consequence, and then two names.\n\n'He would send against them others who would make them suffer' — la yab'athanna 'alayhim ila yawmi al-qiyamati man yasumuhum su'a al-'adhab.\n\n'Yasumuhum' is used of imposing something continuously — a burden laid on and kept there.\n\nNote who the agents are: 'others'. Human beings, sent against them. The punishment described here operates through history, not through a single event like the flood or the earthquake.\n\nThat is a different mode from everything in the first half of the surah, and it fits the position of this community: not destroyed like 'Ad, but subjected to a long correction.\n\n'Indeed, your Lord is swift in punishment' — sari'u al-'iqab.\n\n'But He is certainly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful' — wa innahu la ghafurun rahim.\n\nThe two halves of that sentence sit side by side without contradiction, and the order is deliberate: the warning first, the mercy last.\n\nThe surah has done this before. At 7:152 the wrath, at 7:153 the forgiveness. At 7:156 the punishment 'on whoever I will', then 'My mercy encompasses everything.'\n\nEvery time the pairing appears in this chapter, mercy is given the last word.\n\n'Ghafur' and 'Rahim' are the two names Adam's plea appealed to at 7:23, and they are still available at the end of this history.",
  "168": "'We dispersed them through the land in groups—some were righteous, others were less so. We tested them with prosperity and adversity, so perhaps they would return ˹to the Right Path˺.' (Quran 7:168). The scattering, and the reason for it.\n\n'We dispersed them through the land in groups' — wa qatta'nahum fi al-ardi umama. Cut them into communities.\n\n'Some were righteous, others were less so' — minhumu al-salihun wa minhum duna dhalik. The same balanced judgement as 7:159, repeated so nobody misses it.\n\n'We tested them with prosperity and adversity' — bil-hasanati wal-sayyi'at. With good things and bad.\n\nBoth are named as tests, and this is now the fourth time the surah has said so — at 7:94 with hardship, at 7:95 with ease, at 7:130 and 7:131 with Egypt's harvests, and at 7:141 with the rescue.\n\nEgypt failed this exact examination. Verse 7:131: 'In times of prosperity, they said, \"This is what we deserve,\" but in adversity, they blamed it on Moses.'\n\nThe surah is showing that the test does not change from nation to nation. Only the answers do.\n\n'So perhaps they would return' — la'allahum yarji'un. The purpose, again stated as hope rather than certainty.\n\nLook at how the whole Musa narrative has been closed out. Not with a verdict on a people, but with a description of a mixed community under a continuing test, and a stated purpose of return.\n\n'Perhaps they would return' is the last word on the longest history in the surah.\n\nThat is the tone the Quran keeps: the file is not closed while people are alive.",
  "169": "'Then they were succeeded by other generations who inherited the Scripture. They indulged in unlawful gains, claiming, \"We will be forgiven ˹after all˺.\"... Was a covenant not taken from them in the Scripture that they would not say anything about Allah except the truth?' (Quran 7:169). Inheritance without effort.\n\n'Who inherited the Scripture' — warithu al-kitab. They received it; nothing says they earned it.\n\nThe verb 'inherit' has run through this surah. Nations inherited land at 7:69, 7:74, 7:100 and 7:137. Here a community inherits a Book.\n\n'They indulged in unlawful gains' — ya'khudhuna 'arada hadha al-adna. They take the cheap offering of this lower world. 'Arad' is something passing, a temporary offer.\n\n'Claiming, \"We will be forgiven\"' — sayughfaru lana.\n\nThat is the sentence the verse exists to expose. Forgiveness is being used as a budget line: take the gain now, the pardon is guaranteed.\n\n'And if similar gain came their way, they would seize it' — the proof that the repentance was never intended. The next opportunity is taken on the same terms.\n\nCompare 7:153, where forgiveness follows repentance. Here the order is reversed: the pardon is claimed in advance, so nothing has to change.\n\n'Was a covenant not taken from them... that they would not say anything about Allah except the truth?' — the charge of 7:33, and Musa bound himself against it at 7:105.\n\nSaying 'we will be forgiven' while planning the next offence is a statement about Allah, made without knowledge.\n\n'And they were already well-versed in its teachings' — darasu ma fih. They studied it.\n\nKnowledge was not the missing element.",
  "170": "'As for those who firmly abide by the Scripture and establish prayer—surely We never discount the reward of those acting righteously.' (Quran 7:170). The other kind of heir.\n\n'Those who firmly abide by the Scripture' — alladhina yumassikuna bil-kitab. Those who take hold of it and keep hold.\n\nThe verb is from the same root as the instruction Musa received at 7:145: 'Hold to this firmly.' What was commanded there is described here as a practice.\n\nSet this verse against 7:169 and the difference is entirely in the grip. Both groups have the Book. One inherited it and used it as cover; the other holds on to it.\n\n'And establish prayer' — wa aqamu al-salah. Only one practice is named, and this is the first time in the surah that prayer is mentioned by name.\n\nThat placement is not accidental. Prayer is what a person does when nobody is buying anything — the opposite of the 'cheap offering' in the previous verse.\n\n'We never discount the reward of those acting righteously' — inna la nudi'u ajra al-muslihin.\n\n'Al-muslihin' — the repairers, from 'islah'. The word of 7:35, where the promise went to whoever 'shuns evil and mends their ways', of 7:56, where corruption was forbidden 'after it has been set in order', and of 7:142, where Harun was told 'do what is right.'\n\nThe Quran keeps naming the same category, and it is not the category of people who avoid wrong. It is people who fix things.\n\n'We never discount' — nothing is written off, misplaced or forgotten.",
  "171": "'And ˹remember˺ when We raised the mountain over them as if it were a cloud and they thought it would fall on them. ˹We said,˺ \"Hold firmly to that ˹Scripture˺ which We have given you and observe its teachings so perhaps you will become mindful ˹of Allah˺.\"' (Quran 7:171). The covenant, taken under a mountain.\n\n'We raised the mountain over them' — nataqna al-jabala fawqahum. Al-Baqarah records the same at 2:63 and 2:93, and an-Nisa at 4:154.\n\n'As if it were a cloud' — ka annahu zullah. Like a canopy hanging above them.\n\n'And they thought it would fall on them' — wa zannu annahu waqi'un bihim.\n\nNotice what this scene is. A community is given the Book with the most vivid possible demonstration of who is giving it.\n\n'Hold firmly to that Scripture which We have given you' — khudhu ma ataynakum bi quwwah. Word for word what Musa was told at 7:145, and what the faithful of 7:170 are described as doing.\n\nThe instruction appears three times in this section of the surah, which tells you it is the point.\n\n'So perhaps you will become mindful' — la'allakum tattaqun.\n\nThere is an obvious question here, and the surah has already answered it. If a mountain overhead does not produce lasting obedience, nothing external will.\n\nVerse 7:132 recorded Egypt saying: 'No matter what sign you may bring, we will never believe in you.' Verse 7:146 said the same of those turned from the signs.\n\nAwe does not convert anybody by itself. It only makes the moment unforgettable, which is why the next verse goes back further than any mountain.",
  "172": "'And ˹remember˺ when your Lord brought forth from the loins of the children of Adam their descendants and had them testify regarding themselves. ˹Allah asked,˺ \"Am I not your Lord?\" They replied, \"Yes, You are! We testify.\"' (Quran 7:172). The oldest agreement there is.\n\n'Brought forth from the loins of the children of Adam their descendants' — min zuhurihim dhurriyyatahum. Every human being who would ever exist.\n\n'Am I not your Lord?' — a lastu bi rabbikum. Four words in Arabic.\n\n'Yes, You are! We testify' — bala shahidna. The answer is unanimous. No one is recorded dissenting.\n\nThis is the covenant referred to at 7:102: 'We did not find most of them true to their covenant.' The surah waited seventy verses to explain what was broken.\n\n'Now you have no right to say on Judgment Day, \"We were not aware of this\"' — an taqulu yawma al-qiyamati inna kunna 'an hadha ghafilin.\n\nThat is the purpose of the whole scene: to close the defence of ignorance.\n\nHow is it binding if nobody remembers it? Because the Quran does not treat it as a memory. It treats it as something built in.\n\nThe Prophet, peace be upon him, quoted his Lord saying: 'I have created My servants as one having a natural inclination to the worship of Allah but it is Satan who turns them away' (Sahih Muslim 2865a). Surah ar-Rum calls it 'the natural Way of Allah which He has instilled in all people' (Quran 30:30).\n\nThe mountain of 7:171 was above them. This is underneath everyone.",
  "173": "'Nor say, \"It was our forefathers who had associated others ˹with Allah in worship˺ and we, as their descendants, followed in their footsteps. Will you then destroy us for the falsehood they invented?\"' (Quran 7:173). The second excuse, closed.\n\nVerse 7:172 removed the plea of ignorance. This removes the plea of inheritance.\n\n'It was our forefathers who had associated others' — innama ashraka aba'una min qabl. They started it.\n\nThis surah has recorded that defence three times already, always in the mouths of people about to be destroyed. At 7:28: 'We found our forefathers doing it.' At 7:70: 'abandon what our forefathers used to worship?' At 7:95: 'Our forefathers too had been visited by adversity and prosperity.'\n\n'And we, as their descendants, followed in their footsteps' — dhurriyyatan min ba'dihim. We came after.\n\n'Will you then destroy us for the falsehood they invented?' — the question sounds fair, and that is exactly why the Quran places it here rather than dismissing it in passing.\n\nThe answer is 7:172. If the acknowledgement was taken from every descendant individually, then nobody is standing on their father's testimony.\n\nThe covenant is personal. So is the account.\n\nAl-An'am gave the practical version of the same argument at 6:104: 'whoever chooses to see, it is for their own good. But whoever chooses to be blind, it is to their own loss.'\n\nCulture explains a great deal about what a person believes. It does not transfer the responsibility for checking.",
  "174": "'This is how We make our signs clear, so perhaps they will return ˹to the Right Path˺.' (Quran 7:174). A short closing line, and a familiar one.\n\n'This is how We make our signs clear' — wa kadhalika nufassilu al-ayat. 'Tafsil' — laying them out in detail, item by item.\n\nThe word appeared at 7:52 of the Book itself, 'which We explained with knowledge', at 7:133 of the plagues sent to Egypt, and at 7:145 of the Tablets.\n\n'So perhaps they will return' — la'allahum yarji'un. And the same phrase closed the Musa narrative at 7:168.\n\nNotice how consistently this surah states its purpose. Every major section ends with 'perhaps': perhaps you will be mindful at 7:26, perhaps they would be humbled at 7:94, perhaps they may abstain at 7:164.\n\nNot one of them says 'so that they will'. The Quran describes what it is offering, not what it will force.\n\n'Return' — 'ruju'', going back to where one came from. And the previous two verses have just explained where that is: 'Am I not your Lord?' — 'Yes, You are.'\n\nA person is not being asked to travel somewhere new. He is being asked to return to a position he already holds.\n\nThat reframes the entire argument of the surah. Every prophet from 7:59 onwards said the same sentence — 'Worship Allah; you have no other god except Him' — and none of them was introducing an idea.\n\nThey were reminding people of the answer they had already given.",
  "175": "'And relate to them ˹O Prophet˺ the story of the one to whom We gave Our signs, but he abandoned them, so Satan took hold of him, and he became a deviant.' (Quran 7:175). One man, unnamed.\n\n'The one to whom We gave Our signs' — alladhi ataynahu ayatina. He was given them.\n\nThe early commentators discuss who this was, and the reports differ. Ibn Kathir, commenting on this verse, records several identifications from the early authorities and also the view of Ibn Mas'ud and others that it is a general description.\n\nThe Quran leaves the name out, and that silence is useful. Naming him would turn the verse into a piece of history; leaving him anonymous keeps it a warning.\n\n'But he abandoned them' — fansalakha minha. He slipped out of them.\n\nThat verb is used of a snake shedding its skin, and of night withdrawing from day. Something that was wrapped around him comes off.\n\nNotice that the signs are not taken from him. He steps out of them.\n\n'So Satan took hold of him' — fa atba'ahu al-shaytan. The enemy followed him, caught up with him.\n\nRecall 7:16: 'I will lie in ambush for them on Your Straight Path.' A man walking off the path walks towards the one waiting beside it.\n\n'And he became a deviant' — mina al-ghawin. Of the lost.\n\nThe most sobering detail is the starting position. This is not a story about someone who never received anything. It is about someone who had the signs and let them go.",
  "176": "'If We had willed, We would have elevated him with Our signs, but he clung to this life—following his evil desires. His example is that of a dog: if you chase it away, it pants, and if you leave it, it ˹still˺ pants...' (Quran 7:176). The comparison.\n\n'He clung to this life' — akhlada ila al-ard. He sank towards the earth, made it his permanent home.\n\n'Following his evil desires' — wattaba'a hawah. And there is the diagnosis: 'hawa' — desire, inclination, whatever the self leans towards.\n\nSurah al-Furqan puts it starkly at 25:43: 'the one who has taken their own desires as their god.'\n\n'His example is that of a dog: if you chase it away, it pants, and if you leave it, it still pants' — the panting does not depend on the treatment.\n\nThat is the precision of the image. An animal that pants when driven off and pants when left alone is not responding to anything outside itself. Its condition is internal.\n\nSo it is with a man ruled by 'hawa'. Warned or unwarned, given signs or denied them, the state is the same, because the driver is inside.\n\nRecall 7:146: 'even if they were to see every sign, they still would not believe.' This verse explains the mechanism behind that.\n\n'So narrate to them stories of the past, so perhaps they will reflect' — faqsusi al-qasas la'allahum yatafakkarun.\n\n'Abdullah ibn 'Amr reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'Convey my teachings to the people even if it were a single sentence, and tell others the stories of Bani Israel, for it is not sinful to do so' (Sahih al-Bukhari 3461).\n\nThe remedy prescribed for a self-driven heart is a story about somebody else.",
  "177": "'What an evil example of those who denied Our signs! They ˹only˺ wronged their own souls.' (Quran 7:177). The verdict on the man of the previous two verses.\n\n'What an evil example' — sa'a mathalan. A bad likeness.\n\n'They only wronged their own souls' — wa anfusahum kanu yazlimun.\n\nThat clause has appeared before in this surah, word for word, at 7:160: 'They did not wrong Us, but wronged themselves.'\n\nThe repetition is the closing argument of the whole chapter on this point. Every rejection recorded here — Iblis at 7:12, 'Ad at 7:70, Thamud at 7:77, Pharaoh at 7:132, the Sabbath-breakers at 7:166, and now this man — is damage the doer takes.\n\nAl-An'am said it at 6:12 and 6:26. Adam admitted it about himself at 7:23: 'We have wronged ourselves.'\n\nAnd that admission is the difference. The same sentence, said by a person about himself in time, is repentance. Said about him afterwards, it is the verdict.\n\n'Those who denied Our signs' — the plural returns. The individual case of 7:175 and 7:176 is now generalised.\n\nNotice how the surah handles the unnamed man. It tells his story, draws the comparison, delivers the judgement in ten words, and moves on.\n\nNo gloating, no elaboration. The same restraint shown at 7:41 with the bedding of fire, and at 7:78 with the fall of Thamud.\n\nThe point was never the punishment. It was the warning available beforehand.",
  "178": "'Whoever Allah guides is truly guided. And whoever He leaves to stray, they are the ˹true˺ losers.' (Quran 7:178). The matter, reduced to one line.\n\n'Whoever Allah guides is truly guided' — man yahdi Allahu fa huwa al-muhtad. There is no second source.\n\n'And whoever He leaves to stray, they are the true losers' — fa ula'ika humu al-khasirun. 'Al-khasirun' — the losers, for the last time in a long series: 7:9, 7:23, 7:53, 7:90, 7:92, 7:99, 7:149.\n\nThis verse can be read as fatalism, and this surah has spent 177 verses making sure it is not read that way.\n\nSet it beside 7:176, immediately before it: the man 'clung to this life — following his evil desires.' That is described as his doing.\n\nSet it beside 7:146: the turning away follows 'because they denied Our signs.' Beside 7:101: 'they would not believe in what they had already denied.' Beside 7:100: the sealing comes after the sins.\n\nThe order is consistent throughout. The refusal comes first; the abandonment confirms it.\n\nWhat the verse removes is the idea that anyone guides himself. The people of Paradise said it at 7:43: 'We would have never been guided if Allah had not guided us.'\n\nSo the two halves of the chapter's teaching stand together. Nobody arrives by his own effort, and nobody is lost except by his own choice.\n\nBoth are stated plainly, and neither is explained away by the other.",
  "179": "'Indeed, We have destined many jinn and humans for Hell. They have hearts they do not understand with, eyes they do not see with, and ears they do not hear with. They are like cattle. In fact, they are even less guided! Such ˹people˺ are ˹entirely˺ heedless.' (Quran 7:179). The reason, in terms of equipment.\n\n'Hearts they do not understand with, eyes they do not see with, ears they do not hear with' — three faculties, each named with the function it is not performing.\n\nNotice that nothing is described as missing. The hearts exist. The eyes work. The ears hear sound.\n\nThat is the whole indictment. This is not a verse about disability; it is about disuse.\n\nAl-An'am described the same at 6:25: 'We have cast veils over their hearts — leaving them unable to comprehend it — and deafness in their ears.'\n\n'They are like cattle. In fact, they are even less guided' — bal hum adall. Surah al-Furqan has the identical comparison at 25:44.\n\nWhy less? Because cattle use what they were given exactly as intended. An animal that hears a danger runs. Nothing in it is wasted.\n\nA human being with a working heart who will not think with it has fallen below the standard of an animal, not because he is worse by nature, but because he was given more.\n\n'Such people are entirely heedless' — al-ghafilun. Heedless — the charge laid against Pharaoh's people at 7:136 and warned against at the very end of this surah, in 7:205.\n\nThe three faculties named here are the three the Quran keeps appealing to. It asks people to look at ruins, listen to a recitation, and understand a comparison.",
  "180": "'Allah has the Most Beautiful Names. So call upon Him by them, and keep away from those who abuse His Names. They will be punished for what they used to do.' (Quran 7:180). After the closed hearts, an instruction for open ones.\n\n'Allah has the Most Beautiful Names' — wa lillahi al-asma'u al-husna. Surah al-Isra says it at 17:110, Ta-Ha at 20:8, and al-Hashr at 59:24.\n\n'So call upon Him by them' — fad'uhu biha. Use them when you ask.\n\nThat is a practical instruction, and it is the completion of 7:55: 'Call upon your Lord humbly and secretly.' Here you are told what to say.\n\nAbu Hurayrah reported the Prophet, peace be upon him, saying: 'Allah has ninety-nine names, one hundred minus one, and whoever knows them will go to Paradise' (Sahih al-Bukhari 2736).\n\nThe names have appeared throughout this surah, always at the moment they were needed. 'The best of forgivers' at 7:155. 'The Most Merciful of the merciful' at 7:151. 'All-Forgiving, Most Merciful' at 7:153 and 7:167.\n\n'And keep away from those who abuse His Names' — yulhiduna fi asma'ih. 'Ilhad' is deviation, bending something away from straight.\n\nThe early commentators explain this as distorting the names — denying them, applying them to created things, or inventing names never given.\n\nSet the two halves together. The names are the way in, and the misuse of them is the way out.\n\nNotice how the surah answers 7:179. Those people had hearts that did not understand. This verse hands the reader something specific to do with his.",
  "181": "'And among those We created is a group that guides with the truth and establishes justice accordingly.' (Quran 7:181). A verse that has already appeared in this surah.\n\nCompare 7:159: 'There are some among the people of Moses who guide with the truth and establish justice accordingly.'\n\nThe same sentence, with the subject widened. There it was said of one nation; here it is said of creation.\n\nThat widening is the point. Whatever the failures catalogued in this chapter — five destroyed nations, an empire, a community under a mountain, an unnamed man who shed his signs — there is always this group.\n\n'A group' — ummah. Not a majority, and the surah has been honest about that from 7:17 and 7:102 onwards. But never nobody.\n\n'That guides with the truth' — yahduna bil-haqq. They guide others by it, not merely follow it themselves.\n\n'And establishes justice accordingly' — wa bihi ya'dilun. And judge by it.\n\nThe two verbs are the answer to the two failures the surah keeps recording: people who will not accept guidance, and people who deal unjustly — Midian's scales at 7:85, the chiefs' pressure at 7:75, Pharaoh's decrees at 7:127.\n\nPlaced here, after 7:179 described hearts that do not understand, the verse is a deliberate corrective. The picture is not uniform.\n\nThere is something in this for any reader who feels outnumbered. The Quran states the existence of this group as a fact about creation, not as an aspiration.",
  "182": "'As for those who deny Our signs, We will gradually draw them to destruction in ways they cannot comprehend.' (Quran 7:182). The mechanism named.\n\n'We will gradually draw them to destruction' — sanastadrijuhum. From 'istidraj' — to bring someone down step by step, one rung at a time.\n\n'In ways they cannot comprehend' — min haythu la ya'lamun. From a direction they do not know about.\n\nThis is the process the surah described at 7:95, without naming it: 'Then We changed their adversity to prosperity until they flourished' — and then the seizure.\n\nAl-An'am gave the same account at 6:44: 'We showered them with everything they desired. But just as they became prideful of what they were given, We seized them by surprise.'\n\nSurah al-Qalam uses the identical word at 68:44: 'We will gradually draw them to destruction in ways they cannot comprehend.'\n\nWhat makes 'istidraj' frightening is that every individual step feels like a blessing. Nothing in the experience registers as a warning, which is precisely what 'min haythu la ya'lamun' means.\n\nSurah Al 'Imran states the principle at 3:178: 'Those who disbelieve should not think that living longer is good for them. They are only given more time to increase in sin.'\n\nSet this beside 7:99: 'None would feel secure from Allah's planning except the losers.' The security is the symptom of the process.\n\nAnd beside the enemy's own tactic at 7:16, waiting on the straight road rather than the crooked one.\n\nThe rungs go downward and feel like going up.",
  "183": "“I ˹only˺ delay their end for a while, but My planning is flawless” continues the explanation of gradual drawing, clarifying that the delay is intentional and the plan is perfect. The previous verse said as for those who deny Our signs, We will gradually draw them in ways they cannot comprehend. Now Allah says, “I only delay their end for a while,” or “wa umlee lahum,” I give them rope, respite, extension, as earlier Pharaoh’s people had famine then removal until a term they must reach then broke, and as Iblis was given delay until the appointed Day. The word “umlee” means to extend, to grant time, the same respite that earlier seemed like prosperity until they flourished and were seized while unaware.\n\nThe second clause, “but My planning is flawless,” or “inna kaidi mateen,” uses “kaid,” planning, scheme, stratagem, the same term used for Allah’s planning that none feel secure from except losers, and for the scheming of the wicked whose large numbers and arrogance are of no use. “Mateen” means firm, strong, unbreakable, not flimsy like the deceivers’ elegant words that are devoured. The contrast is deliberate: their makr is decorated speech that inclines hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter and then fails, while His kaid is the encompassing knowledge that was never absent, that knows what is in land and sea, that writes not a leaf falls without a record, that makes the weighing just.\n\nThis verse links to the earlier “We will gradually draw them” and to “Had the people of those societies been faithful We would have opened blessings from heaven and earth, but they disbelieved so We seized them.” The delay is not abandonment but flawless arrangement where each community’s appointed term is kept, where the fertile and infertile are both given rain before harvest is assessed, and where the Book explained with knowledge remains as guide and mercy for those who believe.\n\nA common error treats delay as weakness or forgetfulness. The Quran says delay is mateen, firm, with purpose: to let the warners warned so as to be excused, to let perhaps they may abstain remain possible, and to let the guiding ummah that establishes justice continue. For the living, the lesson is to use the umlah, respite, not as license to claim “We will be forgiven” while seizing again, but as time to hold firmly to the Scripture raised over like a cloud, to observe its teachings so perhaps you will become mindful, before the flawless plan reaches its term that can neither be delayed nor advanced.",
  "184": "“Have they not ever given it a thought? Their fellow man is not insane. He is only sent with a clear warning” turns the critique from the deniers’ being gradually drawn to their accusation against the warner himself, as the previous verses described the flawless plan that delays the wicked. The question, “Have they not ever given it a thought?” or “awa lam yatafakkaroo,” asks about thinking, or “tafakkur,” the reflecting that stories are narrated so perhaps they will reflect, and that fertile production requires thankful variation. The subject of thought is the messenger: “Their fellow man is not insane,” or “ma bi-sahibihim min jinnah.” “Sahib” means companion, fellow, one among them, like Moses as brother, Hud as brother, the unlettered Prophet from among yourselves, not a stranger. “Jinnah” is madness, possession by jinn, the same slur the chiefs used when they called Hud a fool and Noah misguided and the Meccan chiefs called Muhammad poet and magician. The Quran says their companion is not mad; his behaviour shows the opposite of madness: he conveys Lord’s messages with sincere advice, knows from Allah what they do not, commands uprightness and sincere devotion, and asks no reward.\n\nThe correction, “He is only sent with a clear warning,” or “in huwa illa natheerun mubeen,” defines the role as “natheer,” warner, the same role given to every prophet and to the Book sent down to warn the disbelievers and remind the believers. “Mubeen” clear, manifest, as the staff was a real snake, the hand shining white, the she-camel not to be harmed, the gate’s “Hittah” to be said humbly. The warner’s clarity is the sign that arrogance turns away from even if they see every sign, preferring the crooked path.\n\nThis verse links to the next invitation to reflect on wonders of heavens and earth and that perhaps their end is near, showing madness accusation is a substitute for that reflection, as earlier the disbelieving chiefs saw the messenger as misguided while themselves blind.\n\nA common misunderstanding equates intensity of warning with insanity. The Quran shows the warner’s intensity is mercy, like winds ushering rain, and his companionship is the most intimate proof he seeks their good. For successors who inherited Scripture and say “We will be forgiven” while taking gain, diagnosing the warner as insane is the crooked path that keeps hearts sealed; diagnosing him as clear warner opens the chest to Islam, where calling humbly and secretly with hope and fear keeps mercy close to good-doers before the hour that only the Lord knows arrives by surprise.",
  "185": "“Have they ever reflected on the wonders of the heavens and the earth, and everything Allah has created, and that perhaps their end is near? So what message after this ˹Quran˺ would they believe in?” expands the invitation to think from the warner to the world, after asking whether their fellow is insane and affirming he is a clear warner. The question, “Have they ever reflected on the wonders of the heavens and the earth,” or “awa lam yanzuroo fi malakooti al-samawati wal-ardi,” uses “malakoot,” the kingdom, dominion behind heavens and earth shown to Abraham so he would be certain, the same heavens and earth created in six Days, Throne, overlapping night and day, subjected sun, moon, and stars, winds with heavy clouds producing every fruit. This reflection is the fertile production that earlier hardly produced in the infertile; it is the looking that earlier communities refused when they worshipped a lowing calf that neither speaks nor guides.\n\nThe second object, “and everything Allah has created,” generalizes beyond sky to every leaf whose fall is written and every spring among twelve for each tribe, encompassing the Book explained with knowledge as well. The third reflection, “and that perhaps their end is near?” or “wa an asa an yakoona qad iqtaraba ajaluhum,” uses “ajal,” the appointed term for each community that when it comes cannot be delayed or advanced, that seized societies at night while asleep or day while at play, that made Midian as if never there, that drowned Pharaoh after famine and plagues. The term’s nearness is uncertain to humans but certain to the All-Hearing, All-Knowing who was never absent, making perhaps near a call to urgency, like “You will soon know who will fare best in the end.”\n\nThe closing challenge, “So what message after this Quran would they believe in?” or “fa-bi-ayyi hadeethin ba‘dahu yu’minoonoon,” says if the Quran that is the Fundamentals of everything explained, that holds the best of the Tablets, that is the light relieving burdens, does not move them, what other hadeeth, discourse, will? This echoes “This is how We make our signs clear so perhaps they will return” and “Have they not reflected on wonders,” showing reflection is the antidote to sport.\n\nA common illusion is that wonders are obvious and will always be there. The verse joins wonders above, around, and the nearness of end to provoke mindful urgency before the barrier and the weighing, inviting the heedless who are like cattle, even less guided, to use hearts to understand, eyes to see, ears to hear, before the Gradual drawing while unaware reaches its term.",
  "186": "“Whoever Allah allows to stray, none can guide, leaving them to wander blindly in their defiance” gives the immutable law after two reflection questions about the warner’s sanity and the heavens and earth’s wonders with the near term. The previous verse asked what message after the Quran they would believe, showing that without reflection even wonders do not guide. This verse states the outcome: “Whoever Allah allows to stray, none can guide,” or “man yudlil Allahu fa-la hadiya lahu.” The same law appeared as “Whoever Allah guides is truly guided, and whoever He leaves to stray are the true losers,” and as “We will gradually draw those who deny Our signs.” To be left to stray, or “yudlil,” is to be abandoned to the crooked path chosen when one acts arrogantly without right, is turned away from signs even if seeing every sign, sees the right path and does not take it but sees the crooked and follows it, denies and is heedless, as the rebellious whose home will be shown.\n\nThe consequence, “leaving them to wander blindly in their defiance,” or “wa yatharuhum fi tughyanihim ya‘mahoona,” uses “tughyan,” exceeding bounds, transgression, like spreading corruption after order, taking the calf as god, breaking the Sabbath with fish tricks, claiming “We will be forgiven” while seizing. “Ya‘mahoona” is to wander blindly, to be confused, like the disoriented by devils in the wilderness whom companions call “Come to us,” and like those whose hearts and eyes are turned as they refused to believe at first, leaving them to wander. This wandering is the opposite of the guided ummah that guides with truth and establishes justice, and of those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer.\n\nThis verse links the gradual drawing and flawless planning to personal responsibility: the drawing is of those who deny signs and whose hearts, eyes, and ears do not understand, see, or hear, so the straying is both divine leaving and self-chosen.\n\nA common misunderstanding reads this as arbitrary. The Quran’s sequence shows the leaving follows the chain: denying signs and meeting, making deeds vain, acting arrogantly without right, preferring crooked, being heedless. For the successor who still has the Most Beautiful Names to call upon and the Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy, the remedy is to recall the primordial “Am I not your Lord? Yes, we testify,” to hold firmly to the Scripture under the raised mountain, and to call humbly and secretly with hope and fear, lest the wander become permanent and the dog’s panting become the final example.",
  "187": "“They ask you ˹O Prophet˺ regarding the Hour, ‘When will it be?’ Say, ‘That knowledge is only with my Lord. He alone will reveal it when the time comes. It is too tremendous for the heavens and the earth and will only take you by surprise.’ They ask you as if you had full knowledge of it. Say, ‘That knowledge is only with Allah, but most people do not know’” addresses the same fixation on timing that earlier communities used to challenge prophets, now focused on the Hour itself. The previous verse warned that whoever Allah allows to stray will wander blindly in defiance after being gradually drawn. Now the question about the Hour, or “as-Sa‘ah,” is quoted, “When will it be?” or “ayyana mursaha,” when will it anchor, when will it occur. This was the same “When will the Hour be if you are truthful?” asked to hasten punishment, like Pharaoh’s “Bring what you threaten” and the Meccan “Bring the torment” and the modern “If this Quran is truth let the sky fall.”\n\nThe commanded answer repeats the earlier creed that signs are only with Allah and that creation and command belong to Him. Twice it is said, “That knowledge is only with my Lord” and “That knowledge is only with Allah,” or “innama ilmuha inda Rabbi” and “innama ilmuha inda Allah.” The hour’s knowledge is one of the keys of the unseen, the same unseen whose keys are with Him alone as sunset to dawn is His to split. “He alone will reveal it when the time comes,” or “la yujalliha li-waqtiha illa huwa,” means He alone will manifest it at its time, like the mountain levelled when He appears, not a prophet’s estimate. The reason it cannot be known is then given: “It is too tremendous for the heavens and the earth and will only take you by surprise,” or “thaqulat fi al-samawati wal-ard la ta’teekum illa baghtah.” “Thaqulat” heavy, tremendous, too weighty for heavens and earth to bear easily; if its weight is heavy on creation, asking for its date as amusement is treating the tremendous as play, the same taking of faith as amusement for which the Fire’s water is forbidden. “Illa baghtah” only by surprise echoes every term that came at night while asleep or day while at play, and the surprise seizure while unaware after prosperity.\n\nThe Quran notes their false impression: “They ask you as if you had full knowledge of it,” or “ka-annaka hafiyyun anha,” as if you are eager, well-informed, intimate with its news, expecting the warner to be a soothsayer. The reply again, “That knowledge is only with Allah, but most people do not know,” mirrors “most of them did not know” when they blamed omens on Moses, and “most people do not know” after the Hour’s heaviness, showing ignorance is the root of the question itself.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats the Hour’s unknown timing as reason to postpone preparation. The verse teaches the opposite: because it is heavy and surprising, the near term, perhaps your term is near, should keep you reflecting on heavens and earth and calling humbly with hope and fear, before the gradual drawing while unaware ends in wandering.",
  "188": "“Say, ‘I have no power to benefit or protect myself, except by the Will of Allah. If I had known the unknown, I would have benefited myself enormously, and no harm would have ever touched me. I am only a warner and deliverer of good news for those who believe’” follows the Hour’s unknown timing with a personal disclaimer that defines prophethood. The previous verse said the Hour’s knowledge is only with the Lord and is too tremendous for heavens and earth. This verse shows the warner’s own limits before that tremendousness. The opening, “I have no power to benefit or protect myself, except by the Will of Allah,” or “qul la amliku li-nafsi naf‘an wa la darran illa ma sha’a Allah,” mirrors Adam’s parents’ dependence, the magicians’ return to the Lord, and Moses’ prayer for hasanah, all acknowledging that the One whose creation and command are His alone grants benefit and harm. A warner who cannot benefit himself without Allah’s will certainly cannot advance or delay the Hour for others.\n\nThe counterfactual, “If I had known the unknown, I would have benefited myself enormously, and no harm would have ever touched me,” or “wa law kuntu a‘lamu al-ghayba la-istakthartu min al-khair wa ma massaniya al-soo’,” clarifies the keys of the unseen are with Him alone. “Ghaib” is the unseen, the same unseen whose keys no one knows except Him, the Hour, the provision of children, the tomorrow’s rain. If the Prophet knew it, he would have amassed every good and avoided every harm, but his life shows both: victory at Badr and wound at Uhud, proving humanity. This is the same humanness as eating after the staff-snake and needing the mountain’s awe.\n\nThe role definition that follows, “I am only a warner and deliverer of good news for those who believe,” or “in ana illa natheerun wa basheerun li-qawmin yu’minoonoon,” repeats the messenger limits given since Noah’s “I am a messenger from the Lord of all worlds, conveying messages and giving sincere advice.” Warmer, or “natheer,” and bringer of good news, or “basheer,” are the two faces of the Book sent down to warn and remind, of the winds that usher mercy and the plagues that are clear signs.\n\nA common error expects a prophet to be angelic controller of fate or economist of the unseen who can time the Hour. The verse corrects that expectation, aligning with “Call upon your Lord humbly and secretly, He does not like transgressors” and “He has the Most Beautiful Names, so call upon Him by them.” The warner’s humanity makes his warning more merciful, not less, inviting listeners to reflect on wonders and nearness of term, to follow the unlettered Prophet who believes in Allah and His words, and to prepare with prayer and firm abiding before harm touches.",
  "189": "“He is the One Who created you from a single soul, then from it made its spouse so he may find comfort in her. After he had been united with her, she carried a light burden that developed gradually. When it grew heavy, they prayed to Allah, their Lord, ‘If you grant us good offspring, we will certainly be grateful’” returns to the origin of humanity after defining the warner’s limits. The previous verses left the Hour as unknown and the warner as human; now the Quran roots humanity in a single soul and in pairhood, showing the Lord whose Hour is unknown is the same Lord who creates and gives life. The phrase “He is the One Who created you from a single soul, then from it made its spouse so he may find comfort in her,” or “huwa allathee khalaqakum min nafsin wahidatin wa ja‘ala minha zawjaha li-yaskuna ilaiha,” echoes “We have established you on earth” and “created you then shaped you” and “from a single soul, then assigned a place to live,” emphasizing unity of origin that removes the ground for arrogance that said “I am better than he is from fire, him from clay.” The spouse, or “zawj,” made from the same soul, finds “sakan,” tranquility, covering, as clothing covers nakedness and night is made for rest.\n\nThe next scene is intimate: “After he had been united with her, she carried a light burden that developed gradually,” or “fa-lamma taghashshaha hamalat hamlan khafeefan fa-marrat bihi.” “Taghashsha” covering as a garment, “hamlan khafeefan,” light burden, early pregnancy not yet heavy, that passes, or “marrat bihi,” she continues, moves lightly. “When it grew heavy, they prayed to Allah, their Lord, ‘If you grant us good offspring, we will certainly be grateful,’” or “fa-lamma athqalat da‘awaa Allaha Rabbahuma la-in ataitana salihan la-nakoonanna min al-shakireen.” “Athqalat,” heavy, near term, both prayed together, the same joint prayer Adam and his wife prayed after the tree, “If You do not forgive and have mercy we will be losers.” “Salehan” here means upright, sound, righteous child, body and faith, and “shakireen,” grateful, the fertile soil that produces abundantly and for whom lessons are varied.\n\nThis origin story links to the next verse where, when good offspring is granted, some associate false gods in what He gave, forgetting the prayer. It also links to the earlier mountain covenant and the primordial “Am I not your Lord?” where all testified “Yes, we testify,” showing gratitude was the natural vow before birth. A common misunderstanding treats family creation as secular. The Quran frames pair and pregnancy as signs among wonders of heavens and earth to reflect upon, whose near term may be the child’s own term, inviting thankfulness that dresses for worship and eats without waste, rather than later association that makes deeds vain like the dog’s panting and the calf’s lowing that neither speaks nor guides.",
  "190": "“But when He granted their descendants good offspring, they associated false gods in what He has given them. Exalted is Allah above what they associate ˹with Him˺!” continues the pair’s story from a grateful prayer to a subsequent association, showing how quickly the fertile vow can turn infertile. The previous verse described the light burden growing heavy and the joint supplication “If You grant us good offspring we will be certainly grateful,” a vow made in the vulnerability of pregnancy that mirrors the call “He alone you call upon humbly openly and secretly, If You rescue us from this we will be ever grateful” at sea. Now the outcome, “But when He granted their descendants good offspring, they associated false gods in what He has given them,” or “fa-lamma atahuma salihan ja‘ala lahu shurakaa’a fima atahuma,” records that after the gift of a sound, righteous child, some among their descendants, or broader humanity, “ja‘ala lahu shurakaa’,” make for Him partners in what He gave, naming children with names that serve others, dedicating them to others, or believing others granted the child, like the she-camel made a partner’s share unfairly or the calf taken as god after the sea. The phrase “in what He has given them” emphasizes the very gift prayed for becomes the occasion for ungratefulness, as earlier “Is it not clear to those who take over land” and “We tested with prosperity and adversity so perhaps they would return” showed prosperity itself is a test.\n\nThe exclamation, “Exalted is Allah above what they associate with Him!” or “fa-ta‘ala Allahu amma yushrikoon,” is the same glorification uttered after they associated jinn though He created them and falsely attributed sons and daughters out of ignorance, and after “Exalted is He above what they claim.” “Ta‘ala” high, far above, like the Throne established after creation in six Days, the One who makes night and day overlap and subjects sun, moon, stars, whose creation and command belong to Him alone.\n\nThis verse also warns against the common practice of naming children “servant of” other than Allah or attributing a healthy child to a shrine, a spirit, or a star, the same attribution without sultan that invented cattle taboos and mountain carvings. The remedy taught throughout is to remain among those who call humbly and secretly with hope and fear, who firm abide by Scripture and establish prayer, and who remember “We have certainly brought them a Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy,” so gratitude for good offspring remains gratitude to the One who created the single soul and its spouse for tranquility, not to a partner whose idols will soon be shown to have no feet to walk, hands to hold, eyes to see, or ears to hear.",
  "191": "“Do they associate ˹with Allah˺ those ˹idols˺ which cannot create anything, but are in fact created” begins the systematic dismantling of the association just condemned as exalted above. After saying when good offspring was granted they associated in what He gave and that Allah is exalted above that, the Quran now asks about the nature of the associates. The question, “Do they associate those idols which cannot create anything, but are in fact created,” or “a-yushrikoona ma la yakhluqu shay’an wa hum yukhlaqoon,” contrasts inability to create, “ma la yakhluqu shay’an,” anything at all, not a leaf, not a spring among twelve, not a wind that ushers mercy, with being themselves created, “wa hum yukhlaqoon,” fashioned by human hands, carved from stone, cast from golden jewellery into a lowing calf that neither speaks nor guides. The One who created you from a single soul and made earth in six Days and subjected celestial bodies and sends heavy clouds to lifeless land producing every fruit is the Creator, “who created the heavens and earth in six Days” and “who created all things,” while idols are the inverts, possessed, needing creation.\n\nThis contrast echoes the earlier “How could He have children when He has no mate? He created all things and has perfect knowledge” and “That is Allah your Lord, Creator of all things, so worship Him alone, and He is Maintainer of everything,” and directly answers the successor who demanded “Make for us a god like theirs,” showing that like theirs means like the created, not like the Creator.\n\nThe rhetorical “Do they associate?” implies astonishment, as earlier “Do you find it astonishing that a reminder comes through one of your own,” that the mind should find association astonishing given the evidence of winds and fertile land. The next verses will continue, they cannot help them or even themselves, cannot respond, are created beings like yourselves, have no feet, hands, eyes, ears, and if called for guidance cannot hear and appear to face you but do not see.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats idols as metaphorical only. The Quran treats any thing taken as partner that cannot create yet is created, whether wood, stone, gold calf, or desire followed, as falling under this critique. For the heir who says “We will be forgiven” while seizing unlawful gains, the reminder is that the gain cannot create, the idol cannot create, only the Lord whose mercy encompasses everything and whose punishment is swift can create and command, so worship belongs to Him alone before the Hour that is too tremendous for heavens and earth takes by surprise.",
  "192": "“which cannot help them, or even help themselves?” continues the audit of the associates that cannot create and are themselves created. The previous verse established creative impotence; this verse establishes helplessness. The phrase “which cannot help them, or even help themselves,” or “wa la yastatee‘oona lahum nasran wa la anfusahum yansuroon,” says the idols are double-helpless: they cannot grant “nasr,” help, victory, rescue, to those who call upon them as guardians besides Him, nor can they provide it to themselves when harmed. The lowing calf could not speak or guide; the idol devotees’ gods after the sea could not provide; the associate-gods for whom crops were divided could not share or withhold; the fabricated shares did not help when famine, blood, or earthquake came. The same helplessness was seen when the staff devoured illusions, when the sea drowned Pharaoh while his gods remained silent, and when the Fire’s residents begged for water that the disbelievers’ provision could not give.\n\nThis double inability contrasts with the Lord who rescues from the darkest times on land and at sea, who rescues from every distress, who sends winds, who gives twelve springs, shade, manna, and quails, who writes on Tablets guidance and mercy for those who stand in awe, and who is the Guardian forgiving and most merciful to those who repent after evil. He helps them and Himself needs no help, as earlier He provides for all and is not in need of provision.\n\nThe verse also mirrors the earlier test of the Heights where tyrants’ large numbers and arrogance are of no use; idol helpers are of no use either, because the creation belongs to Him alone and the command belongs to Him. Their inability to help themselves proves they cannot help others; a helper who cannot self-preserve cannot preserve followers on the Day the weighing will be just and the barrier stands.\n\nA common error imagines that at least idols provide psychological comfort. The Quran shows comfort without help is self-deception, like the dog’s panting that persists whether chased or left, and like garments that neither cover nor adorn. The proper Protector is then named two verses later, “Indeed, my Protector is Allah Who has revealed this Book, for He alone protects the righteous,” the same Protector to call upon humbly and secretly with hope and fear, whose mercy is close to good-doers and whose most beautiful names are to be called upon while keeping away from those who abuse them.",
  "193": "“And if you ˹idolaters˺ call upon them for guidance, they cannot respond to you. It is all the same whether you call them or remain silent” deepens the helplessness audit from creating and helping to hearing and responding. The previous verses established the associates cannot create and cannot help themselves or others; this verse adds they cannot hear and answer. The condition, “And if you call upon them for guidance,” or “wa in tad‘oohum ila al-huda la yattabi‘ookum,” imagines idolaters sincerely seeking huda, the guidance that is the Book explained with knowledge, the straight path made clear to the mindful, the Tablets’ fundamentals and the light of the unlettered Prophet. The result, “they cannot respond to you,” or “la yattabi‘ookum,” they will not follow you, will not answer, just as earlier the calf could not speak or guide, and as later “Those idols you invoke besides Allah are created beings like yourselves, so call upon them and see if they will answer” challenges. The idols lack the hearing that was just praised as “He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing” and the “We were never absent” that makes a full account with sure knowledge, so they cannot be the guardians besides Him warned against.\n\nThe equivalence, “It is all the same whether you call them or remain silent,” or “sawaa’un alaikum a-da‘awtumoo hum am antum samitoon,” shows futility: calling with humble secret hope and calling not at all produce identical nothing, unlike calling upon your Lord humbly and secretly where mercy is close to good-doers and winds usher rain. The same equivalence was seen when the magicians’ great display stunned people but did not guide; stun without guidance is calling without answer.\n\nThis verse links directly to the later “If you call them to guidance, they cannot hear, and you may see them facing towards you but they cannot see,” extending deafness to blindness, and to the challenge “Do they have feet to walk with? hands to hold? eyes to see? ears to hear?” where the idols’ lack of organs is mocked.\n\nA common misunderstanding assigns subtle agency to idols, thinking silence is still presence. The Quran says silence is absence, and presence that cannot guide is not presence at all. For the heir who says “We will be forgiven” while seizing gain and the student of the calf that lows, the call is to redirect calling to the One whose creation and command are His, blessed Lord of all worlds, who gives life and causes death and who, through the unlettered Prophet, relieves burdens and shackles, so that following him becomes the following that is rightly guided, rather than calling upon the deaf.",
  "194": "“Those ˹idols˺ you invoke besides Allah are created beings like yourselves. So call upon them and see if they will answer you, if your claims are true!” issues the practical challenge after establishing that associates cannot create, cannot help, and cannot respond. The verse states humanity: “Those idols you invoke besides Allah are created beings like yourselves,” or “inna allatheena tad‘oona min dooni Allahi ibadun amthalukum.” “Ibad,” servants, beings like you, as earlier you were created from a single soul, established then shaped, given hearts, eyes, and ears, and as earlier the jinn were created and the mountain raised like a cloud, all are servants, not lords. Idols are servants doubly: they are created by humans and they are created by the Creator who created the humans who made them, the same chain that makes the calf a jasad with lowing and the Sabbath fish a test.\n\nThe word “amthalukum,” like you, removes the aura of mysterious superiority that makes the straight seem crooked in the eyes of those who take devils as masters thinking they are guided. The idol is not a hidden power watching from where you cannot see them; the devils do that, but the idol itself is a servant like you, standing among the twelve tribes who received springs but did not wrong Us but wronged themselves.\n\nThe challenge, “So call upon them and see if they will answer you, if your claims are true!” or “fa-id‘oohum fa-l-yastajeebu lakum in kuntum sadiqeen,” mirrors Moses’ challenge to Pharaoh, “If you have come with a sign bring it if you are truthful,” and Hud’s “Remember He made you successors,” and Shu‘ayb’s “give just measure.” It is an empirical test parallel to “See what was the end of the wicked,” “See how We vary lessons,” and “Have they reflected on the kingdom of heavens and earth.” If invocation is true, let the servant answer, as the All-Hearing answers the humble secret caller and as the winds answer the command to drive heavy clouds.\n\nThe verse prepares the limb-mockery next: feet, hands, eyes, ears, and the Prophet’s counter-challenge “Call upon your associate-gods and conspire against me without delay—my Protector is Allah Who revealed the Book.” A common error treats idols as necessary mediators to the Lord of all worlds. The verse says mediators who are servants like you cannot replace the Lord whose beautiful names are to be called upon directly, whose mercy is close to good-doers, and whose guidance via the unlettered Prophet relieves burdens. Testing the call now, while the term remains, shows silence before the silence of the Fire where residents cry for water and are refused.",
  "195": "“Do they have feet to walk with? Or hands to hold with? Or eyes to see with? Or ears to hear with? Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ ‘Call upon your associate-gods and conspire against me without delay!’” continues the empirical challenge, now dissecting the idols’ lack of faculties that were earlier said to be unused in many jinn and humans destined for Hell. After saying idols are servants like you, so call and see if they answer if you are truthful, the Quran asks four anatomical questions: “Do they have feet to walk with? Or hands to hold with? Or eyes to see with? Or ears to hear with?” or “a-lahum arjulun yamshoona biha am lahum aidin yabtishoona biha am lahum a‘yunun yubsiroona biha am lahum athanun yasma‘oona biha.” Each question expects “No” in the truest sense. They may have carved feet, hands, eyes, ears, but none function: feet do not walk to help, hands do not grasp to withhold or give, eyes do not see the mountain raised like a cloud or the sea split, ears do not hear the humble secret call with hope and fear. This mirrors the earlier anatomy of heedlessness, hearts not understanding, eyes not seeing, ears not hearing, who are like cattle, even less guided, entirely heedless, and contrasts with the Lord who is All-Hearing, All-Knowing, Most Subtle, All-Aware, never absent, whose knowledge encompasses everything and who hears the magicians’ prayer for perseverance.\n\nThe second half is the Prophet’s defiance: “Say, O Prophet, Call upon your associate-gods and conspire against me without delay!” or “qul id‘oo shuraka’akum thumma keedooni fa-la tunziroon.” This is the same defiance Hud showed, “Do you dispute regarding names without sultan? Then wait, I too am waiting,” and Shu‘ayb’s “in Allah we trust, our Lord, judge between us,” and Noah’s Ark against the flood, all trusting creation-and-command belong to Him. The challenge invites all shuraka, partners, to plot, or “keed,” scheme, the word for the flawless planning whose mateen belongs only to Allah, showing their kid is nothing.\n\nThe verse’s placement before “Indeed, my Protector is Allah Who revealed this Book, for He alone protects the righteous” explains the defiance’s basis: the Prophet is not protected by numbers and arrogance that are of no use, but by the Wali who protects the righteous, whose mercy is close to good-doers.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats these questions as mockery without purpose. The Quran’s purpose is diagnostic: idols are servants with decorative limbs, not living guardians, so taking them as guardians besides Him violates “Follow what was sent down and do not take other guardians,” and keeps the scale light. The living are invited to choose feet that walk to worship properly adorned, hands that give just measure, eyes that reflect on heavens and earth, ears that listen to the Quran attentively and silently so mercy may be shown.",
  "196": "“Indeed, my Protector is Allah Who has revealed this Book. For He ˹alone˺ protects the righteous” gives the answer to the defiance just issued, “Call upon your associate-gods and conspire against me without delay.” After inviting all idols to scheme and proving they lack feet, hands, eyes, ears, the Prophet declares his true reliance. The phrase “Indeed, my Protector is Allah Who has revealed this Book,” or “inna waliyyi Allahu allathee nazzala al-kitaba,” names the Wali, the protecting ally, guardian, the same Wali whose allies are not devils for those who disbelieve, the same Wali who was best forgiver and Guardian for Moses’ seventy, the same Wali whom no one should take besides Him. His qualification, “Who has revealed this Book,” ties protection to revelation, the Book explained with knowledge as guide and mercy, the Tablets’ fundamentals, the light sent down with the unlettered Prophet that relieves burdens. The Book is not an abstract text but the cord of protection, as the staff and the sea were protection for Moses.\n\nThe principle that follows, “For He alone protects the righteous,” or “wa huwa yatawalla al-saliheen,” generalizes. “Saliheen,” righteous, are those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, who guide with truth and establish justice, who give just measure, do not spread corruption, whose hearts understand, eyes see, ears hear, unlike cattle. Protection, or “yatawalla,” is to take as wali, to befriend, to manage affairs, the opposite of the dispersing of the heedless through land and sealing of arrogant hearts.\n\nThis declaration contrasts with the immediate context: the idolaters’ gods cannot protect them or themselves, cannot answer, are servants like you, have no functional limbs, while the Prophet’s Wali, who created you from a single soul, created heavens and earth in six Days, sends winds with heavy clouds producing every fruit, and afflicts and relieves, does protect, as He saved Noah in the Ark, Hud and Salih by mercy, Lot’s family except the doomed, Shu‘ayb’s saved, and the magicians who fell prostrate.\n\nA common error seeks protection in numbers, walls, or status, the same large numbers and arrogance said to be of no use. The verse re-centers protection on the One whose most beautiful names are to be called upon and whose knowledge encompasses everything, the same Lord who declared He would send against the heedless others who would make them suffer until the Day, yet is swift in punishment but certainly all-forgiving, most merciful to the righteous He protects when they call humbly and secretly with hope and fear.",
  "197": "“But those ˹false gods˺ you call besides Him can neither help you nor even themselves’” restates the helplessness audit after the declaration that the Protector is Allah who revealed the Book and protects the righteous. The same sentence appeared earlier as “which cannot help them, or even help themselves,” but now it is placed after the Prophet’s defiance, making the contrast between the two protectors explicit. The phrase “But those false gods you call besides Him can neither help you nor even themselves,” or “wa allatheena tad‘oona min doonihi la yastatee‘oona nasrakum wa la anfusahum yansuroon,” uses “dawna,” besides, indicating a guardian taken instead of Him, the very guardians the Book warned not to take. “Nasr,” help, is the same help Moses told his people to seek from Allah and be patient, whose earth belongs to Him and outcome to the righteous. The false gods provide neither help to callers nor to themselves, unlike the winds that usher mercy and the rain that produces every fruit and the Springs that gush for twelve tribes.\n\nThe repetition is not redundancy but emphasis, closing the circle that began with “Do they associate those idols which cannot create anything but are in fact created,” then “cannot help,” then “if you call for guidance they cannot respond,” then “servants like you, call and see if they answer,” then the limb questions, then the challenge to conspire, then the protector declaration, now the summary. Each repetition adds a layer: creation, self-help, hearing, answering, limbs, conspiracy, guardianship, now the final seal that the helpers are helpless.\n\nThe verse also subtly answers the reminder to the Children of Israel “Make for us a god like their gods,” showing that like theirs means like the helpless, while their rescue from dreadful torment, the split sea, and the twelve springs already demonstrated who truly helps.\n\nA common misunderstanding persists in thinking that at least idols do no harm, so calling them is harmless. The Quran shows calling them is harmful because it makes deeds vain, turns away from signs even if every sign is seen, and leads to the home of the rebellious being shown, to the weighing that is just where light scales doom selves, and to gates not opening. The alternative remains to call upon Him by His most beautiful names and to be among the group that guides with truth and establishes justice, whose Protector, who revealed the Book, is already close to good-doers who call with hope and fear and who will be among those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer.",
  "198": "“If you ˹idolaters˺ call them to guidance, they cannot hear. And you ˹O Prophet˺ may see them facing towards you, but they cannot see” returns to the sensory failure of idols, now applied to guidance specifically, after summarizing their double helplessness. The phrase “If you call them to guidance, they cannot hear,” or “wa in tad‘oohum ila al-huda la yasma‘oo,” repeats the earlier “if you call them to guidance they cannot respond,” but with “yasma‘oo,” hear, the faculty most needed for “Listen to the Quran attentively and be silent so you may be shown mercy,” which will be commanded two verses later. Idols lack the ears that earlier were said many jinn and humans possess but do not hear with, becoming like cattle, even less guided, entirely heedless. They cannot hear because they were not given hearing that functions, just as they have no feet to walk, hands to hold, eyes to see, ears to hear.\n\nThe second sentence adds a visual illusion: “And you may see them facing towards you, but they cannot see,” or “wa tarahum yanzuroona ilaika wa hum la yubsiroon.” “Yanzuroona,” looking, facing, gives the appearance of attention, like the magicians’ eyes-deceiving great magic that stunned, or the fish appearing abundantly on Sabbath to test. The idols seem to look, facing the worshipper, carved to appear watchful, yet “la yubsiroon,” they do not see, as earlier “no vision can encompass Him but He encompasses all vision,” the idols encompass nothing. The worshipper feels seen, but the gaze is vacant, the same vacant gaze that led the town by the sea to think tricks would hide Sabbath violation while He is never absent.\n\nThis double failure—deaf to guidance and blind though facing—explains why those turned away from signs for arrogance even if they see every sign will not believe, and why if they see the right path they will not take it but if they see a crooked path they will follow it, as earlier described. The human faculties of the worshippers themselves are also dulled by mirroring: calling to the deaf makes the caller deaf to the Quran’s recitation, a warning that leads directly to “When the Quran is recited, listen attentively and be silent.”\n\nA common error assumes that a deity’s image facing the devotee implies care. The Quran strips that illusion, recalling the calf that neither speaks nor guides despite being fashioned from jewellery, and recalling the winds that do hear the command to drive heavy clouds. The cure is to turn calling to the Most Beautiful Names of the One who does hear and who does see, whose mercy is close, and to remember that the righteous He protects are those who hear the Quran and see the wonders of heavens and earth and that perhaps their term is near, so what message after this Quran would they believe if not this.",
  "199": "“Be gracious, enjoin what is right, and turn away from those who act ignorantly” gives the prophetic ethic after the long exposure of idol helplessness, the protector declaration, and the deaf-blind diagnosis. The command is threefold, each part balancing the previous warnings not to take other guardians and not to spread corruption. First, “Be gracious,” or “khuth al-afwa,” take the easy, pardon what is within capacity, be lenient with people’s faults, as earlier “We never require of any soul more than what it can afford” and “hold firmly to the Scripture which We have given you” with strength but without harshness. “Afwa” is what is readily given without strain, the same ease that the Tayyibat are for enjoyment in this world without burden. Graciousness is the opposite of Pharaoh’s cutting hands and feet and crucifying, and of the chiefs’ “We will kill their sons” domination.\n\nSecond, “enjoin what is right,” or “wa’mur bil-‘urf,” command the known good, the ma‘roof that the unlettered Prophet commands and the evil he forbids, the uprightness and sincere devotion earlier commanded, the just measure, the covering of nakedness with best clothing, the remembrance under the raised mountain. “‘Urf” is what is recognized as good by sound fitrah and revelation combined, the guidance that varies for the thankful and produces abundantly, not the elegant deception that inclines hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter.\n\nThird, “and turn away from those who act ignorantly,” or “wa a‘rid an al-jahileen,” is the same turning away commanded for those who ridicule revelations unless they change topics, for leaving them and their deceit, for not sitting with mockers, and for Moses’ “you are the best of those who judge.” Ignorant here are not merely uninformed but those who act on ignorance, like those who demanded a god like theirs, who made the calf, who changed “Hittah” and who abused the most beautiful names.\n\nTogether the three create a community like the ummah that guides with truth and establishes justice among those We created, and the group among the People of Moses that does so. A common misunderstanding pits graciousness against commanding right, as if one must be harsh to enjoin. The verse joins them: graciousness makes enjoining attractive, turning away from the ignorant keeps graciousness from being exploited by those turned away from signs who even if they see every sign will not believe. This balance keeps the caller humbly, secretly hopeful yet fearful, while the winds still usher mercy and the term nears, so that when the Quran is recited two verses later, listening attentively and in silence is the natural response rather than the heedless panting of the dog that clings to earth.",
  "200": "“If you are tempted by Satan, then seek refuge with Allah. Surely He is All-Hearing, All-Knowing” follows the ethic of graciousness, enjoining right, and turning away from the ignorant with the necessary spiritual protection for that ethic. The previous verse set a demanding balance: to be gracious and to command good while turning from the ignorant who ridicule, change words, and abuse beautiful names. This verse anticipates the inner push that makes that balance wobble. The phrase “If you are tempted by Satan,” or “wa imma yanzaghannaka mina al-shaytani nazghun,” describes a prick, a touch, an incitement, the same nazgh that made forgetfulness about sitting with mockers and that drives the ambush on the Straight Path from front, back, right, and left. Satan’s soldiers who watch from where you cannot see them use such pricks to turn graciousness into resentment or enjoining into harshness.\n\nThe remedy, “then seek refuge with Allah,” or “fa-ista‘ith billah,” is to take shelter, like taking Allah as Protector who revealed the Book and protects the righteous, and like calling upon Him humbly and secretly with hope and fear. Seeking refuge is the practical form of remembering, or “tathakkur,” that fertile land needs to produce abundantly. The reason, “Surely He is All-Hearing, All-Knowing,” or “innahu Samee‘un Aleem,” repeats the pairing that after “To Him belongs whatever is in day and night and He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing,” and that guarantees no vision encompasses Him but He encompasses all vision, that We were never absent, and that the most beautiful names belong to Him. He hears the prick’s whisper and the refuge’s utterance, and He knows the temper that would make the caller transgress and thereby become unliked, since He does not like transgressors.\n\nThis verse links directly to the next, where those mindful, when Satan whispers, remember then they start to see clearly, while devils plunge their associates deeper sparing no effort. The heedless are like cattle, even less guided, while the mindful use refuge to restore sight.\n\nA common error believes willpower alone resists the ignorant’s provocation. The Quran teaches refuge is needed, a return to the Guardian who encompasses, to the Lord who subjects winds and writes not a leaf falls without record. Seeking it keeps the heart among those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, rather than among those turned away from signs for arrogance who see the crooked path and follow it. In daily practice, before responding to an ignorant act, the gracious, commanding, turning caller whispers “I seek refuge with Allah, the All-Hearing, All-Knowing,” and the ambush loses its foothold.",
  "201": "“Indeed, when Satan whispers to those mindful ˹of Allah˺, they remember ˹their Lord˺ then they start to see ˹things˺ clearly” describes the successful use of refuge just commanded, contrasting the heedless who are like cattle with the mindful whose hearts understand. The previous verse warned if Satan tempts you seek refuge with Allah, the All-Hearing, All-Knowing. This verse shows what happens when those who are mindful, or “muttaqoon,” the wearers of the best clothing of righteousness, the hopeful-fearful callers whose mercy is close, the thankful fertile soil, are touched. The phrase “when Satan whispers to those mindful, they remember,” or “inna allatheena ittaqaw itha massahum ta’ifun mina al-shaytani tathakkaroo,” uses “massahum ta’if,” a touch, a visitation, a circling, the lightest contact, not possession, showing even the mindful are touched by the ambush from four sides. “Tathakkaroo” they remember, the same remembrance commanded under the raised mountain, “observe its teachings so perhaps you will become mindful,” and after the winds producing every fruit so perhaps you will be mindful, and after the primordial testimony so perhaps they will return.\n\nThe result, “then they start to see things clearly,” or “fa-itha hum mubsiroon,” they become seeing, insightful, with “basirah,” inner sight, like the insight, or “basa’ir,” from your Lord that came as the Book explained with knowledge, guide and mercy. Clear sight is the opposite of the hearts, eyes, ears that do not understand, see, or hear, and of the idols that face you but do not see and are called to guidance but cannot hear. The mindful’s memory restores the faculty the cattle-like lack.\n\nThis verse links the ethic of being gracious, enjoining right, turning from the ignorant, with the practical need for refuge. The ambush that aims to make the gracious turn harsh and the commander turn transgressing is broken by a remembered Name, one of the Most Beautiful Names they are taught to call upon, like Al-Hafiz, the Guardian.\n\nA common misunderstanding expects the mindful to be never touched. The Quran says they are touched, but they remember. Another error expects remembrance without sight. The verse shows remembrance leads to sight, the same sight that makes the Heights people recognize by marks and that makes the magicians fall prostrate. For successors who inherited Scripture and are tempted to seize lowly gain claiming “We will be forgiven,” remembering the covenant not to say about Allah except truth restores sight before the gradual drawing in ways they cannot comprehend reaches its flawless plan, keeping the path perfectly straight visible while the crooked remains enticing but now seen clearly.",
  "202": "“But the devils persistently plunge their ˹human˺ associates deeper into wickedness, sparing no effort” gives the counterpart to the mindful remembering and then seeing clearly, showing what happens without that remembrance. The previous verse described the mindful touched by a visitation who remember and become insightful. This verse describes the associates of devils: “But the devils persistently plunge their human associates deeper into wickedness, sparing no effort,” or “wa ikhwanuhum yamuddoonahum fil-ghayyi thumma la yuqsiron.” “Ikhwanuhum,” their brothers, associates, those who took devils as masters instead of Allah thinking they are guided. “Yamuddoonahum fil-ghayyi,” they extend them in error, in the crooked path, or “ghayy,” the same ghayy that those turned away from signs choose when they see the right path they will not take but the crooked they will follow. “Thumma la yuqsiron,” then they do not fall short, they spare no effort, they do not tire, matching the relentless ambush that promised to come from front, back, right, left to make most ungrateful and the whispering with elegant words that inclines hearts of those who disbelieve in the Hereafter to be pleased and persistent.\n\nThe contrast is stark: the mindful are touched lightly then remember and see, the associates are extended in ghayy without curtailment, plunged deeper step by step, as earlier those who deny Our signs are gradually drawn in ways they cannot comprehend and delayed with flawless planning until the appointed term. The devils’ effort is the engine of istidraj, the gradual drawing that feels like ease while being descent, like the Sabbath fish appearing abundantly on the forbidden day to test rebelliousness, like the lowly gain that if it came again they would seize.\n\nA common misunderstanding treats association with devils as dramatic possession. The Quran shows it as persistent, unsparing extension into ghayy, ordinary choices: not giving just measure, lying in ambush on every road, changing “Hittah,” breaking Sabbath, seizing Scripture’s price. Each extension is a choice that invites another, until the heart that could have been turned toward signs for arrogance after seeing every sign still does not believe.\n\nThe remedy remains the previous verse’s pair: refuge with the All-Hearing, All-Knowing and the gracious, commanding, turning ethic that keeps the land from corruption after order and keeps calls humble, hopeful, and fearing. For the heir who says “We will be forgiven,” the warning is that devils do not spare effort in extending the easy excuse into deeper wickedness, until the dreadful punishment for rebelliousness and the disgrace of apes becomes the home of the rebellious shown, while the firm holders of Scripture and establishers of prayer, guided by truth and justice, remain the group that guides and is saved.",
  "203": "“If you ˹O Prophet˺ do not bring them a sign ˹which they demanded˺, they ask, ‘Why do you not make it yourself?’ Say, ‘I only follow what is revealed to me from my Lord. This ˹Quran˺ is an insight from your Lord—a guide and a mercy for those who believe’” addresses the demand for signs that has run from Pharaoh’s “Bring it if you are truthful” through the Sabbath tribe’s testing and the gradually drawn deniers. The condition, “If you do not bring them a sign which they demanded,” or “wa itha lam ta’tihim bi-ayatin,” imagines the prophet pausing between signs, as Moses waited with thirty plus ten nights and as winds pause before heavy clouds. The deniers’ retort, “Why do you not make it yourself?” or “law la ijtabaitaha,” why did you not invent it, select it, bring it on your own, assumes revelation is self-authored, like the golden calf’s lowing fashioned from jewellery or the shares divided for associates by claim. It mirrors the chiefs calling Moses a skilled magician who could invent, and the mockers saying “You have studied previous scriptures.”\n\nThe reply defines the prophet’s office: “I only follow what is revealed to me from my Lord,” or “qul innama attabi‘u ma yooha ilayya min Rabbi.” This repeats “I only follow what is revealed to me” from earlier, “we only follow what is revealed,” the same following commanded to the community “Follow what was sent down to you from your Lord and do not take other guardians,” the same holding firmly to the Tablets with strength, and the same abiding firmly by Scripture. Following, or “ittiba‘,” replaces invention, or “ijtabaa,” the root of their question, showing the prophet is not an inventor of signs but a follower, the humble secret caller whose staff and hand were given, not fabricated.\n\nThe description of the Quran that follows elevates it above any demanded spectacle: “This Quran is an insight from your Lord—a guide and a mercy for those who believe,” or “hatha basa’iru min Rabbikum wa hudan wa rahmah li-qawmin yu’minoonoon.” “Basa’ir,” insights, is plural of inner sight, the same insight that came as Book explained with knowledge. Guide and mercy are the pair that makes the Book blessed, that the Tablets contained for those who stand in awe, that defines the group that guides with truth, and that makes the fertile land produce abundantly.\n\nA common error deems the Quran insufficient without an extra demanded sign. The verse says the Quran itself is the sign, the insight, sufficient for eyes that refused even though they saw every sign but were turned away for arrogance. The next verses will command listening to it attentively and silently to be shown mercy, remembering inwardly morning and evening with humility not heedlessness, and joining angels who are not too proud to worship and prostrate, rather than persisting in asking why a sign is not self-made.",
  "204": "“When the Quran is recited, listen to it attentively and be silent, so you may be shown mercy” gives the communal ethic for the insight just described as guide and mercy. The previous verse said this Quran is insight from your Lord, a guide and mercy for those who believe, and that the prophet only follows what is revealed, not inventing signs on demand. This verse tells the audience how to receive that insight. The command, “When the Quran is recited, listen to it attentively and be silent,” or “wa itha quri’a al-Qur’anu fa-istami‘oo lahu wa ansitoo,” uses two verbs: “istama‘a” to listen with intent, giving ear, and “ansata” to be silent, to hush, to still. Together they mean not merely hearing sound while devils plunge associates deeper, nor hearing like cattle with ears they do not hear with, but active, quiet, focused listening, as earlier those who intend to understand, see, or hear use hearts, eyes, ears, and as the mindful remember and then see clearly.\n\nThe purpose, “so you may be shown mercy,” or “la‘allakum turhamoon,” is the same “perhaps you will be shown mercy” promised for following the blessed Book and being mindful that winds produce every fruit similarly to raising the dead. Mercy close to good-doers was promised to those who call humbly with hope and fear and do not spread corruption, and now it is promised to those who listen and be silent before the recitation that is insight, guide, and mercy. This ties to the later “Remember your Lord inwardly with humility morning and evening and do not be among the heedless,” showing outward silence and inward remembrance are paired.\n\nThe verse also corrects the demand “Why do you not make a sign yourself?” By listening attentively, the demanded sign is already present: the Quran’s recitation itself is the sign whose wonders of heavens and earth were earlier said to be heedlessly ignored. The silent, attentive listening defeats the ambush from four sides that aims to make most ungrateful, because gratitude begins with hearing.\n\nA common misunderstanding reserves listening for a mosque. The Quran’s recitation occurs in many places; wherever its insight is recited, the same ethic applies: give it ear, make silence, so mercy may descend, as earlier “We have brought them a Book explained with knowledge, guide and mercy for those who believe” requires reception. For the righteous community that guides with truth and establishes justice and for those who firmly abide by Scripture and establish prayer where reward is never discounted, this listening is the daily fertile reception that makes the land produce abundantly, before the heedless who do not hear are left wandering blindly in defiance.",
  "205": "“Remember your Lord inwardly with humility and reverence and in a moderate tone of voice, both morning and evening. And do not be one of the heedless” details the personal counterpart to communal listening. The previous verse commanded when the Quran is recited listen attentively and be silent so you may be shown mercy, an outward, collective discipline. This verse gives the inward, constant discipline: “Remember your Lord inwardly with humility and reverence and in a moderate tone of voice, both morning and evening,” or “wa uthkur Rabbaka fi nafsika tadarru‘an wa kheefatan wa doona al-jahri mina al-qawli bil-ghuduwwi wal-asaal.” “Fi nafsika,” within yourself, inwardly, is the same inwardness praised as best clothing of righteousness and as secret calling that keeps mercy close. “Tadarru‘an,” humbly, lowly, and “kheefatan,” fearfully, reverently, repeat the humble secret calling with hope and fear, opposite to the arrogance for which gates do not open. “Doona al-jahri,” without loudness, moderate, balanced, like the winds not always as flood, suggests voice between silent heart and shouting show, as earlier not transgressing when calling, and as earlier not making faith as play.\n\nThe time, “both morning and evening,” or “bil-ghuduwwi wal-asaal,” is the same morning and evening of those who invoke their Lord seeking His Face, whose scales become heavy, and of the heights’ prayer. The two ends of the day bookend remembrance, as day and night overlap swiftly by His command and He belongs to whatever is in day and night and is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\n\nThe warning, “And do not be one of the heedless,” or “wa la takun min al-ghafileen,” names the heedless, or “ghafileen,” the cattle-like destined for Hell, hearts not understanding, eyes not seeing, ears not hearing, who are turned away from signs for arrogance and who even if they see every sign will not believe, whose gathering is like the Sabbath town that forgot what they were reminded. Heedlessness is the soil that hardly produces despite winds and Springs.\n\nA common error imagines remembrance only in loud public display. The Quran commends inward, humble, moderate remembrance as the fertile practice that remembers the primordial “Yes, we testify” and the mountain raised like a cloud, keeping the covenant not to say about Allah except truth. Another error defers remembrance to later, but morning and evening mark now, before the near term that perhaps is near and before the Hour that takes by surprise, so perhaps they will return and so stories are narrated to reflect. The next verse shows the model for such remembrance: angels nearest to your Lord not too proud to worship, glorifying and prostrating, whom the mindful join with attentive silence and inward humility.",
  "206": "“Surely those ˹angels˺ nearest to your Lord are not too proud to worship Him. They glorify Him. And to Him they prostrate” closes the chapter with the highest example of the remembrance just commanded. The previous verse taught to remember your Lord inwardly with humility and reverence in a moderate tone morning and evening and not be among the heedless who are like cattle. This verse shows who does remember without heedlessness: “Surely those angels nearest to your Lord are not too proud to worship Him,” or “inna allatheena inda Rabbika la yastakbiroona an ibadatihi.” The phrase “inda Rabbika,” near your Lord, denotes nearness, the Heights’ nearness hoped for, the Home of Peace with the Guardian because of what they used to do, while “la yastakbiroona,” not too proud, directly answers the arrogance for which gates of heaven are not opened, for which the wicked are turned away from signs even if they see every sign, and for which Iblis refused to prostrate for Adam saying “I am better.” The angels, though near, remain humble, opposite to Pharaoh’s “We will completely dominate” and the chiefs’ swearing humble believers would never receive mercy.\n\nTheir activity is then described: “They glorify Him. And to Him they prostrate,” or “wa yusabbihoonahu wa lahu yasjudoon.” To glorify, or “tasbeeh,” is to declare flawless, exalted above what they associate, as after good offspring were associated and after jinn were associated though He created them, and as Moses cried “Glory be to You” after the mountain crumbled. Prostration, or “sujood,” is the same bowing the magicians fell into, the same sijdah commanded at the city gate with “Hittah,” the same humility those who firmly abide manifest. The angels’ prostration mirrors the believer’s listening and inward remembrance, showing worship is continuous between heavens and earth, day and night, as creation belongs to Him.\n\nThis final verse connects to the opening of the surah where the Book was sent down so you may warn and remind and not let anxiety tighten the chest, and to the principle that every community has a term and the weighing will be just: the angels’ constant glorification prefigures the rivers flowing and bitterness removed for the righteous.\n\nA common misunderstanding imagines nearness to Allah breeds complacency. The angels show nearness increases worship and glorification, not decreases. For the successor who inherited the blessed eastern and western lands, who was given springs, shade, manna, and Tablets to hold firmly, the mountain’s shadow still calls, the primordial “Yes” still testifies, and the Quran’s insight still recites to be heard in silence. The surah that began with Alif-Lam-Mim-Sad ends with prostration, inviting the attentive, grateful, mindful hearer to join the nearest to the Lord, not among those who claim “We will be forgiven” while persisting, but among those who guide with truth and establish justice, whose reward the righteous never lose."
};
